N2  - Early-onset neonatal bacterial infections occur in the first 7 days of life. Their incidence has decreased over time, although it remains higher than at any other pediatric age, particularly in preterm neonates. Clinical manifestations are often nonspecific and may be subtle during the first hours of illness. Late-onset neonatal bacterial infections occur between days 7 and 90 of life. For decades, this low clinical specificity led to the treatment of too many neonates with broad-spectrum antibiotics, often for prolonged durations. The current challenge is to improve risk stratification in order to restrict antibiotic therapy to neonates who are truly at high risk of infection. This shift is supported both by changes in the epidemiology of early-onset neonatal infections and by growing awareness of the harmful effects of early antibiotic exposure, including the selection of resistant bacteria, disruption of microbiota establishment, and potential long-term consequences. It has therefore become essential to limit indications for antimicrobial therapy, to use the narrowest-spectrum antibiotic possible, and to keep treatment duration as short as feasible. Blood cultures remain the reference laboratory test for confirming the diagnosis. At least one blood culture should be obtained before starting antibiotic therapy, under strict aseptic conditions, with an adequate blood volume (1 to 2 mL) and using pediatric blood culture bottles. The bacterial species responsible for early-onset neonatal infections have not changed substantially in recent years. Group B streptococci and Escherichia coli remain the leading pathogens and the main targets of empirical therapy. Group B streptococcal infections predominate in term neonates, whereas E. coli is more frequently involved in preterm infants. In healthcare-associated neonatal bacterial infections, coagulase-negative staphylococci are the most common pathogens, followed by Enterobacterales. Empirical antibiotic therapy in these situations should take into account the suspected source of infection, particularly central venous catheter, gastrointestinal, or pulmonary origin, as well as clinical severity and microbiological findings.
