N2  - Introduction: Neonatal sepsis is a major cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, particularly in developing countries. Early diagnosis remains challenging because of its nonspecific clinical presentation and changing bacteriological profile. This study aimed to evaluate the clinical characteristics, risk factors, microbiological profile, and outcomes of neonatal sepsis in a tertiary care hospital. Materials and Methods: A prospective observational study was conducted among 100 neonates (0–28 days) admitted to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit with clinically suspected neonatal sepsis. Demographic details, maternal and neonatal risk factors, clinical presentation, laboratory investigations, blood culture findings, treatment, and outcomes were recorded using a structured proforma. Blood cultures were processed using standard microbiological methods, and data were analyzed using descriptive and appropriate inferential statistics. Results: Among the 100 neonates, 58.0% were males, 62.0% had early-onset sepsis, 44.0% were preterm, and 48.0% had low birth weight. The most common risk factors were low birth weight (48.0%), prematurity (44.0%), mechanical ventilation (29.0%), resuscitation at birth (26.0%), and prolonged rupture of membranes (21.0%). Poor feeding (78.0%), lethargy (65.0%), and respiratory distress (59.0%) were the predominant clinical manifestations. Blood culture was positive in 37.0% of cases, with Klebsiella pneumoniae (32.4%) being the most common isolate, followed by Escherichia coli (21.6%) and Staphylococcus aureus (18.9%). Overall, 84.0% of neonates were discharged successfully, while the mortality rate was 9.0%. Conclusion: Early-onset neonatal sepsis was the predominant presentation, with prematurity and low birth weight being the major risk factors. Klebsiella pneumoniae was the leading pathogen among culture-positive cases. Early recognition, prompt empirical antibiotic therapy guided by local bacteriological trends, and strict infection control measures are essential for improving neonatal outcomes.
