N2  - Background: Early-onset neonatal sepsis (EOS) is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in 8f microbiological concordance between maternal culture isolates and neonatal blood culture pathogens remains poorly characterised in South Asian NICU settings. Objectives: To describe microbiological concordance between maternal vaginal swab (VS) and urine culture (UC) isolates and neonatal blood culture (BC) isolates in early-onset sepsis, and to evaluate standard clinical variables as predictors of neonatal bacteraemia. Methods: A retrospective observational study of 56 neonates admitted to the NICU over a 6-month period whose mothers had at least one positive UC or VS. Organism concordance, clinical risk factors, and outcomes were analysed using Fisher's exact test and Mann-Whitney U test. Results: Blood culture was positive in 25 of 56 neonates (44.6%). When the maternal VS was positive, VS-BC concordance was 63.2% substantially higher than UC-BC concordance (16.0%). Acinetobacter species was the dominant pathogen (52.0% of BC-positive cases) with a case fatality rate of 23.1%. A clinically important trend toward higher mortality was observed in BC-positive neonates (16.0% vs 3.2%; OR 5.71; p = 0.161). No clinical variable significantly predicted BC positivity (all p > 0.05). Conclusions: Maternal vaginal swab demonstrates clinically meaningful concordance with the neonatal blood culture isolate in the majority of BC-positive cases where VS is positive, supporting its potential to guide empiric antibiotic therapy. Acinetobacter-predominant bacteraemia carries high mortality. The failure of clinical variables to predict bacteraemia reinforces the necessity of blood culture-guided management in all at-risk preterm neonates.
