N2  - Context:Sepsis is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in neonates and young children. The management of a child with BSI includes antibiotic therapy and subsequent elimination of the pathogen guided by the identification of the type of microorganism and antibiotic susceptibility. Direct susceptibility testing by using the positive blood culture broth can give susceptibility report within 24 hours and will help in direct patient benefit in terms of administration of narrow and specific antimicrobial therapy, helps in escalation or de-escalation of antimicrobials also will act as antibiotic stewardship. Aims: To evaluate the role of Rapid Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing (RAST) in comparison with the conventional method of positive blood culture for early diagnosis. Settings and Design: comparative study conducted at Department of Microbiology, Indira Gandhi Institute of Child Health, Bengaluru. Methods and Material:Aerobic BacT/ALERT positive blood culture samples of children admitted to Indira Gandhi Institute of Child Health, Bengaluru were included. Samples were tested for RAST and conventional method simultaneously for gram negative bacteria. Discrepancies between RAST and conventional method were analysed using FDA criteria. Results: Among gram negative Enterobacteriae, klebsiella was the most common, RAST and VITEK showed concordance with respect to enterobacteriale and is reliable. For Non Enterobacteriae, RAST is less reliable and mandates confirmation by standard methods especially for Acinetobacter. Conclusions: RAST is of clinical utility in Enterobacteriae and will help in reducing drug resistance burden by early susceptibility reports.
