N2  - Background: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) remains a major gastrointestinal emergency among extremely preterm neonates, and emerging evidence suggests that early gut dysbiosis may precede clinical disease. Objective: To evaluate longitudinal gut microbiome alterations and their association with NEC in extremely preterm neonates. Methods: This prospective observational study included 112 neonates born at <28 weeks of gestation and admitted to a tertiary neonatal intensive care unit. Serial stool samples were collected on days 3, 7, 14, 21 and 28 of life or before NEC onset. Bacterial composition was analyzed using 16S rRNA gene sequencing. NEC was diagnosed according to modified Bell stage II or higher. Clinical variables, feeding practices, antibiotic exposure and microbiome indices were compared between neonates who developed NEC and those who did not. Results: NEC occurred in 18 neonates (16.1%). Neonates who developed NEC had significantly lower gestational age (26.1 ± 0.9 vs 26.8 ± 0.8 weeks, p=0.002), lower birth weight (811 ± 112 vs 902 ± 138 g, p=0.009), prolonged empirical antibiotic exposure (6.7 ± 2.1 vs 4.3 ± 1.9 days, p<0.001) and delayed achievement of full enteral feeds. Microbiome analysis showed lower Shannon diversity before NEC onset (1.72 ± 0.44 vs 2.39 ± 0.51, p<0.001), higher Proteobacteria abundance (62.4 ± 18.7% vs 39.8 ± 16.2%, p<0.001), and reduced Bifidobacterium abundance (3.8 ± 4.6% vs 12.7 ± 8.5%, p<0.001). On multivariable analysis, high Proteobacteria dominance (>60%) independently predicted NEC (adjusted odds ratio 4.82; 95% CI 1.62-14.33; p=0.005). Conclusion: Early dysbiosis characterized by reduced microbial diversity, Proteobacteria expansion and depletion of beneficial anaerobes was significantly associated with subsequent NEC in extremely preterm neonates.
