N2  - Background: Neonatal sepsis remains one of the leading causes of neonatal morbidity and mortality worldwide, particularly in developing countries. The emergence of multidrug-resistant organisms has complicated the management of neonatal infections and necessitates continuous surveillance of microbial profiles and antimicrobial susceptibility patterns. Aim: To determine the microbiological profile of neonatal sepsis and evaluate the antimicrobial resistance patterns of bacterial isolates in a tertiary care hospital. Materials and Methods: A prospective observational study was conducted in the SNCU and neonatal ward of Deben Mahata Government Medical College and Hospital Purulia, West Bengal, over 12 months. A total of 200 neonates with clinical suspicion of sepsis were enrolled. Blood cultures were processed using standard microbiological techniques. Isolates were identified by conventional biochemical methods, and antimicrobial susceptibility testing was performed according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guidelines. Data were analyzed using SPSS version 26.0. Results: Among 200 suspected cases, 82 (41.0%) were culture positive. Gram-negative organisms accounted for 58.5% of isolates, while Gram-positive organisms constituted 41.5%. The most common pathogen was Klebsiella pneumoniae (29.3%), followed by Staphylococcus aureus (20.7%) and Escherichia coli (17.1%). High resistance was observed to ampicillin (78.0%), cefotaxime (65.9%), and gentamicin (53.7%). Meropenem and linezolid demonstrated the highest susceptibility rates among Gram-negative and Gram-positive organisms, respectively. Conclusion: Klebsiella pneumoniae was the predominant pathogen causing neonatal sepsis. High antimicrobial resistance to commonly used antibiotics was observed, emphasizing the need for antibiotic stewardship programs and periodic surveillance to optimize empirical therapy.
