N2  - Purpose: Neonatal bacterial meningitis is a rare yet serious infection, and current practices default to the use of lumbar puncture and broad-spectrum antibiotics. The purpose of this study is to evaluate whether serum procalcitonin (PCT) and Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) in conjunction can safely exclude bacterial meningitis, by reducing the need for lumbar puncture (LP) and use of empiric antibiotics in well-appearing neonates. Secondary objectives will estimate the proportion of potentially avoidable LPs, antibiotic days, as well as the impact on length of stay and costs. Methods: A retrospective cohort (2011–2025) of Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) encounters will be collected upon International Review Board approval. All data will be without direct patient identifiers. Eligibility: well-appearing neonates 8–28 days for possible meningitis infection with an LP and/or serum PCT collected. Exclusions: hemodynamic instability, major congenital anomalies, neurosurgical shunts/hardware, or missing key labs. Data to be collected will include: serum PCT if available, ANC, LP, urinalysis, blood cultures, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) culture/Gram results when obtained, C-reactive protein (CRP), white blood cell count (WBC), antibiotic days, and temperature at time of LP. Each infant will be labeled screen-negative or screen-positive by prespecified strategies. Data collected with negative predictive value for meningitis among screen-negative infants will be reviewed, and diagnostic performance and clinical impact (potentially avoidable LPs, antibiotic exposure, length of stay, revisits, NICU transfer, mortality, and costs) will be assessed to determine whether serum PCT level is a reliable negative predictive biomarker for neonatal meningitis.
