N2  - Purpose: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a gastrointestinal disease characterized by inflammation and necrosis of the intestinal mucosa and is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in preterm infants, with mortality rates ranging from 20-30% and neurodevelopmental disability rates up to 61% in survivors. Several pharmacotherapies have been shown to increase the risk of NEC, including histamine-2 receptor antagonists, proton pump inhibitors, systemic steroids, and prolonged antibiotic therapy. This study aims to identify specific drug classes or treatment patterns that may increase the risk of NEC. Methods: This single-center cohort study compares pharmacological risk factors associated with NEC in preterm neonates hospitalized in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) between January 2020 and July 2025 with a diagnosis of NEC to those without. This study includes patients born at ≤ 38 weeks gestation with a hospital stay of ≥ 7 days; additionally, the NEC diagnosis group consists of patients with the following corresponding ICD-10 codes: P77.1 (stage I NEC), P77.2 (stage II NEC), P77.3 (stage III NEC), and P77.9 (unspecified NEC). Patients with intestinal atresia, Hirschsprung’s disease, gastroschisis, omphalocele, malrotation, severe congenital heart disease, genetic disorders, or chromosomal abnormalities are excluded. The primary outcome is the percentage of patients receiving at least 7 days of exposure to one or more of the following: histamine-2 receptor antagonists, proton pump inhibitors, systemic steroids, or prolonged antibiotic therapy. Secondary outcomes include individual percentage of patients on histamine-2 receptor antagonists, proton pump inhibitors, systemic steroids, or antibiotic therapy; total individual duration of histamine-2 receptor antagonists, proton pump inhibitors, systemic steroids, or antibiotic therapy; hospital length of stay; and 30-day in-hospital mortality. Additional secondary outcomes for the NEC group include the percentage of patients requiring surgical intervention and the severity of NEC based on staging. This study has been submitted to the Institutional Review Board for approval.
