N2  - Purpose: Extremely low birth weight (ELBW) infants (< 1 kg) are at high risk for invasive fungal infections due to risk factors such as immature immune defenses, broad-spectrum antibiotic exposure, and central line use. Fluconazole prophylaxis is typically initiated within the first 24-72 hours of life and continued until the infant is no longer considered high-risk. In August 2023, the study institution increased fluconazole prophylactic dosing from 3 mg/kg to 6 mg/kg every 72 hours in response to observed infection rates. This evaluation aims to compare clinical efficacy and safety outcomes before and after implementation of the dosing change among ELBW neonates. Methods: This single-center, retrospective, bi-phasic chart review will include ELBW infants admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) who received at least one dose of fluconazole prophylaxis from August 2021 to July 2023 (3 mg/kg dosing) and September 2023 to August 2025 (6 mg/kg dosing). August 2023 will be excluded as a washout month. Patients who received systemic antifungal therapy prior to fluconazole prophylaxis initiation, had prophylaxis initiated at an outside hospital prior to transfer, received both dosing regimens during the same admission, or who expired within 5 days of birth will be excluded. Patients to be screened will be identified through a report generated from NeoData® and will be reviewed in reverse chronological order. The primary outcome is fungemia incidence. Secondary outcomes include fungal isolation from sterile sites, suspected fungal infection of clinical significance, hepatotoxicity (alanine aminotransferase or aspartate aminotransferase increase to above three times the upper limit of normal), hospital length of stay, and in-hospital mortality. Statistical analysis will include Chi-square or Fisher’s exact test for categorical variables and Student’s t-test or Mann-Whitney U for continuous variables. This project was deemed exempt from Institutional Review Board review.
