N2  - Purpose: Neonatal sepsis is a systemic infection in newborns, typically diagnosed by isolating pathogens from the blood. Early onset sepsis occurs within 72 hours of birth and affects approximately one neonate per 1,000 U.S. live births. Due to immature immunity and nonspecific symptoms, many asymptomatic neonates undergo sepsis evaluations based on risk factors. The duration of empiric therapy is guided by time to positivity which helps distinguish true infections from contaminants. Although 7-13% of neonates receive antibiotics, only 3-8% have culture-confirmed sepsis. This discrepancy underscores the need for more precise diagnostic strategies to reduce unnecessary antibiotic exposure in this vulnerable population. Methods: A retrospective chart review was conducted on neonates admitted to the Level III Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) at Children’s Hospital Colorado – Colorado Springs Hospital. Neonates included were those who received empiric antibiotic therapy for suspected early-onset sepsis (EOS). Neonates were excluded if they received antibiotics for other types of infections, late-onset sepsis evaluations, or if they were transferred from outside hospitals where antibiotic dosing could not be verified in the electronic medical record (EMR). Data collected included timing of EOS evaluation, initial empiric antibiotic agents and doses, and the time to resulting complete blood count (CBC), categorized as within 24, 36, or 48 hours. The objective of this study is to evaluate the incidence of antibiotic administration lasting 24 hours or longer in neonates who present with clinically appropriate complete blood count (CBC) results and negative blood cultures, in order to assess the safety and appropriateness of discontinuing empiric antibiotic therapy after 24 hours.
