N2  - Purpose: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a leading gastrointestinal cause of neonatal hospitalization and mortality. Increased incidence is seen in patients treated for infections such as meningitis and sepsis with antibiotics. Cefotaxime has been the preferred antibiotic for these indications. However, national discontinuation led to ceftazidime use as an alternative, with limited cefotaxime available only through importation. Previous studies have shown that both antibiotics are associated with NEC development. Therefore, our study will evaluate differences in NEC rates between ceftazidime and cefotaxime to assess the clinical and financial risks versus benefits of continuing to utilize imported cefotaxime within our institutional population. Methods: The primary endpoint of this study is to determine whether transitioning from ceftazidime to cefotaxime for empiric treatment of neonatal sepsis and meningitis at the Children’s Hospital of Illinois is associated with reduced rates of NEC diagnosis, severity, morbidity, and mortality. Secondary endpoints include comparisons of hospital length of stay and duration of antibiotic therapy. This retrospective cohort study will include patients 0 to 44 weeks postmenstrual age admitted to the OSF Children’s Hospital of Illinois or the OSF Saint Francis Medical Center Emergency Department. Cohort 1 will consist of neonates who received at least one dose of ceftazidime between September 1, 2023, and August 30, 2024. Cohort 2 will include neonates who received at least one dose of cefotaxime between September 1, 2024, and August 1, 2025. Inclusion criteria are patients aged 0–44 weeks postmenstrual age who received either ceftazidime or cefotaxime during admission. Exclusion criteria include receipt of both antibiotics prior to NEC diagnosis, receipt of antibiotics at an outside institution, age greater than 44 weeks postmenstrual age, transfer after NEC diagnosis elsewhere, or spontaneous intestinal perforation without antibiotic exposure Following IRB approval, data will be extracted through healthcare analytics and supplemented with manual chart review for completeness and accuracy. The final dataset will be analyzed with support from biostatistics.
