N2  - Purpose: Neonatal early-onset sepsis (EOS) is a severe, life-threatening infection, affecting 1 in 1,000 births and mortality rate up to 50%. Biomarkers for diagnosing EOS are nonspecific, often leading to prolonged antibiotic treatment for suspected EOS, leading to complications such as necrotizing enterocolitis and antibiotic resistance. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends antibiotics for suspected EOS but lacks clear guidelines on treatment duration, often leading to prolonged therapy. Traditionally, a 48-hour empiric antibiotic protocol is used, but studies suggest that a 36-hour duration may reduce antibiotic exposure without compromising outcomes. This study evaluates whether a reduced antibiotic duration impacts clinical outcomes. Methods: This is a retrospective, IRB-approved, single-center chart review comparing two cohorts of neonates treated for EOS. Cohort 1 (2020–2023) followed the 48-hour protocol, while Cohort 2 (2023–2024) received the 36-hour protocol. Primary outcomes were compliance with the 36-hour protocol and the duration of antibiotic use for culture-negative EOS. Secondary outcomes included 30-day reinfection rates, mortality, and factors associated with prolonged therapy. Statistical analyses, including Chi-square tests, t-tests, and regression analysis, were used to identify significant differences between cohorts. Results: A total of 197 infants (97 in Cohort 1 and 100 in Cohort 2) were included. Mean gestational age (33.8 ± 4.8 vs 33.9 ± 4.9 weeks, p = 0.89) and median birth weight (2060 [IQR 1350, 2950] vs 2204 [IQR 1340, 3142] grams, p = 0.89) were similar between groups. Compliance with the 36-hour antibiotic duration for culture-negative EOS was 90%. The median number of ampicillin and gentamicin doses was significantly lower in Cohort 2 (ampicillin: 5 [IQR 5, 5.2] vs 6 [IQR 6, 12], p < 0.001; gentamicin: 1 [IQR 1, 2] vs 2 [IQR 1, 3], p < 0.001). The 30-day reinfection rate was similar between cohorts (0% vs 2%, p = 0.5), with two cases of coagulase-negative staphylococcus bacteremia in Cohort 2. Mortality was similar between groups (Cohort 1: 2 deaths, Cohort 2: 4 deaths, p = 0.68). Treatment duration beyond 48 hours was significantly higher in Cohort 1 (30% vs 15%, p = 0.04). CRP levels were the only biomarkers significantly different between cohorts (median 1.6 [IQR 0.5, 3.6] vs 2.5 [IQR 0.5, 7.8], p = 0.02), with elevated CRP associated with prolonged antibiotic therapy Conclusion: Adoption of the 36-hour antibiotic protocol for culture-negative EOS significantly reduced antibiotic exposure without increasing reinfection rates. Implementing automatic stop times may improve compliance, reduce unnecessary antibiotic use, and lower healthcare costs. These findings support revising EOS treatment protocols to minimize prolonged antibiotic therapy while maintaining effective clinical outcomes.
