N2  - Purpose: Pyelonephritis is a common type of urinary tract infection and is traditionally treated with 7 to 14 days of antibiotics. Evidence suggests shorter courses may be equally effective in uncomplicated cases, with added benefits of reduced resistance, adverse effects, decreased hospital stay length, and healthcare costs. However, prolonged treatment durations remain common in clinical practice. This discrepancy between evidence and practice underscores the need for real-world data to inform prescribing behaviors and optimize treatment duration. The objective of this study compares clinical outcomes of a 7-day antibiotic regimen versus prolonged (>7 days) therapy for adult patients with pyelonephritis. Methods: By assessing treatment success, recurrence rates, and adverse events, the study determines whether shorter regimens can safely reduce antibiotic exposure without compromising patient outcomes. This is a retrospective, observational cohort study involving approximately 100 adult patients treated for pyelonephritis. Inclusion criteria involve patients of at least 18 years of age with pyelonephritis. Patients will be excluded if they are part of the Texas Department of Criminal Justice (TDCJ) or a part of the pediatric population. Patients who received less than 6 days of antimicrobial therapy were also excluded to ensure consistent comparison between complete treatment courses and to eliminate confounding from prematurely discontinued regimens that may not reflect intended clinical practice. Eligible patients were identified through electronic medical records between January 1, 2024, and May 31, 2024. Data collected included patient demographics, antibiotic regimens, and microbiologic findings. Outcomes data were collected on 30-day all-cause mortality, recurrence, readmissions, and adverse events. Patients were categorized into two groups based on antibiotic duration: 7-day versus greater than 7-day therapy. Results: The primary outcome was 30-day all-cause mortality. Secondary outcomes included infection recurrence, hospital readmissions beyond 30, 60, and 90 days, and adverse drug reactions. A two-proportion t-test was conducted to compare all-cause mortality. Of the total 101 patients included, 90 patients had no record of all cause morality. The remaining 10 patients were concluded as unknown for all-cause mortality per their electronic medical records. The analysis demonstrated that patients receiving 7-day therapy had comparable rates of mortality and recurrence to those receiving longer courses, suggesting that extended antibiotic duration did not provide additional clinical benefit. No significant differences were observed in hospital readmission rates at 30, 60, or 90 days, nor in the incidence of adverse drug reactions between the two treatment durations, further reinforcing the comparability of clinical outcomes across groups. These findings support the safety and effectiveness of shorter treatment regimens for pyelonephritis, aligning with antimicrobial stewardship goals. Shorter courses of therapy have no effect on treatment outcomes or failure when compared to a longer course. Conclusion: By examining treatment duration in this population across a single academic institution, this study bridges the gap between guideline recommendations and prescribing practices. It offers insight into uncomplicated infections with prevalent overtreatment. Findings from this study stated that treatment outcomes was not different between 7 days of therapy and greater than 7 days of therapy. This supports antimicrobial stewardship by providing real-world evidence on the safety and efficacy of shorter antibiotic courses for pyelonephritis. It also contributes to broader efforts to reduce antimicrobial resistance, inform future quality improvement initiatives, and improve patient care through more evidence-based treatment durations.
