N2  - Purpose: The 2021 American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) guidelines recommend discontinuing antibiotic use for neonatal sepsis rule-out at 24 to 36 hours when blood cultures remain negative and the infant is clinically well. The standard antibiotic regimen for neonatal sepsis rule-out consists of ampicillin and gentamicin. Neonatal blood cultures typically exhibit rapid time to positivity, with approximately 96% of bacteremia results available within 36 hours. Reducing antibiotic duration may decrease the incidence of resistant bacteria and adverse outcomes. Methods: This study at CHI St. Alexius Health will be submitted to the Institutional Review Board (IRB) for approval. Patients will be retrospectively identified from an Electronic Health Record System who were treated for neonatal sepsis rule-out between September 2024 and February 2026. Individuals admitted to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) who received 36 to 48 hours of ampicillin and gentamicin, regardless of gestational age, will be included. Patients with a continuous antibiotic treatment duration exceeding 48 hours will be excluded. Data to be collected will encompass: gestational age (GA), weight, postmenstrual age (PMA), gender, microbiology results, complete blood count (CBC) with differential, serum creatinine, antibiotic (dose, frequency, treatment duration, restart, doses received at an outside facility), length of stay, and clinical symptoms of an infection or re-infection. All data collected will be recorded without patient identifiers and stored securely to maintain confidentiality. The incidence of antibiotic restart after initial rule-out treatment completion and length of hospital stay will be the primary outcomes measured. The data will be analyzed to assess whether a 36-hour duration is non-inferior to a 48-hour duration for neonatal sepsis rule-out.
