N2  - Purpose: Empiric antibiotic therapy is essential in management of early-onset sepsis (EOS) in neonates. Prolonged exposure to antibiotics carries risks including necrotizing enterocolitis, late-onset sepsis, and chronic lung disease. Clinical markers for infections such as respiratory instability, lethargy, or temperature instability, lack specificity for EOS which can complicate decision making regarding antibiotic discontinuation. Our institution currently uses a 48-hour stop time on empiric antibiotics for EOS; however, other institutions have reported safe implementation of a 24-hour stop time. This study aims to evaluate the time- to-positivity (TTP) of blood cultures obtained during EOS evaluations in the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU). Methods: This retrospective observational study will be conducted at Norton Children’s Hospital (NCH) and Norton Women and Children’s Hospital (NWC) in the NICU. Eligible patients include neonates aged 0–7 days of life admitted between July 1, 2022, and July 31, 2025, who underwent EOS evaluation with blood culture testing and received empiric antibiotics. The primary outcome is TTP of blood cultures obtained during EOS evaluations. Secondary outcomes include duration of antibiotics for treatment of culture-positive and culture-negative sepsis and organism identified on culture. Statistical analysis will include descriptive statistics and independent t-tests.
