N2  - Bleeding related to portal hypertension is among the most serious complications of cirrhosis. It most commonly presents as esophageal or gastric variceal hemorrhage, with portal hypertensive gastropathy and ectopic varices as other causes. Despite advances in endoscopic, pharmacologic, and radiologic treatment, rebleeding after initial hemostasis remains associated with substantial morbidity, prolonged hospitalization, and high short-term mortality. Early recognition is therefore crucial, before progression to hemodynamic shock, or organ failure. In patients with cirrhosis, recurrent hemorrhage is not only a local hemostatic problem but also a marker of advanced portal hypertension and poor hepatic reserve. Rebleeding should be suspected in patients with fresh hematemesis, recurrent melena after initial stabilization, hematochezia suggesting brisk upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, or hemodynamic deterioration after apparent control of bleeding. A fall in hemoglobin level, increasing transfusion need, worsening tachycardia, hypotension, rising lactate, oliguria, altered sensorium, or new vasopressor requirement should raise suspicion. Laboratory assessment includes serial hemoglobin, platelet count, international normalized ratio, creatinine, bilirubin, and ideally dynamic evaluation of trends rather than single values. The risk of early rebleeding and death is particularly high in patients with advanced hepatic dysfunction, including those with Child–Pugh class C cirrhosis or Child–Pugh class B cirrhosis with active bleeding at endoscopy. Initial management includes airway protection, hemodynamic resuscitation, restrictive red-cell transfusion, vasoactive therapy, and antibiotic prophylaxis. Excessive volume replacement can increase portal pressure and precipitate further bleeding; a restrictive transfusion strategy targeting a hemoglobin concentration of 7 to 9 g/dL is generally recommended. Early antibiotic treatment reduces bacterial infection, rebleeding, and mortality in cirrhosis with upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Rebleeding in cirrhosis and portal hypertension can result from postbanding ulcer bleeding, premature band slippage, incomplete variceal eradication, persistent severe portal hypertension, advanced liver dysfunction, infection, or renal impairment. Management requires a structured stepwise plan. After initial stabilization, repeat endoscopy is indicated when there is clinical evidence of recurrent bleeding or persistent instability. Therapeutic options include, repeat endoscopic band ligation for esophageal varices or tissue adhesive injection for gastric varices, depending on lesion type and anatomy. Hemostatic powders or combined techniques may be used as rescue measures in selected cases. Failure of combined pharmacological and endoscopic therapy requires early multidisciplinary discussion with interventional radiology team. Preemptive transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS), ideally within 24 to 72 hours, should be considered in high-risk patients, particularly those with Child–Pugh class C cirrhosis (score < 14) or Child–Pugh class B cirrhosis with active bleeding at endoscopy. Salvage TIPS is the preferred definitive rescue treatment for uncontrolled or recurrent variceal bleeding despite standard therapy. Balloon tamponade or a self-expanding covered esophageal stent (SX-Ella Danis stent) can provide temporary hemostasis as a bridge to TIPS in selected patients. After stabilization, secondary prophylaxis with a nonselective beta-blocker plus repeated endoscopic variceal ligation is first-line treatment for prevention of recurrent esophageal variceal bleeding. Liver transplantation should be considered individually, particularly in patients with recurrent decompensation, and severe hepatic dysfunction.
