N2  - Purpose: Most current antibiotic protocols require discontinuation of antibiotic therapy in empiric neonatal sepsis therapy after at least 48h of negative blood cultures. This study would aim to determine the time to positivity of blood cultures obtained from infants with risk factors or clinical signs of sepsis so that our empiric antibiotic usage can be optimized, while also minimizing unnecessary exposure in this population. We hypothesize that with advancements that have been made in microbiologic testing, true positive cultures could be safely identified within a shorter duration and hence reduce the duration of empiric antibiotic exposure could be reduced during NICU stay. Methods: This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study that included infants admitted to the Regional One Health NICU who underwent a sepsis work-up and were determined to have positive blood cultures. We defined early-onset sepsis(EOS)(≤72 hrs of life) or late-onset sepsis (LOS) (>72 hrs of life) based on the time of their clinical presentation. For infants who received multiple cultures during an event, only the initial culture was included. After, IRB approval, we recorded the date and time of blood culture collection, the date and time that a positive result was identified, and recorded the species of bacteria identified. Variables recorded for each infant who had a positive culture included gestational age, age at onset of sepsis, birth weight, maternal GBS status, and chorioamnionitis status. We also reviewed each patient's chart to identify the type and duration of antibiotic therapy. Results: Between January 2023- March 2025, 1509 blood cultures were obtained in our NICU. We identified 86 events (5.6%)with positive blood cultures. Median GA of this cohort was 27.2 wk, with birthweight of 820gm, 59% were males. EOS was identified in 20/86 events. The overall median time between collection of blood culture and preliminary positive culture result was 16.8 h (IQR 13, 27). When potential contaminants were excluded the median time for first positive report was at 16h (IQR 13, 24.5). For EOS cases the median time to initial preliminary positive culture was 14.6h (IQR 13.1, 20.6), while for LOS cases the median time was 18.6h (IQR 13.8, 27.45). Conclusions: Our data indicate that majority of blood cultures that result positive are noted within 24h of collection, for both, EOS and LOS. In asymptomatic babies it may be safe to discontinue empiric therapy if cultures remain negative for 24-36h. NICUs should develop guidelines assessing the comfort level of clinical teams and reviewing their unit specific data to promote antibiotic stewardship.
