N2  - Purpose: Group B Streptococcus (GBS) is the leading cause of neonatal meningitis worldwide with a high rate of morbidity and mortality in infants less than three months of age.1 Complicated cases appear to be increasing, leading to prolonged hospital admissions and extended antibiotic courses. This study aimed to characterize GBS-associated central nervous system infections and evaluate whether the use of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) influenced the duration of antibiotic treatment. Methods: We conducted a retrospective descriptive chart review of pediatric patients less than three months of age admitted to Oklahoma Children's Hospital in the previous five years with culture-confirmed GBS meningitis. The data collected included age at admission, gestational age, gender, symptoms present at admission, clinical course and complications, laboratory results, MRI findings, and duration of antibiotic therapy. These findings were then compared and analyzed for commonalities. Results: Seven pediatric patients met inclusion criteria. The average age at admission was 7.76 weeks (2 to 15 weeks). The mean length of hospitalization was 5.35 weeks (2 to 11 weeks). The average duration of treatment with antibiotics was 4.57 weeks (2 to 8 weeks). Of the cases reviewed, 86% had abnormal MRIs and 57% patients received greater than 4 weeks of antibiotics. Initial MRI imaging revealed abnormalities of subdural empyema (57%), hydrocephalus (29%), cerebritis (29%), and ventriculitis (14%). Fives patients had repeat MRIs 4-8 weeks after diagnosis, all had resolution of the subdural collections, but findings of hydrocephalus (29%), encephalomalacia (14%), and dural enhancement (14%) persisted on subsequent imaging despite appropriate antibiotic treatment. Cases with notable complications included additional MRI findings of thrombi of the venous sinuses (29%), and cerebral infarcts (14%). Conclusions: Per the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) Red Book, the standard duration of treatment for uncomplicated GBS meningitis is just 14 days, while complicated cases that include common findings on MRI (ventriculitis, abscess) are recommended to be treated for a minimum of four weeks.2,3 In this review, the average duration of treatment exceeded those guidelines in 57% cases. Additionally, a similar case review found that 81% of infants with bacterial meningitis had abnormal MRI findings.4 MRI remains an important tool for evaluation of a complicated central nervous system infection, but its role in guiding therapy duration is unclear and should be carefully considered. Further prospective studies are warranted to establish evidence-based recommendations that include MRI to balance adequate infection control without promoting overtreatment and extending hospitalization.
