N2  - Background: Antibiotic resistance has become an emerging global menace, posing challenges for doctors in treating infections and increasing drug costs. This problem could be particularly severe in intensive care units, where the prognosis could be severely affected. Cautious and rational use of antibiotics, along with de-escalation whenever necessary, can better prevent the development of superbugs and pan-resistant bacteria.Objectives:To evaluate the most common microorganisms causing infection among various different intensive care units. To assess the sensitivity and resistance pattern towards various antibiotics in the study setting. Methods:An observational study was conducted over a period of 9 months in a tertiary care hospital, and 200 different sample specimens sent to the Department of Microbiology were assessed to identify the microorganisms causing infection among patients admitted to different intensive care units and their sensitivity and resistance patterns to various antibiotics. Results:After thoroughly evaluating the collected data, it was found that most of the samples were sent from the neonatal ICU, followed by the surgical and other ICUs. The most common pathogen causing infections was Klebsiella, followed by Staphylococcus, E. Coli, etc. Pathogens were more sensitive to newer antibiotics, such as meropenem and imipenem, as well as to other antibiotics, such as tetracycline and cotrimoxazole. In contrast, resistance to ciprofloxacin, ampicillin, and ceftriaxone was more common in the study setting. Conclusion: Periodic antibiotic resistance monitoring and antibiotic stewardship programs are needed to optimize the use of antibiotics and prevent overuse and misuse of available antibiotics.
