N2  - Bifidobacterium infantis (B. infantis), currently classified as Bifidobacterium longum subsp. infantis, is a key intestinal symbiont in infancy, specializing in the metabolism of human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs). This probiotic bacterium plays a crucial role in both immunological and metabolic maturation; however, its prevalence has markedly declined in industrialized populations due to practices such as cesarean delivery, antibiotic exposure, and non-exclusive breastfeeding. Objective: The aim of this study was to evaluate the efficacy and clinical impact of B. infantis supplementation on intestinal microbiome maturation, immune modulation, and disease prevention during early childhood. Methodology: A systematic review was conducted in accordance with PRISMA guidelines. Scopus, PubMed, Google Scholar, and ScienceDirect were searched, resulting in the inclusion of 22 human studies after a screening process involving title, abstract, and full-text reviews. Results: The most relevant findings indicate that B. infantis colonization is strongly influenced by perinatal practices; its mechanisms of action include HMO metabolism and immune modulation; and probiotic supplementation is associated with a reduction in necrotizing enterocolitis in preterm infants, as well as improvements in gastrointestinal and respiratory symptoms, although efficacy varies depending on the strain used and the clinical context. Conclusions: B. infantis is an essential microbial modulator, whose restoration through probiotics represents a safe and promising strategy, especially for neonatal health, although its success depends on factors such as strain, dosage, and appropriate clinical practices.
