N2  - Background: This study investigated the clinical and genomic characteristics of Entervbacter cloacae complex (ECC) and Klebsiella aerogenes bloodstream infections (BSIs) in Korean children. Methods: A total of 115 BSI episodes (ECC: 86, K. aerogenes: 29) from 110 children hospitalized between 2011 and 2024 were retrospectively analyzed. Whole-genome sequencing was performed on available isolates to determine species, sequence types, and antimicrobial resistance (AMR) genes. Clinical characteristics, antibiotic usage, and outcomes were compared between groups. Results: Patients with K aerogenes BSI were younger and more likely to be preterm or diagnosed with urosepsis, while ECC infections were more frequently associated with hematologic malignancies. Multivariable analysis revealed that K. aerogenes infection (odds ratio [OR]=6.3)and gentamicin resistance (OR=10.1) were independent risk factors for 30-day mortality. Enferobacrerrtonnaecfte/wasthe most common ECC species (68.4%) and exhibited the highest prevalence of AMR genes, particularly those conferring resistance to aminoglycosides, B-lactams, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. In contrast, K. aerogenes harbored few resistance genes. Multi-locus sequence typing analysis revealed high genetic diversity in both ECC and K. aerogenes, without evidence of dominant clonal expansion. Conclusions: In conclusion, despite similarities in clinical presentation, ECC and K aerogenes exhibit distinct age distributions, resistance profiles. and genetic diversity in pediatric BSIs, These findings underscore the importance of species-level identification and continued genomic surveillance to inform empirical antibiotic strategies and prevent the spread of resistant strains.
