N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters (AS) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2025 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Chromosome 8 deletions are rare, and their clinical effects remain poorly understood. Limited studies have identified key regions, such as 8p23.1, associated with congenital heart disease (CHD), congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH), and neurodevelopmental disorders (1). Shi et al. suggest haploinsufficiency of genes like GATA4, NEIL2, and SOX7 may contribute to CHD and CDH (1). The National Organization for Rare Disorders (NORD) supports GATA4's role in cardiac development (4). Patients with chromosome 8 deletions are at increased risk of coagulopathies and clotting abnormalities, including atrial fibrillation-related hemostatic disruptions (3,6,7). Spontaneous hematomas can arise due to bleeding disorders, prolonged ICU stays, anticoagulation therapy, or seizures (4,5,8,9,10). Here, we report a young female with a chromosome 8 deletion, seizure disorder, and intellectual disability who experienced cardiac arrhythmias and developed a spontaneous hematoma infected with a rare carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old female with cerebral palsy, paraplegia, seizure disorder, and scoliosis presented with fever (103°F), hypotension, and thrombocytopenia. She was nonverbal, bedbound, and had flexion contractures. Initial workup indicated a urinary tract infection (UTI), bacteremia, and atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response (RVR), requiring pressor therapy and antiarrhythmic management. Despite antibiotics and intensive care, she experienced persistent tachyarrhythmias, hypotension, and recurrent fevers. A CT on day 19 revealed a right hamstring hematoma, later found to be infected with multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas. Despite targeted therapy, she remained critically ill, requiring ongoing pressor support. Her condition worsened with pulmonary congestion and septic shock. On day 45, she developed respiratory distress, leading to a goals-of-care discussion. The patient was transitioned to comfort measures and passed away the same day. DISCUSSION: The exact cause of this patient's hematoma remains unclear but is likely multifactorial. Contributing factors include seizure activity, possible coagulopathy due to Chromosome 8 deletion, cardiac arrhythmias, and unknown genetic factors. Her inability to verbalize pain complicated assessment. Pediatric TTE findings suggest genetics, arrhythmias, or abnormal anatomy could have played a role. Regarding her infectious hematoma, the source of the hematoma is uncertain. The patient had MRSA bacteremia, persistent fevers, and prolonged antibiotic use, increasing the risk for resistance. While carbapenem-resistant infections are linked to long hospital stays, such infections in hematomas are rare, with no clear evidence found here. (15) Current guidelines recommend ceftazidime-avibactam, which was used in this case. (13,14) Limitations include the lack of confirmed genetic testing and difficulties identifying infection sources due to numerous complications. Early detection and imaging are vital for patients with intermittent fevers, prolonged ICU stays, or cognitive impairments, with advanced diagnostics like metagenomic next-generation sequencing (mNGS) recommended for more targeted treatment. (12) CONCLUSIONS: Attributing this patient's carbapenem resistance solely to prolonged use would be shortsighted, as she had multiple risk factors, including arrhythmias, seizures, and genetic predispositions. Her complex medical history and prolonged hospitalization increased morbidity and mortality risks. Recognizing superinfections and spontaneous hematomas in similar cases is crucial for clinical practice. REFERENCE #1: 1.) Logan LK, Weinstein RA. The Epidemiology of Carbapenem-Resistant Enterobacteriaceae: The Impact and Evolution of a Global Menace. J Infect Dis. 2017;215(suppl_1):S28-S36. doi:10.1093/infdis/jiw282. 2.) Chromosome 8, Monosomy 8p. National Organization for Rare Disorders. Accessed February 23, 2025. https://rarediseases.org/rare-diseases/chromosome-8-monosomy-8p/ 3.) Zipes DP, Camm AJ, Borggrefe M, et al. ACC/AHA/ESC 2006 Guidelines for Management of Patients With Ventricular Arrhythmias and the Prevention of Sudden Cardiac Death. Circulation. 2006;114(10):e385-e484. doi:10.1161/01.cir.0000436140.77832.7a. 4.) Zhang Y, Xiao M, Gawande P, et al. Metagenomic Next-Generation Sequencing for Pathogen Detection and Antimicrobial Resistance Prediction in Clinical Samples. J Clin Microbiol. 2019;57(9):e00491-19. doi:10.1128/JCM.00491-19. 5.) Togel F, Hu Z, Weiss K, et al. Kidney Regeneration and Endothelial Progenitor Cell Therapy in Acute Renal Failure. Nephrology (Carlton). 2004;9(6):277-285. doi:10.1111/j.1440-1797.2004.00269.x. REFERENCE #2: 6.) Yamashita M, Oka T, Kagiyama N, et al. Impact of arrhythmic burden on left ventricular function in patients with premature ventricular contractions. Heart. 2020;106(19):1463-1469. doi:10.1136/heartjnl-2020-316628. 7.) Moussouttas M. Secondary stroke prevention: challenges and solutions. Lancet. 2009;373(9667):1512-1524. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(09)60040-4. 8.) Powers WJ, Rabinstein AA, Ackerson T, et al. Guidelines for the Early Management of Patients With Acute Ischemic Stroke: 2019 Update. Stroke. 2019;50(12):e344-e418. doi:10.1161/STR.0000000000000407. 9.) Amarenco P, Bogousslavsky J, Caplan LR, Donnan GA, Hennerici MG. Classification of stroke subtypes. J Stroke Cerebrovasc Dis. 2019;28(12):104335. doi:10.1016/j.jstrokecerebrovasdis.2018.10.003. 10.) Spetzler RF, McDougall CG, Zabramski JM, et al. The Barrow Ruptured Aneurysm Trial: 6-year results. J Neurosurg. 1997;86(3):433-439. doi:10.3171/jns.1997.86.3.0433. REFERENCE #3: 12.) Xu Y, Jia B, Huo Z, et al. Metagenomic next-generation sequencing technology for bacterial DNA detection in infectious endocarditis. Front Cell Infect Microbiol. 2021;11:745156. doi:10.3389/fcimb.2021.745156. 13.) Patel TS, Kaakeh R, Nagel JL, Newton DW, Stevenson JG. Cost analysis of rapid diagnostic testing for bloodstream infections: Evaluating assay with antimicrobial stewardship intervention. Open Forum Infect Dis. 2021;8(8):ofab385. doi:10.1093/ofid/ofab385. 14.) Buehrle DJ, Shields RK, Clarke LG, Potoski BA, Clancy CJ, Nguyen MH. Carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia: Risk factors for mortality and microbiologic treatment failure. Antimicrob Agents Chemother. 2017;61(9):e01243-17. doi:10.1128/AAC.01243-17. 15.) Morrill HJ, Pogue JM, Kaye KS, LaPlante KL. Treatment options for carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae infections. Open Forum Infect Dis. 2015;2(2):ofv050. doi:10.1093/ofid/ofv050. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Natalia Pogosian No relevant relationships by David Smith No relevant relationships by Anton Uhlen
