N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters (R) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2025 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Chryseobacterium Indologenes (C. Indologenes), is an emerging bacterium found mainly insoil and water. It is a non-enteric gram-negative aerobic rod, of which there are very limitedcases of severe disease due to its low virulence factor. It frequently manifests itself inimmunocompromised patients, such as neonates, those on immunosuppressants, and patientswith cancer, HIV, or chronic hepatitis infections. This species is often multidrug resistant and has a mortality rate of 36-50%. The majority of published case reports present as ventilator associated pneumonia. We present a case of C. Indologenes tracheobronchitis in an immunocompetent patient following a motor vehicle accident. CASE PRESENTATION: Our patient is a 70 y/o female with a history of COPD Gold B on supplemental oxygen and HTN who presented as a trauma alert after a motor vehicle accident. She was found to have profound dyspnea, hypotension, leukocytosis, and large skin avulsion on the center of the chest. The patient's imaging was negative for any acute traumatic injury. She was subsequently intubated for acute respiratory distress. Empiric antibiotics and sputum cultures were obtained due to degree of hypoxia and concern for COPD exacerbation/Pneumonia. Sputum cultures were positive for multidrug resistant C. Indologenes. Patient was extubated to BiPAP and required reintubation a few hours later. Serial Chest x-rays were done and were negative for any acute cardiopulmonary process. Patient underwent Chest CT and the only pertinent finds were consistent with known centrilobular emphysema. Repeat sputum cultures were positive for pan-sensitive Pseudomonas. Patient was treated with IV Ciprofloxacin. Her respiratory status improved and she was extubated to BiPAP. The patient clinically improved and was discharged on home supplemental oxygen. The patient presented back to the hospital with worsening respiratory symptoms three months later and described a mMRC 4 following discharge. Due to her continued decline the patient and her family opted for home hospice. DISCUSSION: C. Indologenes is predominantly a rare nosocomial pathogen associated with invasive devices and seen in the immunocompromised, trauma, elderly, or those with frequent antibiotic use. The SENTRY program revealed an incidence of Chyrseobacterium species over a 5-year period to be 0.03% of all isolated bacteria from hospitalized patients. The species was isolated in the respiratory tract 52% of the time with the other major site on blood cultures. Our patient's risk factors included trauma and older age. CONCLUSIONS: C. Indologenes is a rare nosocomial pathogen with a high mortality due to its high frequency for multidrug resistance. Early consideration, detection and treatment is crucial in trauma patients. REFERENCE #1: Nemli, S.A., Demirdal, T., Ural, S. "A case of healthcare associated pneumonia caused by Chryseobacterium Indolenes in a Immunocompetent patient". Case reports in Infectious diseases. https://doi.org/10.1155/2015/483923. Chang J, Kim S, Kwak YG, Um TH, Cho CR, Song JE. Clinical and Microbiological Characteristics of Chryseobacterium Indolgenes Bacteremia: A 20-Year Experience in a Single University Hospital. Infect Chemottherp.Sep;55(3):322-327.https://doi.org/10.3947/ic.2022.0133 REFERENCE #2: Izaguirre-Anariba, D. E., and Sivapalan, V. Chryseobacterium indologenes, an EmergingBacteria: A Case Report and Review of Literature. Cureus, 12(1), e6720.https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.6720. Mukerji R, Kakarala R, Smith SJ, et al. Chryseobacterium indologenes: an emerginginfection in the USACase Reports 2016;2016:bcr2016214486 REFERENCE #3: Kirby J. T., Sader H. S., Walsh T. R., Jones R. N. Antimicrobial Susceptibility and Epidemiology of a Worldwide Collection of Chryseobacterium spp.: Report from the SENTRY Antimicrobial Surveillance Program (1997–2001) Journal of Clinical Microbiology. 2004;42(1):445–448. doi: 10.1128/jcm.42.1.445-448.2004. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mauricio Danckers No relevant relationships by Adam Devine No relevant relationships by Adam Devine No relevant relationships by Ashley Diaz No relevant relationships by Siddarth Kathuria No relevant relationships by Jude Tabba
