N2  - Background: Randomised studies on antibiotic duration for culture-negative early-onset infection (EOI) are scarce. In Denmark, ~0.5% of term neonates receive antibiotics for 5-7 days for culture-negative EOI. We aimed to assess whether individualised treatment duration would safely reduce the duration of antibiotic therapy compared to standard treatment. Methods: A nationwide, multicentre (17 neonatal units), open-label, randomised controlled non-inferiority trial. We included term-born neonates with probable EOI (treatment initiation ≤ 72 hours after birth) and a negative blood culture. Neonates meeting criteria to discontinue antibiotics within 48 hours were ineligible. Participants were randomised 1:1 to individualised or standard treatment 36-48 hours after antibiotic initiation. Neonates allocated to individualised duration discontinued antibiotics when symptom-free for 24 hours, and C-reactive protein (CRP) was declining and ≤ 30 mg/l. Standard duration was 5-7 days. The co-primary outcomes were readmission due to bacterial infection within 28 days or death of any cause (non-inferiority margin 4%) and total antibiotic duration within 28 days after treatment initiation (superiority assessment). Analyses were done in the intention-to-treat population. Results: Between April 22, 2021, and April 18, 2025, 493 patients were randomly assigned to individualised (n = 245) or standard (n = 248) duration. Antibiotics were initiated at a median of 23 hours after birth (IQR 15 to 28). Median maximum CRP was 73 mg/L (IQR 56 to 91). Readmission due to bacterial infection occurred in two (0.8%) of 245 patients in the individualised group and one (0.4%) of 248 in the standard group (difference 0.4%, two-sided 95% CI [-1.5 % to 2.6%], pnon-inferiority = 0.003). Median total antibiotic treatment within 28 days was 2.8 days (IQR 2.4 to 3.4) in the individualised group and 6.8 days (IQR 6.6 to 7.0) in the standard group (difference 4.0 days (95% CI [3.8;4.1], p < 0.001) (Fig.1). There was no (0%) serious adverse event in the individualised group and one (0.4%) in the standard group (difference -0.4%, 95% CI [-2.3%;1.2%]). Conclusion: In neonates with culture-negative EOI, individualised treatment duration was safe and non-inferior to standard therapy regarding readmission due to bacterial infection and significantly reduced antibiotic exposure. These findings endorse reduced antibiotic use in neonates through individualised treatment strategies.
