N2  - Background: Effectiveness of probiotics in preventing necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) in preterm infants remains widely debated. Our aim was to evaluate the effects of implementing routine multi-strain probiotics on NEC incidence in two Dutch NICUs in a real-time clinical setting. Methods: A controlled retrospective pre-post epoch study was conducted comparing NEC incidence (Bell ≥ stage 2) before and after implementing probiotics (ProPrems®; Bifidobacterium infantis Bb-02, Bifidobacterium lactis BB-12, and Streptococcus thermophilus TH-4). Probiotics were routinely administered to infants born <30 weeks' GA or <1000 g birth weight (BW) (study group). Infants born at 30-32 weeks' GA and 1000-1500 g BW, who per-protocol never received probiotics, were included to analyze underlying NEC-trends over time in the absence of probiotics (reference group). Our primary objective was NEC incidence, comparing pre-implementation (January 2018-October 2020/March 2021) and post-implementation (October 2020/March 2021-June 2024) periods. Generalized logistic regression models were applied, controlling for pre-existing trends and confounding factors (GA, BW-z-score, sex, antibiotic exposure, perinatal factors, and seasonality). Results: We assessed 2195 infants: 602 in the pre-implementation study group, 822 in the post-implementation study group, 319 in the pre-implementation reference group, and 425 in the post-implementation reference group. Baseline characteristics, including GA (median 27 + 5 weeks) and BW (median 950 g), were comparable between the two study groups. Probiotic exposure was significantly associated with reduced NEC risk, corresponding to a 52% relative risk reduction (aRR: 0.48; 95% CI: 0.32-0.69; p < 0.001). A consistent risk reduction was observed below 30 weeks GA, but not above. In the extremely preterm subgroup, a comparable reduction of 52% was observed. No significant pre-existing temporal trends in NEC-incidence or mortality were observed. Conclusion: Routine probiotic supplementation was effective and appeared safe in reducing the risk of NEC in preterm infants. Significant risk reductions were observed in infants born before 30 weeks of gestation, with comparable effects in the extremely preterm subgroup. These findings support the use of probiotics as a preventive strategy in high-risk neonatal populations.
