N2  - Background: Neonatal sepsis causes significant mortality globally. Data describing early- and late-onset neonatal sepsis guide empirical antibiotic therapy but are predominantly from high-income countries. Causative pathogens are likely to differ between settings, potentially impacting the efficacy of prescribed treatment.<br><br>Aims: To identify the epidemiology and mortality associated with neonatal sepsis, including delineation of culture-negative cases, culture–positive cases, and early-versus late-onset hospital-acquired infections.<br><br>Methods: We implemented the “A Clinically Oriented antimicrobial Resistance surveillance Network” protocol to undertake systematic infection surveillance in a 70-bed neonatal intensive care unit at the Philippine General Hospital. We enrolled all admitted neonates and young infants with clinically suspected sepsis between November 14, 2023, and Nov 13, 2024.<br><br>Results: 799 participants with 1,024 separate clinically suspected sepsis episodes were enrolled. Over two-thirds (74%; 758/1,024) were early-onset (EOS, defined as infection occurring within 48 hours of life) and 26% (266/1,024) were late-onset, hospital-acquired infections. Only 1% of EOS episodes were culture-positive (6/758), of which 5 of 6 were caused by gram-negative bacteria (GNB). 15% (41/266) of late-onset episodes were culture-positive, of which 65% (27/41) were caused by GNB. Overall, 15% of participants died (117/799), including 12% (93/754) with culture-negative sepsis and 53% (24/45) of those who experienced an episode of culture-positive sepsis.<br><br>Conclusion: There was a high burden of sepsis and significant mortality in hospitalised neonates. Culture-negative sepsis predominates, and gram-negative bacteria are causative of most culture-positive sepsis. Empirical antibiotic guidelines need to reflect geographic variability, to ensure babies receive efficacious therapy.
