N2  - This study investigates the antibiotic sensitivity patterns of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms isolated from vaginal infections in preterm labor and term labor patients. A total of 200 pregnant women were included, categorized into two groups: 100 preterm labor and 100 term labor patients. Vaginal swabs were collected and analyzed for microbial identification and antibiotic sensitivity using the Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion method. The results revealed that vaginal infections were significantly more prevalent in preterm labor patients (70.7%) compared to term labor patients (29.2%). Common pathogens included Coagulase-negative Staphylococcus (CONS), Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and E. coli, with Gram-negative organisms exhibiting higher antibiotic resistance. Sensitivity testing indicated that Amikacin, Ceftriaxone, and Ciprofloxacin were the most effective antibiotics against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. The study highlights the role of antibiotic resistance in managing vaginal infections in preterm labor patients and suggests that targeted treatment based on antibiotic sensitivity could improve patient outcomes. It also emphasizes the importance of early diagnosis, preventive measures, and antibiotic stewardship to reduce the burden of infections and preterm labor.
