N2  - Introduction: Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection. Neonatal sepsis incurs significant morbidity and mortality, with premature babies suffering the most severe sequalae. However, there is no universally accepted definition for organ dysfunction or sepsis in this vulnerable population. A lack of diagnostic criteria leads to overuse of antibiotics which adversely affects the developing microbiome and cultivates resistant microorganisms. The Phoenix sepsis criteria (2024) were derived and validated to define sepsis in children (>37 weeks gestation to 18 years) using organ dysfunction criteria associated with mortality risk. These criteria excluded babies admitted to a NICU and premature neonates. Pediatric and neonatal sepsis share similarities and differences. Thus, it is important to assess if the pediatric criteria can be applied to neonates. We aim to apply the 8-point Phoenix sepsis score to neonates who underwent a sepsis evaluation. We hypothesize that neonates with a clinical diagnosis of sepsis will have a higher Phoenix sepsis score compared to neonates who were evaluated for infection but did not have antibiotics continued beyond 48 hrs. Methods: In this retrospective cohort study, we used data from neonates admitted to any of our four system NICU's (one level 4, two level 3 and one level 2 facilities) who underwent sepsis evaluation within the first 3 months of life. A sepsis evaluation was defined as having a blood culture order (time 0) and antibiotics ordered within 6 hours of that culture. The 8-point Phoenix sepsis score was assigned using data obtained between 12 hours before and 24 hours after time 0. The Phoenix scores were compared between 2 cohorts: rule-out cohort (antibiotic duration ≤48 hours) and sepsis cohort (antibiotic duration >48 hours). One event per patient was included. Scores were compared between the two cohorts via Wilcoxon-rank sum test. Results: There were 2,694 included patients (57% male, mean gestational age 35 weeks) (rule out n = 2,106; sepsis n = 588). Mean Phoenix score was 2.1 ± 1.6 in the rule-out cohort and 2.8 ± 2.1 in sepsis cohort (p < 0.0001). Median (Q1-Q3) length of stay was 3 (7 to 16) days in the rule-out cohort and 15 (7 to 37) days in the sepsis cohort. Mortality was 1.1% in the rule-out cohort and 3.1% in the sepsis cohort. Analyses using the covariates of gestational age, corrected gestational age and of subsequent sepsis events in the same patient are ongoing. Conclusions: There was a statistically significant difference in Phoenix scores between neonates with sepsis and those who clinically were determined not to have sepsis. Further study, including evaluation of specific score cut-off values, can determine if the Phoenix score can be used to develop a universal definition of neonatal sepsis..
