N2  - Background:Pediatricbacteremia remains a critical cause of morbidity and mortality, particularly in neonates and critically ill children, where early microbiological confirmation is essential for timely targeted therapy. Conventional (manual) blood culture methods are limited by intermittent monitoring and delayed growth recognition, whereas automated blood culture systems provide continuous monitoring and earlier positivity signals, potentially improving diagnostic yield and reducing time-to-detection. Aim: To compare automated blood culture and conventional culture methods in pediatricbacteremia with respect to diagnostic yield, contamination rate, organism profile, time-to-detection, and the influence of prior antibiotic exposure. Materials and Methods: A hospital-based comparative study was conducted in a tertiary care pediatric setting. Ninety-two children with clinical suspicion of bacteremia were included. For each patient, blood samples were processed in parallel using an automated blood culture system and a conventional manual culture method. Culture positivity, contamination rate, organism type (Gram-positive cocci, Gram-negative bacilli, Candida spp.), mono-/polymicrobial growth, and time-to-detection were recorded. The effect of prior antibiotic exposure on automated culture positivity was assessed. Data were analyzed using SPSS version 25.0. Categorical variables were compared using chi-square/Fisher’s exact test and continuous variables using independent t-test; p <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results: The study population comprised predominantly neonates (30.43%) and infants (26.09%), with male preponderance (58.70%). Automated culture showed significantly higher positivity than conventional culture (41.30% vs 29.35%; p = 0.041). Contamination was lower with automated culture (4.35%) compared to conventional (8.70%), though not statistically significant (p = 0.221). Among positive cultures, Gram-positive cocci (47.37% automated vs 48.15% conventional), Gram-negative bacilli (44.74% vs 44.44%), and Candida spp. (7.89% vs 7.41%) were comparable between methods (p >0.05). Mean time-to-detection was significantly shorter with automated culture (18.42 ± 6.35 hours) than conventional culture (46.78 ± 10.54 hours; p <0.001). Prior antibiotic exposure significantly reduced automated culture positivity (34.00% vs 50.00%; p = 0.048). Conclusion: Automated blood culture provides superior diagnostic yield and markedly faster pathogen detection compared with conventional culture in pediatricbacteremia, supporting its clinical utility in tertiary care settings, particularly for early decision-making and antimicrobial optimization.
