N2  - Background: Chronic Suppurative Otitis Media (CSOM) is a long-standing infection of the middle ear associated with ear discharge through a perforated tympanic membrane. It poses a significant public health burden, especially in low- and middle-income countries, due to its complications, including hearing loss and intracranial infections. Emerging antimicrobial resistance among causative pathogens complicates treatment and necessitates periodic surveillance. Methods: A cross-sectional study was conducted over 18 months in the Department of Otolaryngology at RKDF Medical College, Hospital, and Research Centre, Bhopal. A total of 135 patients presenting with active ear discharge and a diagnosis of CSOM were enrolled. Ear discharge samples were collected and subjected to Gram staining, culture, and antimicrobial susceptibility testing using the Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion method. Data were analysed using Stata 17.0, with significance set at p < 0.05. Results: Microbial growth was observed in 62.2% of samples. Among culture-positive cases, 37% showed monobacterial growth, 8.15% polymicrobial bacterial growth, 9.63% mixed bacterial-fungal growth, and 7.41% fungal-only growth. The most frequently isolated bacteria were Staphylococcus aureus (37.84%) and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (35.14%), followed by Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella spp., and Enterobacter spp. Fungal isolates included Aspergillus niger (10.4%), Aspergillus flavus (5.2%), and Candida albicans (1.5%). Both S. aureus and P. aeruginosa showed high sensitivity to aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin) and piperacillin-tazobactam, while resistance was notable against fluoroquinolones and cephalosporins. Conclusion: S. aureus and P. aeruginosa remain the predominant pathogens in CSOM, with increasing resistance to commonly used antibiotics. Fungal pathogens, particularly Aspergillus spp., also play a significant role in chronic or refractory cases. Empirical treatment should be guided by local antimicrobial resistance patterns, and fungal diagnostics should be integrated into standard management. Regular microbiological surveillance and antibiotic stewardship are crucial to optimise CSOM treatment outcomes.
