N2  - Escherichia coli meningitis, while common in neonates, is a rare cause of meningitis in adults. Overall, mortality due to meningitis is improving, but for gram-negative bacteria, it remains high. It often progresses rapidly and, even if treated appropriately, can lead to long-term complications. An emerging concern is bacterial resistance to the empiric antibiotic regimen used in meningitis. Treatment with adjunct dexamethasone continues to be inconclusive on its effect on improving mortality or preventing complications. Here, we discuss a case of severe E. coli meningitis in a 56-year-old woman after a hemorrhoidectomy, leading to brain herniation and cardiac arrest.
