N2  - Background: Neonatal sepsis remains a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in newborns worldwide. Prompt and appropriate antibiotic therapy is critical for successful management. However, variability in prescribing practices can lead to suboptimal outcomes, including antimicrobial resistance and treatment failure. Aim: This study aimed to evaluate antibiotic prescribing practices for the management of neonatal sepsis. Method: A retrospective study was conducted in the neonatal intensive care unit of a private institute in the UAE. The medical records of neonates diagnosed with sepsis and admitted over a 4-year period were reviewed. Data collected included clinical and demographic factors, as well as neonatal parameters such as sex, gestational age, birth weight, clinical condition at admission, duration of hospital stay, and antibiotic exposure. Maternal factors such as maternal age, obstetric history, perinatal complications, and risk factors like fever or prolonged rupture of membranes were also analyzed. Sepsis-related data included pathogen identification, antibiotic susceptibility patterns, and risk factors for sepsis. Statistical analysis was performed to identify patterns of antibiotic prescribing. Results: Among the 25 cases reviewed, empirical antibiotic therapy was initiated in all cases, with 88% receiving treatment within the first hour of diagnosis. The combination of ampicillin and gentamicin was the most commonly prescribed regimen, used in 72% of cases. Broad-spectrum antibiotics, such as meropenem, were used in 16% of cases, primarily in neonates with severe clinical presentations. All antibiotic regimens were adjusted based on culture and sensitivity results when available. The average course length of trea.tment was 7-10 days. Conclusion: Standardized protocols and continuous medical education are essential to improve prescribing practices and ensure better clinical outcomes.
