N2  - Broad-spectrum antibiotics are increasingly used in obstetric patients without long-term follow-up on neonatal risks. Previous data have suggested that use of broad-spectrum antibiotics for preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM) increases the rate of late-onset neonatal sepsis and necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC), but the mechanisms remain poorly elucidated. Breastfeeding has been identified as a protective factor against development of NEC and specifically, decreased levels of maternal mammary immunoglobulin A (IgA) have been implicated in infants with NEC. Broad-spectrum antibiotics have been shown to decrease mammary IgA in mouse models. Chemokines implicated in the regulation of mammary IgA include C-Cmotif ligand 28 (CCL28), C-X-C motif ligand 12 (CXCL12), and polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR). We compared levels of immunoglobulins and chemokines in maternal breastmilk collected between 10 and 45 days postpartum from PPROM patients exposed to different latency antibiotic regimens. Latency antibiotic regimens were classified as "narrow-spectrum" (penicillin/ampicillin) or "broad-spectrum" (second and third generation cephalosporin, extended-spectrum beta-lactams, gentamicin). The duration of antibiotic exposure was also quantified. We hypothesized that exposure to broadspectrum antibiotics and longer duration of antibiotic exposure would be associated with decreased immunoglobulin levels, specifically IgA, in maternal breastmilk. Immunoglobulins (IgA, IgM, and IgG) and chemokines (CXCL12, CCL28, pIgR) were analyzed using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) methods. Breastmilk samples were obtained from the Maternal ImmunoglobulinAControl over theNeonatalMicrobiota and the Development of NEC study, a prospective trial which collected maternal and infant biospecimens from patients who underwent preterm delivery before 32 weeks. Data were compared by t-test and one-way ANOVA. At baseline, mammary IgA was higher than IgG (p < 0.0001) and IgM (p < 0.005). Mammary pIgR was higher than CCL28 (p < 0.05) and CXCL12 (p < 0.05). IgA, IgM, and CCL28 were significantly decreased with any antibiotic exposure compared to no antibiotic exposure (p = 0.02, p = 0.01, p = 0.005, respectively); whereas IgG, CXCL12, and pIgR were unchanged. A stepwise downtrend in both IgA (p = 0.004) and CCL28 (p = 0.005) was observed in association with expanding spectrum of antibiotic regimen (none vs narrow-spectrum vs broad-spectrum). Both IgA (p = 0.02) and CCL28 (p = 0.04) also decreased with increasing duration of antibiotic exposure (none vs perioperative dosing vs < 48 hours vs > 48 hours). CCL28 and IgA in matched samples had a poor positive correlation with an R2 of 0.214. IgG, IgM, CXCL12, and pIgR were not affected by antibiotic spectrum or duration of exposure. Our data suggest that antenatal antibiotic exposure decreases mammary IgA and IgM in human breastmilk and further that duration of antimicrobial exposure and expanding antimicrobial spectrum decrease IgA specifically. Chemokine data suggest that CCL28 may be similarly altered by maternal antibiotics. These data may implicate a regulatory role of CCL28 in mammary IgA secretion and suggest a possible mechanism by which antenatal antibiotics may lead to neonatal sepsis and NEC and suggest that narrow spectrum antibiotics should be prioritized when appropriate.
