N2  - Background: Prolonged antibiotic exposure in uninfected neonates is associated with increased morbidity and mortality. A potential strategy to reduce early-life antibiotic exposure is to identify preterm neonates at low-risk of EOS. We analysed perinatal risk factors for, and incidence of, confirmed EOS and antimicrobial exposure in very preterm infants. Methods: This single center retrospective cohort study included infants < 32 weeks admitted 2012-2022. Infants were considered low-risk if the following were present: caesarean section delivery, ROM < 1 h, and no maternal fever. We compared the incidence of culture-confirmed EOS and antimicrobial exposure between low-risk and not low-risk infants. Antibiotic treatment ≥ 5 days was considered prolonged. Results: Of the total 3295 neonates, 1330 were low-risk (40.4%) and 1965 (59.6%) were not low-risk. Confirmed EOS occurred in 7/1330 (0.5%) of low-risk infants and in 30/1965 (1.5%) of the not low-risk infants. Antibiotics were administered more frequently in the first 48 h of life in not low-risk infants compared to low-risk infants (96.8% vs. 79.6%). However, prolonged antibiotic exposure was similar between the groups (not low-risk 19.1% vs. low-risk 19.1%). Among infants in both groups, most of the prolonged antibiotic exposure was for reasons other than culture-confirmed EOS (low-risk 97% vs. not low-risk 92.9%). Conclusion: Early antibiotic exposure could safely be reduced in LR infants and prolonged antibiotic exposure in both LR and NLR infants < 32 weeks gestation to decrease the associated short-and long-term effects of prolonged antibiotic exposure.
