N2  - Introduction: Prolonged antibiotic treatment in culture-negative infants is associated with short-and long-term complications. We describe antibiotic exposure in preterm neonates and explore the association between prolonged antibiotic exposure and neonatal outcomes. Methods: A single centre, retrospective cohort study was conducted at King Edward Memorial Hospital (KEMH), Perth. Infants < 32 weeks gestation were included; those with major congenital anomalies and/or early-onset sepsis (EOS) were excluded. Antibiotic exposure was measured in days and the antibiotic utilisation rate (AUR) calculated. Antibiotics ≥ 5 days was considered prolonged. Outcomes included mortality, late-onset sepsis (LOS), necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), chronic lung disease (CLD), retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) and/or brain injury. Stata version 17 was used for statistical analyses. Models were adjusted for gestational age, sex, birthweight z-score and year of birth. Results: There were 3265 neonates included of which 2999 received any, 841 received prolonged and 266 received no antibiotics. Total antibiotic days and AUR decreased from 2012 to 2021. AUR was inversely proportional to gestational age. Antibiotics were ceased within 48 h in 1565 (60.4%) infants with no sepsis, NEC and/or SIP and were continued for ≥ 5 days in 572 (22.1%). Prolonged antibiotic exposure was associated with increased mortality (p < 0.001), length of hospital stay (p < 0.001), NEC (p = 0.006), CLD (p < 0.001), significant brain injury (p = 0.020), and the composite of any adverse outcome (p < 0.001). Conclusion: Antimicrobial exposure decreased from 2012 to 2021. However, a significant number of culture-negative infants received prolonged antibiotics. Prolonged antibiotic exposure was associated with increased neonatal morbidity and mortality.
