N2  - Case Report: Introduction: Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is the most common cardiac abnormality in neonates. Significant morbidities are associated with hemodynamically significant PDA (hsPDA) such as bronchopulmonary dysplasia, necrotizing enterocolitis, and death. PDA closure either medically or with an occlusive device (Figure 1) can potentially lead to an improvement in respiratory status. However, percutaneous device closure may be associated with risks such as device malposition, residual shunt, lower limb ischemia, and bacterial endocarditis. Our case describes a case of suspected infective endocarditis (IE) in an extremely lowbirthweight (ELBW) neonate after PDA occluder placement. Case Presentation: A 25w0d 785 g female infant developed pulmonary hemorrhage and severe hypotension on DOL 2 (Table 1). An echocardiogram on DOL 4 demonstrated a large hsPDA. A sepsis work up on DOL 18 revealed coagulase negative staphylococcus (CoNS) and was treated with a 7-day course of vancomycin. However, the peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC) was not removed due to the critical need for a central venous access. Non-improvement in cardio-respiratory status prompted a catheter-based PDA closure on DOL 31. A sepsis evaluation on DOL 53 revealed Staphylococcus capitis. Empiric antibiotics amikacin and nafcillin were replaced with vancomycin following speciation and the PICC was removed on DOL 54. Though blood cultures on DOL 54 and 55 were negative, cultures from DOL 56 grew Staph. Capitis. Suspecting infection of the PDA occluder device, the infant was treated with a two-week course of amikacin, and six-week course of vancomycin and rifampin. Infant recovered well and was discharged home on DOL 154. Discussion: In our ELBW infant, we speculate that the non-removal of PICC during the first episode of sepsis might have contributed to the second episode of CoNS. Since occluder devices are made of synthetic material, they can serve as nidus of infection. The persistently positive blood culture for Staphylococcus capitis even after removal of PICC suggested potential infection of the PDA occluder device. Staph. capitis, though less common, has been described to cause IE in patients with prosthetic valves, pacemakers in children. Though guidelines for treating IE in preterm infants with prosthetic material are lacking, adult guidelines recommend therapy with vancomycin and rifampin for a minimum duration of six weeks, along with amikacin added for synergistic effect during the first two weeks. Conclusion: Premature infants with prosthetic are at high risk for development of late onset sepsis. While catheter related blood stream infections are much more common, other rarer causes of persistent bacteremia should still be considered particularly in infants with prosthetic devices. Hight index of suspicion, prompt recognition, extended antibiotic therapy can be life-saving in such vulnerable patients.
