N2  - Case Report: Bacterial meningitis is a devastating condition in neonates and is most commonly caused by group B streptococcus, E. coli, and Listeria monocytogenes. Among these, Listeria is a significant cause of morbidity and mortality that is often transmitted transplacentally. This case emphasizes the importance of thorough maternal education to limit exposures to Listeria during pregnancy as well as prompt workup, diagnosis, and treatment of suspected neonatal meningitis. Listeria infection in the first week of life typically manifests as severe sepsis. However, as in our case, after the first week of life, it typically presents as a meningitis. A 17-day-old premature (35w2d) femalewas transferred from an outside hospital for further workup of neonatal fever with initial laboratory studies concerning for meningitis. The patient originally presented with mother with a rectal temperature of 101 F with lack of other clinical symptoms. Laboratory testing at the outside hospital was notable for leukocytosis (23.2) and thrombocytosis (960). UA was unremarkable. Procalcitonin was elevated and CRP was within normal limits. Lumbar puncture was performed, and CSF studies revealed pleocytosis (704), elevated RBCs (1,344), elevated protein (175), and normal glucose. The mother's GBS status was unknown at the time of delivery. Per chart review, the mother was treated with vancomycin at the time of delivery. The patient began empiric treatment with ampicillin, ceftazidime, and acyclovir. CSF PCR was positive for Listeria, thus infectious disease was consulted and suggested that ceftazidime be changed to gentamicin for better coverage. Her hospital course was complicated by persistent tachycardia, hypoglycemia, anemia requiring blood transfusion, and bacteremia. She required close monitoring due to concern for clinical instability and was transferred to the PICU for a portion of her hospital stay. Upon receiving a negative HSV PCR test, acyclovir was discontinued. She completed a 21-day course of gentamicin and ampicillin per infectious disease. Her clinical status improved, and she was discharged home. Upon further investigation, her mother reported that she frequently consumed grilled hotdogs throughout her pregnancy, which was felt to be a source of Listeria infection. Empiric antibiotic treatment for suspected neonatal meningitis typically includes ampicillin, gentamicin, and acyclovir; however, our institution uses cephalosporins instead of aminoglycosides due to ease of dosing. Listeria is largely resistant to cephalosporins, thus a regimen with gentamicin is most effective and improves outcomes in this population. Although Listeria is a relatively uncommon cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis, this case emphasizes the importance of maintaining a high level of clinical suspicion in both pregnant individuals with nonspecific symptoms and potential exposures as well as febrile neonates to guide clinical intervention and improve both maternal and fetal outcomes.
