N2  - Case Report: Abstract: A 2-month-old term male with history of milk protein allergy was admitted to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) following an episode of bloody stool. Abdominal radiograph was normal, but ultrasound revealed pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas. The infant was treated for Bell's stage IIA necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC). Viral gastroenteritis PCR panel identified rotavirus A. Mother later reported infant received his rotavirus vaccine 5 days prior to admission. Feeds were restarted without recurrence of bloody stools and infant was discharged home. Introduction: Rotavirus infection causes significant morbidity and mortality worldwide, and can present as watery diarrhea, vomiting, fever, and severe dehydration1–3. Though association between NEC and rotavirus infection has been described, the association between NEC and rotavirus vaccination has not. In fact, vaccination is deemed safe even in preterm infants.4 We present a unique case of an NICU patient with ultrasonographic evidence of NEC, in context of recent rotavirus vaccination. Case: A 2-month-old term male presented to the hospital following an episode of bloody stool. The patient has a long history of constipation, diarrhea, and emesis despite several formula changes. He was eventually diagnosed with milk protein allergy by gastroenterologists. The infantwas admitted after a bloody stool at home. He was well-appearing and abdominal radiograph was unremarkable, but ultrasound revealed pneumatosis and portal venous gas. He was treated for Bell's stage IIA NEC with nil per os status, parenteral nutrition, sepsis evaluation and IV Metronidazole, Ampicillin, and Amikacin. The patient remained well throughout the NICU stay. Feeds were restarted after 7 days of antibiotics, without recurrence of bloody stools. The viral gastroenteritis PCR panel was positive for Rotavirus A. Mother later reported that the infant received the oral rotavirus vaccine 5 days prior to admission as part of his routine 2-month vaccines. Repeat abdominal ultrasound upon reaching full feeds showed resolution of pneumatosis. He was discharged home with gastroenterology follow up for milk protein allergy. Discussion: Rotavirus gastroenteritis has been associated with NEC, but such cases are generally milder and pneumatosis intestinalis tend to be localized to the descending colon and rectum5−8. Even though rotavirus infection has been frequently implicated in NEC, there have not been any described cases of NEC following rotavirus vaccination. Future research should investigate the potential association between NEC and live oral rotavirus vaccine, with hopes of avoiding unnecessary antibiotic exposure. It is important to emphasize that this case should not deter vaccination against rotavirus, since the safety profile of the vaccine is well established, and the benefits unequivocally outweigh the potential risks.
