N2  - Case Report: Case Presentation: This male infant was born by C-section at 36 weeks and 4 days. Signs of fulminant liver failure including hyperbilirubinemia, blueberry muffin rash, coagulopathy, and transaminitis were noted shortly after birth. Initial management included coagulation factor replacement, packed red cell and platelet transfusions, phototherapy, and empiric antibiotics. Cytomegalovirus (CMV) testing and buccal mucosal biopsy confirmed CMV infection and neonatal hemochromatosis (NH) respectively. Valgancyclovir was started and two exchange transfusions and intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) were given for hyperbilirubinemia and NH secondary to presumed gestational alloimmune liver disease (GALD). Ferritin, Interleukin 2, and natural killer cell levels were sent due to suspicion for CMV induced histophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) and results were consonant. The infant was transferred to an outside facility for liver transplant however, transplant was deferred because of low weight. In the interim, treatment for HLH was begun with minimal improvement. Ultimately, the infant died of multiorgan failure prior transplant. Discussion: Multiple etiologies can lead to neonatal acute liver failure (NALF) including but not limited to inborn errors of metabolism, infection, mitochondrial diseases, and NH. Intra- and extra-hepatic iron deposition characterizes NH and the presence of these in magnetic resonance imaging or mucosal biopsy confirms the diagnosis. GALD is the most common cause of NH characterized by maternal immunoglobulin G (IgG) that attack fetal hepatocytes. The gold standard for diagnosis is liver biopsy and treatments include IVIG and exchange transfusions. Congenital infection is another major cause of NALF, of which CMV is most common. Less commonly, congenital infections can trigger HLH. It is unlikely that this infant had both CMV and GALD. This patient's clinical picture is more likely the result of CMV. However, given the rapid progression and high mortality of GALD, it was reasonable to treat empirically in the setting of the positive buccal biopsy result while awaiting further testing results. Without the liver biopsy result, the final diagnosis remains uncertain, but this case highlights the importance of maintaining a broad differential for NALF and not delaying treatment for multiple potential etiologies while completing workup.
