N2  - Case Report: A male neonate was born at 37 weeks GA by STAT CS complicated by breech extraction. Infant required immediate resuscitation, but quickly returned to baseline following initial interventions including noninvasive respiratory support. Apgar scores were 1 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes of life. A subgaleal hemorrhage was noted on physical exam. The infant was admitted to couplet care for routine post-natal care but was transferred to the NICU at 24 hours of life for closer vital sign monitoring and frequent lab checks given an increasing subgaleal hemorrhage. He was placed on continuous pulse oximetry at that time. Around 36 hours of life, he had two apneic events requiring O2 therapy via nasal cannula. VBG, CBC, BMP, and blood culturewere collected, a lumbar puncture was performed, and empiric antibiotics and antivirals including Ampicillin, Gentamicin, and Acyclovir were started. CSF results were as follows: gram stain without microorganisms, protein 100 mg/dl, glucose 87 mg/dl, and a negative PCR panel. The infant's blood culture grew gram negative bacilli at six hours of incubation, concerning for neonatal sepsis secondary to Escherichia coli bacteremia. Meropenem was initiated. CSF culture remained without growth. Infant's admission was further complicated by septic shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation requiring urgent intubation. He was initially placed on conventional ventilation but was transitioned to high frequency ventilation due to progressive hypoxia. He required further resuscitation with normal saline boluses, platelets, cryoprecipitate, fresh frozen plasma, and packed RBC transfusions with mild improvement in perfusion. He was started on stress dose hydrocortisone and inhaled nitric oxide then was transferred to a higher level of care. An echo was obtained and significant for pulmonary hypertension, nitric oxide and hydrocortisone were continued and weaned as tolerated. Blood products were administered as needed. Antibiotics were transitioned to Cefepime, and he completed a full 10-day course without further concerns. He was extubated on DOL 9 and weaned to room air on DOL 14. Enteral feeds were initiated and the patient was breastfeeding at goal prior to discharge. Infant was discharged home in good health on DOL 18. Early onset sepsis (EOS) remains a significant cause of morbidity and mortality in newborn infants, with group B Streptococcus (GBS) and Escherichia coli the most common etiologic agents. Although risk factors related to neonatal sepsis are well established, it is important to recognize that the clinical signs and symptoms vary significantly depending on newborn size and gestational age. Subtle changes in respiratory status can suggest an infection that can quickly become life-threatening. Although respiratory distress can often be attributed to less severe etiologies, especially in term infants, it is essential that clinicians consider sepsis as their initial differential diagnosis and perform a thorough work-up.
