N2  - Purpose: Early onset sepsis (EOS), defined as sepsis occurring in the first 72 hours after birth, poses a serious threat to neonates in hospital NICUs. Sepsis can be difficult to diagnose in neonates. Clinical symptoms can be vague, inflammatory markers such as CRP and CBC values have high false positive rates, and empiric antibiotics are initiated based on risk factors and frequently continued for longer duration especially in preterm infants. In a culture negative setting there are no standardized guidelines for duration of antibiotic administration, leading to unnecessary prolonged exposure to antibiotics. Studies have shown that neonatal exposure to antibiotics can alter the gut microbiome and increase morbidities during hospital stay in VLBW infants. The purpose of this study was to determine whether neonates born at 30–34 weeks gestation experience any clinical benefit from receiving antibiotics for over 48 hours in the setting of culture-negative EOS. Methods: This was a retrospective chart review for 209 neonates born 30–34 weeks gestation between January and December 2023 at the Regional One Health NICU in Memphis, TN. Neonates who were culture positive, were diagnosed with congenital syphilis, or who died from congenital abnormalities within the first week of life were excluded from the cohort. From the remaining 195 charts, we collected data on infant demographics, clinical signs and symptoms in the firstweek of life,maternal risk factors, and antibiotic use and duration. Neonates were assigned to one of three groups based on antibiotic usage: no antibiotics in the first 48 hours of life (N = 55), received <48 h antibiotics (N = 119), or received ≥48 h antibiotics (N = 21). Clinical outcomes like length of hospital stay (LOS), length of respiratory support required, necrotizing enterocolitis stage 2 or 3 (NEC), and sepsis recurrence were compared using two tailed t-tests or chi-square test. Results: Infants given a prolonged (≥48 h) antibiotic course were significantly more likely to have a lower gestational age (mean 31.6 weeks compared to 33.2 and 32.2 for the no antibiotics and <48 h groups, respectively); longer length of stay (average 47.2 days compared to 20.4 and 28.4); and longer duration of respiratory support (average 16.4 days compared to 3.4 and 0.5). There was no statistically significant difference across groups for sepsis recurrence. Notably, NEC was significantly more likely to occur after a prolonged course of antibiotics compared to infants who were given a short course (p = 0.04). Table shows comparison of demographics and clinical outcomes. Conclusions: In this cohort of relatively mature babies between 30–34wk EGA, we do not see any clinical benefit to prolonged administration of antibiotics in the setting of culture-negative suspected EOS. There may be an increase in NEC incidence in this group of babies when exposed to prolonged antibiotics.
