N2  - Rationale: The differential for a neonate with hematochezia includes non-IgE-mediated cow’s milk allergy (CMA) and necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC). This study investigated whether CMA and NEC differ in clinical presentation, laboratory findings, imaging, and resulting treatment. We hypothesized that both term and preterm neonates experience CMA due to exposure to cow's milk protein in breast milk and formula. Methods: Charts from 176 neonates admitted to the University of Rochester neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) from 2017 to 2023 diagnosed with NEC, CMA, or hematochezia were reviewed. Patients were categorized into: NEC, CMA, mixed presentation, or other diagnoses. Kruskal-Wallis, chi-square, and Fisher’s exact tests analyzed between-group differences in presentation, birth/medical history, feeding habits, imaging, labs, and treatment. Results: 162 pre-term and 14 full-term infants were included and categorized as NEC (n=94), CMA (n=42), mixed NEC and CMA (n=32), or other diagnoses (n=8). Differences between groups existed for gestational age at birth and at diagnosis (p<0.0001), birth weight (p<0.0001), days to first enteral feed (p=0.0111), days to first symptom (p=0.0001), nutrition source at presentation (p<0.0001), duration of antibiotic course (p<0.0001), presence of imaging findings (p<0.0026), and serum eosinophilia (p=0.0029). Birth weight, gestational age at birth, and absolute serum eosinophils were highest in CMA. Nutritional management differences existed with transition to hydrolyzed formula (p<0.0001) and nutrition source at discharge (p<0.0001). Conclusions: Term and premature neonates experience CMA and differ from those with NEC in clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings. Some neonates experience a mixed presentation, indicating need for diagnostic tools for CMA in the NICU.
