N2  - Background: Neonatal infections represent a major source of morbidity and mortality in neonatal intensive care units (NICUs), especially in underdeveloped nations. The rise of antimicrobial resistance in both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms complicates treatment options, necessitating ongoing monitoring of local microbial populations and their susceptibility patterns. Objective: To evaluate the prevalence and antibiotic susceptibility profiles of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria isolated from NICU admissions at a tertiary care facility in Bihar during a one-year duration. Methods: This prospective observational study was carried out at NSMCH, Bihta, spanning a duration of 12 months (from July 14, 2023, to July 13, 2024). A total of 112 neonates with presumed sepsis were included. Blood cultures were conducted, and isolates were identified by normal microbiological techniques. Antibiotic susceptibility testing was performed via the Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion method, with results interpreted in accordance with CLSI recommendations. Results: Among the 112 samples, Gram-negative bacteria constituted 64.3% of the isolates, whilst Gram-positive organisms represented 35.7%. Klebsiella pneumoniae was the most prevalent Gram-negative isolate at 28.6%, followed by Escherichia coli at 18.8%, whilst Staphylococcus aureus was the major Gram-positive organism at 20.5%. Gram-negative isolates exhibited significant resistance to third-generation cephalosporins, although shown increased susceptibility to carbapenems and colistin. Gram-positive bacteria exhibited significant resistance to penicillin and erythromycin, however shown considerable vulnerability to vancomycin and linezolid. Conclusion: The prevalence of Gram-negative pathogens in NICU infections and their escalating resistance to frequently utilized antibiotics underscore the necessity for regular antibiogram revisions and rigorous antibiotic management. Robust infection control policies and empirical treatment guidelines informed by local susceptibility data are essential for enhancing newborn outcomes.
