N2  - Objective To analyze perinatal risk factors for asphyxia and its association with in-hospital adverse outcomes in very preterm infants under 32 weeks' gestation. Methods This multicenter prospective cohort study utilized data from the Sino-northern Neonatal Network. Clinical records of very preterm infants born at 23-31 weeks' gestation and admitted to neonatal intensive care units across 53 hospitals between January 1, 2018, and December 31, 2024, were analyzed. Infants were classified into severe asphyxia, mild asphyxia, and no asphyxia groups. Risk factors for asphyxia and its association with adverse outcomes were evaluated using Chi square test, Wilcoxon rank-sum test, and multivariate logistic regression. Results Among 10 256 very preterm infants included, 5 861 (57.1%) were male. The incidence of severe and mild asphyxia was 10.4% (1 068/10 256) and 38.4% (3 938/10 256), respectively. Of the 1 783 extremely preterm infants born at <28 weeks (17.4%), 24.1% (429/1 783) had severe asphyxia and 55.0% (981/1 783) had mild asphyxia. Multivariable logistic regression analysis demonstrated that, in addition to gestational age and birth weight serving as significant protective factors, antenatal corticosteroid use [adjusted odds ratio (aOR)=0.793 for mild asphyxia; aOR=0.551 for severe asphyxia] and antibiotic exposure (aOR=0.884 for mild asphyxia; aOR=0.793 for severe asphyxia) were also protective factors against both asphyxia severities, with 95% confidence intervals (CI) of 0.714-0.881 and 0.801-0.975 for mild asphyxia, and 0.465-0.653 and 0.668-0.941 for severe asphyxia, respectively. Placental abruption (aOR=5.153, 95%CI: 3.390-7.832 for severe; aOR=2.537, 95%CI: 1.845-3.489 for mild), hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (aOR=1.420, 95%CI: 1.164-1.734 for severe; aOR=1.361, 95%CI: 1.206-1.536 for mild), and early-onset sepsis (aOR=1.495, 95%CI: 1.259-1.774 for severe; aOR=1.357, 95%CI: 1.219-1.512 for mild) were identified as independent risk factors for both severe and mild asphyxia. Grade Ⅲ meconium-stained amniotic fluid was an independent risk factor specifically for severe asphyxia (aOR=1.514, 95%CI: 1.133-2.023), while multiple gestation was an independent risk factor for mild asphyxia (aOR=1.406, 95%CI: 1.250-1.582). Both severe and mild asphyxia significantly increased the risk of mortality (aOR=3.622, 95%CI: 2.875-4.564; and aOR=1.911, 95%CI: 1.592-2.293, respectively). Additionally, severe asphyxia was associated with an increased risk of severe intraventricular hemorrhage (aOR=2.935, 95%CI: 2.047-4.209) and moderate-to-severe bronchopulmonary dysplasia (aOR=1.367, 95%CI: 1.054-1.774). Conclusions Asphyxia is highly prevalent among very preterm infants under 32 weeks, especially those born before 28 weeks, and significantly increases risks of mortality and major morbidities, such as severe intraventricular hemorrhage and moderate-to-severe bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Improved management of placental abruption, hypertensive disorders, and early-onset sepsis, along with antenatal corticosteroid administration and rational antibiotic use, may reduce asphyxia and improve outcomes.
