N2  - Purpose: Early onset sepsis (EOS), defined as systemic infection within 72 hours of life, significantly contributes to newborn morbidity and mortality. Traditionally, EOS evaluation included 36-48 hours of empiric antibiotics. A 2019 study analyzing neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) blood cultures for EOS found that 91% of gram-positive cultures and 98.6% of gram-negative cultures were positive at 24 hours. In April 2023, Cone Health shifted to a 24-hour antibiotic rule out for EOS to decrease antibiotic exposure and enhance standard of care. This project evaluated whether a shortened 24-hour protocol safely provides sufficient empiric antibiotic coverage to neonates evaluated for EOS. Methods: A dual-site, IRB-reviewed and determined exempt, retrospective cohort review was conducted to evaluate neonates within the Cone Health System who received an EOS rule-out course of antibiotics within the first 72 hours of life between May 1, 2023, and January 1, 2024. Patients were identified using an electronic health record (EHR) report based on the presence of antibiotic orders within this time frame. Exclusion criteria included initiation of antibiotics after 72 hours of life. Retrospective chart reviews were performed to assess baseline characteristics, including factors known to increase infection risk, type and duration of antibiotic use, and culture results. The primary outcome was incidence of neonatal early onset sepsis with confirmed or suspected bacteremia/meningitis. Culture results analyzed the incidence of positive blood cultures, time to positivity, pathogen detected, and incidence of repeat sepsis rule-out courses within the first seven days of life. Data was analyzed using descriptive statistics.
