N2  - Purpose: In neonatal intensive care, it is known that premature infants are at risk for the development of necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC). While the development of NEC is well recognized and a variety of treatment strategies have been described, a standardized approach to therapy at Sacred Heart Medical Center (SHMC) has not yet been developed. Because of this, NEC management with antibiotics and bowel rest may vary based on provider preference or perceived severity of illness. This project will evaluate primary literature and review current NEC management at SHMC to evaluate inconsistencies in care and create a standardized, evidence-based treatment algorithm. Methods: For this project, patients with the diagnosis of NEC in the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) at Sacred Heart Medical Center & Children's Hospital from January of 2022 to September of 2024 will be reviewed for data including risk factors for the development of NEC and subsequent management strategies. Patients with a diagnosis of NEC will be identified using SlicerDicer (a data exploration tool in EPIC). Patients will be included if they have a diagnosis of NEC and a gestational age less than 37 weeks. Neonates with heart defects will also be included. A retrospective chart review will then be conducted to collect data on administration of probiotics and steroids prior to diagnosis, feeding regimen prior to diagnosis, antibiotic duration and selection, number of days being nothing by mouth (NPO) and whether the patient required surgical intervention. This data, along with literature review, will be used to evaluate current trends in NEC management at SHMC and create a standardized, evidence-based treatment algorithm for patients with evidence of NEC. Post implementation of the algorithm, the same data points will be collected to assess adherence, review any differences in patient characteristics and identify opportunities for further education. Descriptive statistics will be used to compare pre-implementation and post-implementation data. Based on the data and literature review, education on NEC management will be provided to providers and pharmacists.
