N2  - Purpose: Automatic Stop Orders (ASOs) are recommended by national antimicrobial stewardship guidelines as a strategy to improve antibiotic prescribing. ASOs are often effective for specific indications, such as surgical prophylaxis and community-acquired pneumonia; however, for other infections, ASOs can lead to premature discontinuation or prolonged broad-spectrum empiric therapy. In 2016, ASOs were implemented across Ascension Seton, but due to an increase in unintentional antibiotic discontinuations, ASOs were revoked in 2024. This study will assess the impact of discontinuing ASOs on appropriate duration of antibiotic therapy within a multi-center health-system. Methods: This is a retrospective, multi-center cohort study evaluating the effects of discontinuing ASOs in adult hospitals on the length of antibiotic therapy. Data will be collected from January 2023 to March 2025. The study will include adult patients aged 18 years and older who received at least 48 hours of intravenous antibiotics. Both ICU and medical floor patients will be included. Patients receiving prophylactic antibiotics, those with multiple concurrent infections, or those with an ICD-10 diagnosis of community acquired pneumonia or febrile neutropenia will be excluded. A matched-pair design will be used to compare the pre- and post-ASO groups, with patients matched based on infection type. The primary outcome is the total length of antibiotic therapy before and after the discontinuation of ASOs. Secondary outcomes include missed doses, clinical worsening associated with these missed doses, rates of Clostridioides difficile infection within 90 days from the first antimicrobial dose, all-cause in-hospital mortality and 30-day all-cause readmission from discharge. A Mann-Whitney U test or Student's t-test will be used to assess the primary outcome and appropriate statistical tests will be used to analyze secondary outcomes.
