N2  - BACKGROUND: Bacterial meningitis is a serious infection of the central nervous system that is associated with a high mortality rate and the risk of permanent neurological damage such as cerebral palsy and developmental delay. In neonates, especially preterm infants, diagnosis is challenging due to nonspecific symptoms that often overlap with other conditions. The distinction between sepsis, meningitis or their coexistence is essential to guide appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment. Studies in adults suggest that cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) lactate may be an early and accessible diagnostic biomarker, with a threshold of 4 mmol/L to initiate therapy. The aim was to assess the accuracy of CSF lactate measurement using a POCT (not CE-IVDR certified for CSF matrix) and compare it with the laboratory reference method, addressing the need for rapid turnaround and minimal sample volume in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU). METHODS: CSF lactate was measured using the RAPIDPoint 500 blood gas analyzer (Siemens Healthineers) and the Cobas C702 (Roche) on 286 samples from 141 patients admitted to the ASST Spedali Civili in Brescia, including 95 samples from 9 reservoir- bearing neonates in NICU. Statistical analysis, performed with Analyse-it software, included Passing- Bablok regression and Bland-Altman comparison. RESULTS: CSF lactate data from Spedali Civili inpatients (2020-2025), generated by the Central Laboratory, were processed with the APS simulator software (Clin Chem Lab Med 2025; 63(7):1292-1300) to determine the acceptable bias at the 4 mmol/L cut-off. RESULTS: Passing-Bablok analysis showed a proportional bias (slope=0.89; 95% CI 0.860.91; intercept=-0.10; 95% CI 0.050.16). The Bland-Altman analysis revealed a systematic mean difference of -5.19% between the two instruments. This bias was within the Analytical Performance Specification of ±7%, ensuring <5% misclassification at the diagnostic threshold. CONCLUSIONS: The blood gas analyzer delivers clinically reliable and fit-for-purpose CSF lactate values and supports early meningitis diagnosis. Its low sample volume requirement makes it highly suitable for use in NICU settings.
