N2  - Purpose of Study Early onset sepsis (EOS) is a serious condition characterized by systemic infections-fungal, bacterial, or viral-occurring within the first 72 hours of life. These infections often present with non-specific symptoms, leading to the broad use of sepsis rule-out protocols to mitigate infant mortality. The most common pathogens causing EOS are Group B Streptococcus (GBS) and E. coli, accounting for 43% and 29% of neonatal infections, respectively. These protocols, while effective in reducing rates of EOS, contribute to a high antibiotic and financial burden among NICU families, prompting researchers to explore the safety and efficacy of shortening EOS antibiotic rule-out protocols from 48 to 36 hours. A review at our institution revealed that all true positive EOS cases had a time to positivity (TTP) of less than 24 hours, with positive cultures beyond this timeframe later identified as contaminants. Based on this finding, in April 2022, our Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) further reduced the duration of antibiotic coverage for EOS workups from 36 to 24 hours and secondary analysis was performed to confirm the safety and efficacy of this protocol change. Methods Used This study analyzed data collected from January 1, 2018, to December 31, 2023, with the transition from a 36 to 24-hour rule-out occurring on April 25, 2022. Inclusion criteria included infants admitted to the NICU younger than 72 hours of age and exhibiting signs of sepsis, such as temperature instability, respiratory distress, or hypoglycemia, prompting the initiation of a sepsis rule-out protocol. Patients with positive cultures were further evaluated to determine if the results indicated true infections or contamination. Analysis was conducted using R Studio 2023.06.1+524. Summary of Results Initial analysis confirmed no statistically significant differences between the cohorts receiving 36 vs. 24-hour EOS rule-outs regarding gestational age, birth weight, chronological age, or delivery method. Analysis of the positive cultures revealed that E. coli and GBS were the most commonly identified organisms, consistent with existing data, and all these cultures had a TTP of less than 24 hours. Cultures with a TTP greater than 24 hours were subsequently found to be contaminants. Further analysis shown below in Figure 1 indicated a decrease in total days of antibiotics used for EOS per month in the NICU following the protocol change. Conclusions Our analysis demonstrated that true positive EOS cultures typically have a TTP of less than 24 hours, supporting the transition from a 36 to 24- hour sepsis rule-out. The protocol change led to decreased antibiotic use in our department. While our data reflect only our local environment and may not apply to other NICUs due to institution-specific factors such as laboratory protocols, staffing, and antibiotic administration times, we hope our analysis can guide other NICUs in determining the shortest safe duration of antibiotic coverage to reduce the antibiotic burden within this population.
