N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Reports Posters (BF) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Salmonella meningitis is a rare Gram-negative bacilli enterobacteriaceae and not often seen compared to other bacterial pathogens of the central nervous system (CNS). These infections result in significant neurological damage and have been seen to have a higher mortality rate than other bacterial meningitis infections(1). Newborns, children and immunosuppressed adults are most commonly affected(2,3,4). In immunocompetent adults, incidence is limited to reported cases(5,6,7,8,9). Here we present a case of salmonella meningitis in an immunocompetent host with no predisposing risk factors. CASE PRESENTATION: A 37-year-old female with no relevant past medical history presented with progressive encephalopathy for one week to an outside hospital after reported seizure-like activity and unresponsiveness by family. Patient was intubated due to poor mental status, had blood cultures drawn, started on empiric antibiotics ceftriaxone, vancomycin, and acyclovir. Naltrexone was administered for possible drug overdose without improvement and she was transferred to our facility. Cerebral spinal fluid (CSF) was collected. CSF results showed white blood cells 10,231, red blood cells 2,684, opening pressure 34, closing pressure 17, xanthochromic 93% neutrophils 5% lymphocytes 2% monocytes. These finding were most consistent with bacterial meningitis. Initial blood and CSF cultures resulted positive for gram-negative bacilli which speciated salmonella group D enterica. CT scan of the head showed diffuse cerebral edema with concern for hydrocephalus requiring extra-ventricular drain placement. Patient did improve on antibiotic therapy but encephalopathy persisted throughout hospital stay, requiring tracheostomy and feeding tube placement on day 10. She was discharged after 17 days but neurological exam remained limited. DISCUSSION: Of the approximately 1.4 million annual salmonella infections in the US, the vast majority cause intestinal manifestations such as gastroenteritis. Infection most commonly occurs through the feco-oral route. Typical symptom presentations are fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramping and diarrhea. Extra intestinal complications occur if salmonella enters the bloodstream by invading through the abdominal barrier. Bacteremia occurs in 5-10% of infected patients and can evolve into focal infections such as meningitis, bone, joint infections. These complications are more often seen in newborns and immunocompromised hosts. Less than 1% of Salmonella infections are associated with meningitis(10,11). Unusually, our patient presented primarily with encephalopathy with almost no intestinal complaints and no risk factors for immunocompromise. Lumbar puncture with CSF studies is the gold standard for diagnosing bacterial meningitis and was key to determining the pathogen in our patient. Rapid administration of antibiotics after CSF collection can be live-saving(2). Surgical intervention with ventricular drains may be warranted to reduce intracranial pressure if hydrocephalus occurs. This also allows continuous intracranial pressure monitoring and access to CSF for serial cultures(12). Definitive treatment has not been established; various antibiotic regimens including chloramphenicol, ampicillin, cephalosporins, and fluoroquinolones have been administered with various success(13,14). Our patient was treated with meropenem, but had limited neurological improvement. CONCLUSIONS: Bacterial meningitis is a life-threatening infection that if not immediately treated can result in death. This case highlights the importance of CSF studies and rapid antibiotic administration even with atypical infections such as salmonella. REFERENCE #1: Molyneux E, Walsh A, Phiri A, Molyneux M. Acute bacterial meningitis in children admitted to the Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital, Blantyre, Malawi in 1996-97. Trop Med Int Health. 1998;3(8):610-618. doi:10.1046/j.1365-3156.1998.00278.x REFERENCE #2: van de Beek D, de Gans J, Spanjaard L, Weisfelt M, Reitsma JB, Vermeulen M. Clinical features and prognostic factors in adults with bacterial meningitis [published correction appears in N Engl J Med. 2005 Mar 3;352(9):950]. N Engl J Med. 2004;351(18):1849-1859. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa040845 REFERENCE #3: Olsen SJ, Bishop R, Brenner FW, et al. The changing epidemiology of salmonella: trends in serotypes isolated from humans in the United States, 1987-1997. J Infect Dis. 2001;183(5):753-761. doi:10.1086/318832. Schuchat A, Robinson K, Wenger JD, et al. Bacterial meningitis in the United States in 1995. Active Surveillance Team. N Engl J Med. 1997;337(14):970-976. doi:10.1056/NEJM199710023371404. Nadeem R, Elahi N, Elhoufi A, Elsousi A, Chaudhry M. Salmonella typhi Meningitis in an Immunocompetent Asian Adult. Cureus. 2019;11(11):e6112. Published 2019 Nov 10. doi:10.7759/cureus.6112. Carr BG, Weisbein JL, Gaieski DF. Salmonella meningitis in an immunocompetent adult. J Emerg Med. 2011;40(3):267-270. doi:10.1016/j.jemermed.2007.11.046. Dower J, Lerner D, Geva T, Wener K. The Risk of immunosuppression: A case of Salmonella Meningitis.Hindawi. 2018; 48744575. Published 2018 May 21. doi.org/10.1155/2018/4874575. Kauffman CA, St Hilaire RJ. Salmonella meningitis. Occurrence in an adult. Arch Neurol. 1979;36(9):578-580. doi:10.1001/archneur.1979.00500450072015. Karim, M., Islam, N. Salmonella Meningitis: Report of Three Cases in Adults and Literature Review. Infection 30, 104–108 (2002). Shu-Kee Eng, Priyia Pusparajah, Nurul-Syakima Ab Mutalib, Hooi-Leng Ser, Kok-Gan Chan & Learn-Han Lee (2015) Salmonella: A review on pathogenesis, epidemiology and antibiotic resistance, Frontiers in Life Science, 8:3, 284-293, DOI: 10.1080/21553769.2015.1051243. Chen HM, Wang Y, Su LH, Chiu CH. Nontyphoid salmonella infection: microbiology, clinical features, and antimicrobial therapy. Pediatr Neonatol. 2013;54(3):147-152. doi:10.1016/j.pedneo.2013.01.010. Munakomi S, M Das J. Ventriculostomy. [Updated 2023 Aug 13]. In: StatPearls [Internet]. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing; 2024 Jan. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545317/. Owusu-Ofori A, Scheld WM. Treatment of Salmonella meningitis: two case reports and a review of the literature. Int J Infect Dis 2003;7:53–60. Srifuengfung, S., Chokephaibulkit, K., Yungyuen, T., & Tribuddharat, C. (2005). Salmonella meningitis and antimicrobial susceptibilities. Southeast Asian Journal of Tropical Medicine & Public Health, 36(2), 312-316. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Zachary Hodge No disclosure on file for Pranav Mahadaven No relevant relationships by Sarah Ellen Stephens No relevant relationships by Matthew Thomas
