N2  - Background-Aim: Acute infections and septicemia are a common cause of severe morbidity and high mortality. Sepsis, defined as a life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dis regulated host-response to infection, is a worldwide highly prevalent syndrome in intensive care and pediatric age group. The best strategy of sepsis management is its early detection and timely and specific therapy with adequate antibiotics. However, there is a huge challenge of picking correct case and differentiating it from non-infectious causes. Furthermore, there is also a serious issue of correct antibiotic therapy. The incorrect choice of antimicrobial therapies lead to an increased risk for opportunistic infections, resistances to multiple antimicrobial agents and toxic side effects, which not only increase mortality but enhances costs at individual, and governmental level. The use of blood biomarkers has great potential to improve sepsis detection and timely care. In this regard, the predictive markers that are accurate and could be performed and outcome gained in brief time are ideal. Moreover, repeatedly measured biomarkers also help in monitoring patients for tailoring therapy according to individual need. This study is aimed to assess the role of PCT in diagnosing acute bacterial infections and sepsis and monitoring the diagnosed patients for judicious use of antibiotics Methods: This will be a retrospective facility based study, comparing the results of pro calcitonin with blood culture results for ICU PICU and NICU patients. Results: The mean age of patients was 33.83 ± 25.79 years. There were 69 (44.5 %) patients below 5 years of age. Majority were males 89 (57.4 %). The most frequent organisms identified was Escherichia coli 13 (9.1 %), klebsiella pneumonia 6 (4.2 %), pseudomonas aeruginosa 8 (5.6 %), staphylococcus aureus 6 (4.2 %) and enterococcus faecalis 6 (4.2 %). The sensitivity and specificity of PCT in detecting acute bacterial systemic infection/sepsis was 72 % and 60.8 % respectively. Whereas Positive Predictive Value and Negative Predictive Value were found out to be 69.6 % and 63.6 % respectively. Conclusions: PCT is highly sensitive marker of acute severe bacterial infections and sepsis. PCT has promising role in detecting early and delayed onset bacterial infections and may decrease use of unnecessary antibiotic therapy.
