N2  - Background: Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC) predominantly affects preterm infants, being rare in term infants (∼13%) and has a multifactorial etiology, most common being prematurity, gastrointestinal tract immaturity, and enteral feeding. Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) is the most common arrhythmia presenting in neonatal period. Case Description: A 7 day old term infant with uncomplicated vaginal delivery, weighing 3.5 Kg was admitted to an outside hospital for fever, lethargy and poor feeding and was found have SVT. He received multiple doses of adenosine and DC cardioversion, but continued to have SVT, and was transferred to our institution for further management. On presentation, patient was in SVT with rates of 280-300/min (confirmed on surface ECG). He was given two doses of adenosine 0.1 mg/kg followed by 0.2 mg/kg which reverted him to sinus rhythm. Echocardiogram showed a structurally normal heart with mild LV dysfunction. Patient continued to have multiple episodes of tachycardia and was started on amiodarone infusion. On Day 8 of life, baby developed mild abdominal distension with a yellow-green aspirate. Stool for occult blood was negative. USG abdomen showed intramural hypoechoic foci in some bowel loops, suspicious of NEC. Patient was started on empiric antibiotics and made NPO. Patient did not show any clinical deterioration, abdomen was soft on further serial examinations and there was no furtherOGtube aspirate. He was started on trophic feeds and was gradually transitioned to full feeds. He did not have any further episodes of SVT and Amiodarone was stopped after 48 hours and oral propranolol was initiated. Baby was discharged on Day 12 after full feeds were established and there were no further episodes of SVT. Conclusion: Recurrent episodes of SVT can lead to gut ischemia and reperfusion injury. Our patient developed clinical signs suspicious of NEC, which subsided. On review of literature, about 7-8 case reports mention SVT as an etiology for NEC. Thus, there should be a high index of suspicion for NEC in any infant presenting with recurrent SVT. So far there are only case reports to substantiate this claim and further detailed studies are required to elucidate an association between SVT and NEC.
