N2  - Background and Objectives: Bacterial sepsis is a leading cause of mortality and morbidity in neonates, highlighting the importance of early recognition and prompt antibiotic treatment. In practice there are overlapping symptoms with viral illnesses, and there is a tendency to overtreat neonates with empirical antibiotics to prevent a missed sepsis. This study aimed to assess ED practice prior to implementation of a sepsis risk screening tool with automated investigation ordering and antibiotic guidelines. Methods: Retrospective descriptive study of all presentations aged less than 3 months to the two EDs in the Australian Capital Territory (population 440,000) over 12 months from 11-Nov-2022. Charts of presentations with a recorded clinical suspicion of infection/sepsis or with subsequent urine or blood cultures confirming infection, were analyzed, subdivided by age under and over 28 days. Results: Of 148,366 presentations, 1377 were less than 3 months (0.93%, 95% CI: 0.88-0.98), 73 seen in both EDs within 24 h. 1129 were analyzed, 610 with suspicion and 80 with positive culture. 37% (33%-41%) of these had a urine culture, with 40.4% (34%-47%) showing contamination, all attempted «clean catch.» 45% (41%-49%) had blood cultures with a significant finding rate of 5.2% (3.1%-8.6%). 8.9% had lumbar puncture, 11.9% (8%-17%) in under 28 days olds and 7.4% (5%-10%) in over. Antibiotics were given to 38% (32%-45%) of under 28 days olds and 26% (22%-31%) of those over. Excluding oral antibiotics for visible superficial infections, only 26% of the 170 patients given antibiotics had a radiology or culture result consistent with bacterial infection. Antibiotic choices showed low consistency, the most common choice (IV Cefotaxime and Benzyl Penicillin) comprising 36% (28%-44%). Based on cultures, 24 cases missed in ED were identified, of which bacterial co-infection to viral illness was the most common (8/24), with 5/8 due to the patient having a positive COVID test. Conclusion: These EDs currently demonstrates a pattern of under-screening and overtreatment with inadequate antibiotics stewardship. There were high urine contamination rates, indicating that «clean catch» as undertaken is not suitable for this age group. A gap also exists in the form of insufficient consideration given for bacterial co-infection to viral illnesses, especially in the context of a positive COVID test. The sepsis screening tool aims to address these shortfalls.
