N2  - Background: Antibiotic therapy for suspected and confirmed bacterial sepsis is common in the neonatal intensive care unit. The potential adverse effects of unwarranted antibiotic therapy are increasingly recognised and highlight the importance of antibiotic stewardship. Robust data on antibiotic exposure in relation suspected and confirmed neonatal sepsis are needed to inform such efforts. Methods: This is a retrospective cohort study from the sole tertiary neonatal intensive care unit in Western Australia. All infants admitted between January 1, 2012 and June 30, 2022, were included. Results: A total of 23,396 infants were admitted; 1134 (4.8%) were born <28 weeks, 2177 (9.3%) 28-31+6 weeks, 8103 (34.6%) 32-36+6 weeks and 11982 >37 weeks gestation. During their birth-related admission, 12,540 infants (53.6%, 95% CI 53.0%-54.2%) were treated with antibiotics for a median of 6 days (IQR: 5-8). There were 409 (3.3%) cases of blood culturepositive sepsis. Of these, 107 (26.2%) infants had a positive blood culture within 48h of birth, with an early-onset sepsis rate of 4.57/1,000 infants. There were 302 culture-positive late-onset sepsis cases with a rate of 12.96/1,000 infants. 12,131 infants received antibiotic therapy with a negative blood culture result; of these 2,653 infants received antibiotic treatment for <5 days, while 9,478 infants received antibiotic treatment for ≥5 days. Conclusions: Antibiotic exposure is common during the neonatal period, especially in preterm infants, and greatly exceeds the rate of confirmed sepsis. Novel decision tools are urgently required to assist clinicians in safely reducing antibiotic exposure in the neonatal intensive care unit.
