N2  - Background: Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) may be associated with renal injury and sometimes with co-existing risk factors for early onset infection. Commonly used empiric antibiotic regimens include gentamicin, which can cause nephrotoxicity and/or ototoxicity if elevated levels occur. As a quality improvement project, we reviewed our unit data on antibiotic use in infants with moderate or severe HIE for a 5-year period. Methods: Specifically, infants diagnosed with HIE from May 2018 to March 2023 were identified and data on antibiotic exposure, positive culture and gentamicin levels collected. The highest gentamicin level was documented with elevated levels defined as trough >1mg/L. In addition, referral to audiology was audited against standard practice and outcome recorded. Results: Eighty-six babies were identified as having HIE with 42 outborn. The majority 69/86 (80%) received gentamicin including 54 (78.2%) who had documented trough levels with a median (range) 1.6 (0.4-4.4) mg/L. Notably 39/69 (56%) had elevated levels above 1mg/L. Only three infants had positive cultures including Group B Streptococcus, Haemophilus Influenza and coagulase negative staph. The 14 babies who died had particularly high levels (median gent trough: 3.05 (1.2-3.4) mg/L. As both HIE and high gent levels are indications for audiology referral 65 babies were referred for audiology follow-up. Conclusions: These data indicate significant potential for ototoxicity and associated adverse outcome so we are currently monitoring the data on audiology outcomes. Importantly, as 42/86 (49%) were retrieved from other units any practice change will require education not only in our unit but the referring units.
