N2  - Case Report: Introduction: Sepsis is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in neonatal intensive care units (NICU). Common causes of sepsis are well described in the NICU literature. Certain risk factors and exposures may warrant investigation for less common causes of sepsis.We present a case of early onset sepsis leading to septic shock and death caused by Flavobacterium lindanitolerans (F. lindanitolerans), an environmental gram-negative rod that typically does not cause human disease. Case Presentation: A male infant weighing 960 grams was delivered at home at 25 3/7 weeks’ gestation to a 38- year-old mother who received prenatal care. Prenatal labs and screening were normal. Birth occurred via spontaneous vaginal delivery 90 minutes prior to hospital arrival. The infant was born into toilet and the umbilical cord was tied with a shoestring and cut prior to hospital arrival. On admission, labs were notable for: white blood cell (WBC) count 6.3 and C-reactive protein (CRP) 0.03. Blood cultures were obtained, and penicillin and gentamicin were started. On day of life (DOL) 2, labs showed:WBC1.8 and CRP 1.24. On DOL 3, labs showed:WBC0.8 and CRP 2.24. On DOL 3, the blood culture grew gram-negative rods. Antibiotics were transitioned from gentamicin to ceftazidime due to acute kidney injury. On DOL 4, blood culture speciated F. lindanitolerans. Infectious disease was consulted and recommended adding ciprofloxacin. The hospital course was complicated by respiratory distress, thrombocytopenia, pulmonary hemorrhage and bilateral grade IV intraventricular hemorrhage. During admission, the infant required increasing respiratory support and status remained critical. On DOL 8, the infant was started on vasoactive medications and transitioned to high settings on oscillator ventilation. Despite the changes, the infant became increasingly bradycardic and hypotensive with worsening metabolic acidosis. The family elected to redirect care to comfort measures and withdraw life-sustaining measures. Discussion: This case report is the first documented case of F. lindanitolerans bacteremia and sepsis in a neonate at birth. F. lindanitolerans is commonly isolated in soil and water samples. Literature on this bacterium in humans is limited. One report highlights F. lindanitolerans causing bacteremia in a 76-year-old female. To our knowledge, no case reports exist of F. lindanitolerans causing sepsis in neonates. The infant was likely exposed to F. lindanitolerans during precipitous delivery with exposure to toilet bowl and shoestring. The infant was likely more susceptible to the infection due to prematurity. Once the blood culture grew F. lindanitolerans, we broadened antibiotic coverage to add ciprofloxacin. Ultimately, our patient remained in septic shock with multiple signs of end organ dysfunction, leading to clinical deterioration and death. Our case highlights the need for broadening empiric antibiotic coverage in newborns exposed to unconventional environments.
