N2  - Purpose of Study: Gastroschisis (GS) is a congenital abdominal wall defect affecting ∼4 in 10,000 babies. Care for GS patients is widely variable. In other patient populations, studies suggest that standardization of care improves patient outcomes. This quality improvement (QI) project aims to standardize multidisciplinary care of GS patients from birth to discharge to improve patient outcomes at Children's Memorial Hermann Hospital (CMHH). Methods Used: A retrospective chart review of all GS patients at CMHH from December 2008 to May 2020 was conducted to establish a baseline group. Outcomes tracked included antibiotic exposure, length of hospital stay (LOS), total parenteral nutrition (TPN) duration, time to full feeds, and average daily weight gain. Clinical guidelines on intravenous fluid (IV) use, pain management, antibiotic use, albumin level monitoring, and milk choice on enteral feed initiation were established and implemented in November 2020. Cycle 1 involved implementation of the developed guidelines. Cycle 2 involved placing printed guidelines at the newly admitted patient bedside and team reeducation. We compared outcomes of patients born 0–6 months and 6–18 months post guideline implementation, respectively. To evaluate sustainability, we continued team compliance to guidelines. After an initial analysis at 18 months post guideline implementation, Cycle 2 was continued for an additional year to evaluate compliance and neonatal outcomes. ANOVA/ Wilcoxon and Fisher's exact tests were used to compare continuous and categorical variables, respectively, and an F test was used to compare differences in variability between outcomes. Summary of Results: Baseline (n = 161), Cycle 1 (n = 9), and Cycle 2 (n = 26) patients were similar in baseline characteristics including sex, gestational age, and complexity of GS. Antibiotic exposurewas reduced in Cycles 1 and 2 compared to baseline (baseline: 97.5% vs cycle 1: 77.8% vs cycle 2: 61.5%; P < 0.001). Nutrition outcomes were similar amongst groups. There was no significant difference in rates of necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) or late onset sepsis between groups. Variability in weight gain (p < 0.001), TPN length (p = 0.008), length of admission (p = 0.007), and time to full feeds (p < 0.001) were decreased in Cycles 1 and 2. There was a significant difference in the use of antibiotics for more than 48 hours in the first week of life between Cycles 1 and 2 (33.3% vs 3.8%, p = 0.044). Audit of Cycle 1 and 2 showed that guideline compliancewas highest in choice of IV fluid, use of PRN acetaminophen for pain control, and use of expressed breast milk at enteral feed initiation. Conclusions: This QI project has resulted in decreased variability in the care provided to GS patients and improvement in outcomes with the implementation of clinical guidelines. There was a significant decrease in antibiotic usage with no increase in diagnosed infections. Future work will focus on sustainability of the implemented guidelines and decreasing opioid usage for postoperative pain management.
