N2  - Purpose of Study: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a life-threatening form of intestinal failure with a high risk of long-term morbidity. The etiology of NEC is multifactorial, including immaturity of the gastrointestinal tract, dysbiosis, dysregulated inflammation, and bowel ischemia. NEC affects premature infants 4 to 6 weeks after birth. Interestingly, classic findings of NEC are described in infants <14 postnatal days (PND). However, little is known if early NEC cases are similar to later, more traditionally diagnosed NEC.We aim to better characterize phenotypic differences in baseline characteristics, maternal and prenatal history, neonatal morbidities, growth, and feeding outcomes between infants diagnosed with Early NEC (≤14 PND). Methods Used: This prospective cohort study used the Vermont Oxford Network Database of inborn infants from a level IV neonatal intensive care unit diagnosed with NEC from July 2022–July 2023. All infants admitted to the NICU who received any ampicillin 72 hours after birthwere included. Groups were divided by ≤14 PND. Continuous variables were compared with Kruskal-Wallis, and categorical variables were compared with Fischer Exact or Chi squared tests. Summary of Results: From 2022–2023 at our center, n = 119 infants were diagnosed with NEC. Infants were divided by ≤14 PND (n = 56, Early NEC) and >14 PND (n = 63, Late NEC). Infants diagnosed with Early NEC in comparison to Late NEC infants were 580 g larger at birth (1817 vs 1237 g, p < 0.001), had a larger head circumference (29.1 vs 25.4 cm, p < 0.001), had higher gestational age a birth (31.7 vs 28.8 weeks, p = 0.001), and had 20% more males (71% vs 51%, p = 0.038). Early NEC in comparison to Late NEC infants weighed >600 g less at discharge (3007 vs 3619 g, p = 0.106), and had >50% shorter length of stay (44 vs 107 days, p < 0.001). Early NEC infants were prescribed 32% less inotropes (9% vs 41%, p < 0.001) and dopamine (5% vs 37%, p < 0.001) compared to Late NEC infants. Early NEC infants were 50% less likely to have oxygen at 36 weeks (34% vs 59%, p = 0.006) and had 12 fewer days of antibiotic exposure prior to NEC diagnosis (2 vs 14 days, p < 0.001). Early NEC infants were almost half as likely to receive transfusion of blood products within 5 days of a NEC diagnosis (20% vs 38%, p = 0.041). Early NEC infants had less negative weight (p = 0.035, p = 0.008), length (p = 0.002, p = 0.001) and head circumference (p = 0.22, p = 0.046) z scores at birth and discharge, respectively. Early NEC compared to Late NEC infants had 50% less feeding delays >24 hours (26% vs 63%, p < 0.001). Conclusions: Phenotypic differences exist between infants diagnosed with NEC at different time points. This descriptive review of baseline demographics and clinical outcomes illustrate that infants who develop NEC ≤14 PND are different from those who develop it later in hospitalization. Notable differences, such as less exposure to inotropes, specifically dopamine, warrant further study to better define NEC and its associated risk factors for diagnosis within the first 14 days after birth. $T$
