N2  - Introduction: Neonatal sepsis is defined as invasive bacterial infection occurring in first 4 weeks of life. Neonatal sepsis causes high morbidity and mortality during neonatal period, and is a thrust area for research in neonatal medicine. During neonatal period excess use of antibiotic for neonatal sepsis leading to antibiotic resistance. CRP level may help in early diagnosis and management of neonatal sepsis, initiation and adjustment of antibiotic therapy. The aim of this study was to evaluate the role of CRP in deciding duration of antibiotic treatment in neonatal sepsis. Methods: A prospective study conducted in ninety neonates admitted with suspected neonatal sepsis admitted in pediatrics SNCU of MLB Medical College Jhansi. CRP was estimated within 24–48 hours of admission. hen neonates were assigned into 3 groups according to CRP levels. Infection unlikely group, infection likely group with two subgroups-CRP guided therapy and 7 days antibiotic therapy. Results: Out of total 100 cases males were higher incidence of neonatal sepsis and more common in preterm babies (<37wks) and also commonly occurred in babies below 2500gm of birth weight. The sensitivity of serum CRP was 100% against blood culture while specificity at day 2 was 71.7% and it increases with time. The positive predictive value was also increases with increase in time. Conclusion: Serum CRP is a reliable test in establishing the diagnosis of sepsis in neonates and it accurately monitors the duration of antibiotic therapy in these neonates and results in significant reduction in the treatment duration of neonatal sepsis.
