N2  - Background: Group A Streptococcus (GAS) infection in newborns can rarely lead to invasive diseases such as Streptococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (STSS), a potentially life-threatening condition. Due to its low incidence and non-specific symptoms, a high index of suspicion is required for early diagnosis. Portal vein thrombosis (PVT) is another rare condition in neonates, more common in preterm infants who have undergone umbilical catheterization. Case Presentation Summary: We present the case of a term male newborn with no relevant medical history who was admitted to the emergency room on the ninth day of life with persistent grunting and feeding difficulties. Both parents exhibited symptoms of pharyngitis. On physical (Figure present) examination, the infant was hypotonic and lethargic, with jaundice, prolonged capillary refill time, and weak peripheral pulses. Due to hemodynamic instability, mechanical ventilation and inotropic support were initiated. Empirical antibiotic therapy with intravenous (IV) ampicillin, gentamicin, and cefotaxime was started. Laboratory tests revealed elevated C-reactive protein and procalcitonin levels, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, hyponatremia, hepatic cytolysis, and cholestasis. A blood culture confirmed the presence of GAS, leading to the diagnosis of STSS. During the hospitalization, the newborn developed significant abdominal distension. An abdominal ultrasound revealed ascites and a heterogenous hepatic lobe. Suspecting hepatic hemorrhage, the neonate was transferred to a tertiary neonatal intensive care unit for pediatric surgery evaluation. At admission, a color Doppler scan was performed, showing left hepatic vein thrombosis. Enoxaparin therapy was initiated, and, due to the severity of this case, IV immunoglobulin and clindamycin were added to the ongoing treatment. The newborn completed a 28-day course of antibiotics, showing both clinical and laboratory improvement. The inotropic support was discontinued on the fifth day of treatment, and the infant was extubated on the sixth day. At discharge, the left branch of the portal vein was already recanalized. Learning Points Discussion: GSA infection in the neonatal period is rare and may result from vertical transmission or contact with infected individuals. Cases of neonatal STSS have been reported in the literature, some resulting in death. Neonatologists should be aware of this condition, as prompt treatment can significantly reduce morbidity and mortality. Although rare, complications such as thrombotic events can occur and must be actively investigated and managed. This case underscores the importance of continuous monitoring and intensive supportive care for neonates with STSS.
