N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters 42 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: This case highlights the importance of maintaining a broad differential and utilizing a combination of diagnostic modalities for diagnosis in a patient with acute change in mental status. Furthermore, it highlights the challenges associated with diagnosis of CNS listeriosis due to its common lack of clinical examination and laboratory findings. CASE PRESENTATION: We present a case of a 77 year old male with a past medical history of COPD, Diabetes mellitus II. On presentation he was febrile, tachypneic, tachycardic, nauseous, and B symptoms. Zosyn/Vancomycin were initiated empirically. Following 48 hours of empiric antibiotics, the patient remained febrile with no interval improvement, he required admission to the ICU, and–although the patient did not exhibit meningeal signs on clinical exam, due to his continued fever–a lumbar puncture was performed with CSF sampling. CSF fluid studies resulted in leukocytosis, antimicrobial coverage was broadened to vancomycin/ceftriaxone/ampicillin/acyclovir for undifferentiated meningitis. The patient continued to deteriorate and was transferred to another facility for higher level of care. The CSF PCR panel returned positive for Listeria and CSF culture grew Listeria monocytogenes. Antibiotic treatment was subsequently narrowed to IV Ampicillin and IV Gentamicin. With 48 hours of targeted antibiotic therapy the patient continued to clinically deteriorate–most notably his mental status–requiring initiation of mechanical ventilation. An EEG was performed showing moderate generalized slowing, but no electrographic seizures or nonconvulsive status epilepticus. A CT-Head W/O was obtained revealing new onset hydrocephalus, therefore he was given mannitol, hypertonic saline, and protamine, then transferred to a facility with neurosurgical capabilities for EVD placement. IV Ampicillin, IV Gentamicin, and hypertonic saline were continued postoperatively and keppra was initiated for seizure prophylaxis. DISCUSSION: Listeria monocytogenes, most notable for causing infections in neonates, elderly, and the immunocompromised. Listeria has the 3rd highest rate of mortality among foodborne infections1. It is a rare infection accounting for 0.1% of all foodborne infections; of the 0.1%, 20-25% are CNS listeriosis. It is responsible for 2% of bacterial meningitis cases in the United States and 20% of cases in neonates and adults above 50 years old3. Of the cases of CNS listeriosis, meningitis and meningoencephalitis are the most common manifestations. In this small cohort of individuals meningism is present in 43%, CSF findings predictive for infection are absent in 23% of patients, and CSF gram stain was only found to yield the organism in 24% of patients. In bacterial meningitis, hydrocephalus is a major complication that occurs in roughly 5% of adult patients with non-tuberculosis/fungal meningitis. CNS listeriosis is the second leading cause of hydrocephalus in community-acquired meningitis at up to 14% and is noted to be an independent risk factor for increased mortality. CONCLUSIONS: Patient was extubated to NIV 4 days following EVD placement. Following extubation, CSF fluid studies were non-acute, gentamicin and keppra were discontinued. A repeat CT-Head W/O following EVD removal showed a maintained reduction in size of the 3rd ventricle. Patient was discharged to a facility for continued physical therapy and continued on Ampicillin for a total course of 4 weeks. REFERENCE #1: Scallan, Elaine, et al. "Foodborne Illness Acquired in the United States—Major Pathogens." Emerging Infectious Diseases, vol. 17, no. 1, 2011, pp. 7–15., https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1701.p11101. Accessed 18 Mar. 2023. REFERENCE #2: Disson, Olivier, and Marc Lecuit. "Targeting of the Central Nervous System byListeria Monocytogenes." Virulence, vol. 3, no. 2, 2012, pp. 213–221., https://doi.org/10.4161/viru.19586. Accessed 18 Mar. 2023. REFERENCE #3: Winn, H. Richard, and Julian R. Youmans. "Meningitis and Encephalitis." Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery, 8th ed., Elsevier, Philadelphia, PA, 2017, https://www.clinicalkey.com/#!/content/book/3-s2.0-B9780323661928000574. Accessed 18 Mar. 2023. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Priyanka Jain No relevant relationships by Christopher Moore
