N2  - Background: Prolonged antibiotic use in preterm infants increases the risk of functional gastrointestinal (GI) disorders (FGIDs) and feeding intolerance. The relationship between duration of antibiotic exposure and GI motility in preterm infants has not been delineated. A gastric dysrhythmia of 4-9 cycles per minute (tachygastria) is associated with gastric hypomotility and FGIDs in older children. Tachygastria may be higher in infants receiving long vs. short courses of antibiotics, suggesting gastric dysmotility. Objective: We aim to compare weekly changes in percent tachygastria between preterm infants receiving long (>48hrs) vs. short (≤48hrs) courses of antibiotics for early onset sepsis evaluation. Methods: We conducted a secondary analysis of a longitudinal, prospective cohort study of 88 preterm infants (< 34 weeks' gestation) who underwent weekly electrogastrography (EGG) recordings, a non-invasive method to measure gastric motility, from the first week of life until 40 weeks' post-menstrual age, discharge, or death. We calculated the percentage of EGG recording time in tachygastria (% tachygastria) and determined the mean ± standard error (SE) across weekly EGG sessions. A mixed effects model was used to assess variance in % tachygastria between the short and long antibiotic exposure groups across all weeks. After confirming normal distribution, a student's t-test was performed to determine differences in % tachygastria between groups at weeks 2 and 4, which are integral developmental periods of GI motility in neonates. Results: Every preterm infant in the NICU was exposed to antibiotics <72 hours after birth for early onset sepsis evaluation. Gestational age and weight were the same between short vs. long antibiotic exposure groups. Length of hospital stay was higher for infants in the long antibiotic exposure group (p=0.04), but rates of sepsis were the same as the short antibiotic exposure group (Table 1). Using the mixed effects model, there was no difference in % tachygastria between short and long antibiotic exposure groups across 9 postnatal weeks (p = 0.079). Additionally, there was no difference in % tachygastria between groups at weeks 2 and 4 (Fig 1&2). Conclusions: This study suggests that prolonged, early antibiotic exposure among preterm infants does not lead to significant changes in % tachygastria. Although prior studies suggest judicious use of antibiotics improves time to reach full enteral feeds and reduces the risk of developing FGIDs, early prolonged antibiotics may not affect GI motility. Further research is needed to delineate the mechanism behind these associations.
