N2  - Objectives Culture proven Early Onset Neonatal Sepsis (EONS) is rare, however, it is associated with a high morbidity and mortality. Using NICE guidelines for the management of EONS demonstrated antibiotic treatment being given in 16% of infants on the postnatal ward. Current NICE guidelines approve the use of Kaiser Permanente Sepsis Risk Calculator (KPSRC) as a screening tool in neonates >34 gestational age with risk factors for EONS. Its application has shown a reduction in antibiotic usage with no missed cases of EONS. Since its implementation at our local district general hospital, we audited the use of the KPSRC in neonates >34 gestational age with risk factors for EONS. Methods Neonates >34 gestational age with risk factors for EONS (as per NICE Guidelines), were identified using patient lists. Those who had KPSRC tool applied were further analysed. Incidence of EONS used was 1 in 1,000 live births. Data was collected retrospectively through patient electronic records. Results Over 6 months, a total of 227 patients were identified of which, 55 patients had KPSRC tool used (24%). Incomplete documentation noted in 10 patients. In the remaining 45, 31 were advised for 'no culture, no antibiotics' but 5 were subsequently screened due to tachypnoea. A total of 26 babies avoided antibiotics. One baby re-presented with poor feeding and hypothermia within 7 days of life, they were screened and had a negative blood culture. Number of babies who were incorrectly identified by the calculator who do not develop a culture-confirmed neonatal infection was 14. There were no cases of babies missed by the calculator. Conclusions In total 14 patients were incorrectly identified who did not develop a culture confirmed infection. 26 patients avoided antibiotics and no babies were missed by the calculator. Interestingly, the use of KPSRC tool was noted to be infrequent. This was improved through regular training sessions, posters and Kaiser champions. Junior doctors were named as Kaiser Champions to spread awareness about the tool. We propose to have Kaiser Champions amongst both neonatal and midwifery teams, include Kaiser training within induction and continue regular refreshers to ensure staff is appropriately informed and supported in using the tool. We plan to conduct a prospective audit following these changes, with additional data collection on documentation, observations, discharge check and provision of patient leaflets. We believe that KPSRC tool can safely improve antibiotic stewardship and reduce number of bed days.
