N2  - Objectives and Study: Over 90% of very preterm-born neonates are subjected to neonatal antibiotic exposure (NABE). Early antibiotic exposure might affect immune system development by, i.e. altering gut microbiota colonization. The effects of antibiotic exposure on long-term health outcome needs to be elucidated. We aimed to investigate the association between the duration of NABE and childhood health outcome after preterm birth, including respiratory, gastrointestinal, growth, and neurocognitive outcome. Methods: This cross-sectional study was embedded in an ongoing multicenter preterm (<30 weeks gestational age (GA)) cohort study on early biomarkers for neonatal disease in the Netherlands and Belgium. Health and lifestyle questionnaires were sent to the parents of children who were >2 years old. Three groups of subjects were defined, based on duration of NABE: (1) <5days NABE; (2) ≥5days NABE without acute neonatal diseases; (3) ≥5days NABE with neonatal necrotizing enterocolitis, sepsis or meningitis. We analyzed the association of NABE with parents' reported childhood constipation (ROME IV criteria and laxative use), obstructive respiratory disease (bronchodilation use) and patient-record data on growth and neurodevelopment (Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development-(BSID)-III) at 24 months' corrected age. Results: Of 1185 approached parents/care-givers, 29% completed the questionnaire at their child's median age of 55 months. Gestational age (GA) was significantly higher in the first (<5 days NABE) group, while birth weight (BW) was lowest in the second (≥5days NABE without acute neonatal diseases). Growth and median BSID-III-scores were within normal range in all groups. Constipation and bronchodilation use did not differ between the 3 NABE subgroups (Table 1), even when corrected for GA, BW Z-score and center of birth. Conclusions: Despite differences in GA and BW, we did not find associations between duration of NABE and health outcome in early childhood. Future studies are needed to investigate whether NABE after preterm birth affects health in the longer term. (Table Presented).
