N2  - Late onset Neonatal sepsis (LONS) is a leading cause of mortality in preterm infants, affecting up to 15% of preterm neonates and a mortality rate of ~15%. Clinical symptoms of LONS are nonspecific and diagnosis is therefore often established in an advanced stage of infection, illustrating the need for novel preclinical biomarkers. This study aimed to search for predictive biomarkers for LONS development in preterm infants using fecal metagenomics and targeted metabolomics. Methods: In a Dutch multicentric study, fecal samples were collected daily from birth to S. aureus and E. coli LONS development in preterm neonates and matched to healthy controls by gestational and postnatal age, antibiotic exposure and birth center (n= 40). 16S amplicon sequencing, shotgun metagenomics and quantitative tryptophan (Trp) metabolomics was performed in fecal samples up to 7 days prior to and 5 days after LONS diagnosis. Functional differences were evaluated by pan-genome analysis and targeted analysis of Trp metabolites. The predictive value of 16S amplicon sequences was assessed by classification modeling based on an extreme gradient boosting algorithm. In vitro aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR) activity was measured by reporter assay. Results: Relative abundance of fecal E. coli was significantly higher pre-onset in E. coli LONS, compared to matched controls (t- 5/-4 days, p=0.017). In contrast, no significant difference in relative abundance of fecal S. aureus was detected in S. aureus LONS patients, however higher abundance of E. cloacae, K. pneumonia and E. faecalis was observed. This suggests a gut origin of E. coli LONS, compared to S. aureus LONS which may have a more elusive relation to the gut. Fecal Trp metabolites detected included kynurenine, serotonin and Indole-lactic-acid in controla and patients diagnosed with either E.coli and S. aureus LONS. Indole-x was significantly elevated 5 days pre-diagnosis of E. coli LONS compared to controls, indicating potential as a novel biomarker (p=0.04). Indole-x also ~3-fold activated AhR in HT29 epithelial cell reporter assay over control at fecal concentrations prior to E. coli LONS. In support, metagenomics analysis indicated negative association of genes related to microbial Trp biosynthesis in the E. coli LONS metagenome 4-5 days pre-diagnosis, suggesting higher dependence on exogenous Trp. This multi-omics approach indicates, in line with previous findings, a strong increase in relative abundance of E. coli prior to LONS onset, and a functional difference mediated by Trp metabolism and indole-x. Our data demonstrates that E. coli affects hostmicrobe interaction in the underdeveloped neonatal gut prior to onset of LONS. Ongoing research focuses on elucidating fecal microbiota-host interaction to better understand neonatal GI disease.
