N2  - Background: Ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP) is a common occurrence in neonates resulting in increased mortality, morbidity, hospital stay. Diagnostic criteria and specific guidelines for management of neonatal VAP are limited resulting in excessive antibiotic use. This study aimed to investigate if mechanically ventilated neonates who had antibiotics administered for treatment of VAP were correctly diagnosed and whether antibiotic treatment improved their condition. Methods: A chart audit was conducted of a large tertiary hospital database from 1 January 2016 to 1 May 2020. Records of all neonates who were mechanically ventilated for at least 48 h and prescribed antibiotics for a lower respiratory tract infection were searched to obtain clinical information. Collected data were compared to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) criteria to determine if VAP was correctly diagnosed. Results: Of the 40 neonates who met inclusion criteria, 23 (58%) fulfilled the CDC criteria for VAP. There was no significant difference between neonates who met VAP criteria and those who did not for gestational age ( P = 0.24), chronological age ( P = 0.61), birth weight ( P = 0.23), ventilation duration ( P = 0.75), or antibiotic course duration ( P = 0.41). After antibiotic administration, ventilation improved in 29 neonates (76.3%), with no significant difference between those correctly diagnosed and those incorrectly diagnosed with VAP. Conclusions: VAP was correctly identified in just over half of the neonates, suggesting that correct diagnosis is difficult to achieve. Correct diagnosis is necessary for health professionals to avoid incorrect or excessive use of antibiotics and minimise the negative effects of antibiotic use in neonates.
