N2  - Purpose of Study: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a life-threatening form of intestinal failure with a high risk of long-term morbidity. The etiology of NEC is multifactorial, including immaturity of the gastrointestinal tract, dysbiosis, dysregulated inflammation, and bowel ischemia. NEC typically affects premature infants 4 to 6 weeks after birth. Interestingly, classic findings of NEC are described in infants less than two weeks old and some =7 days of life (DOL). However, little is known if these early NEC cases are similar to traditional NEC. We aimed to measure phenotypic differences in baseline characteristics, maternal and prenatal history, neonatal morbidities, growth, and feeding outcomes between infants diagnosed with NEC at 14 and 7 DOL. Methods Used: This retrospective cohort study used the Vermont Oxford Network (VON) Database of inborn infants from a level IV neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) diagnosed with NEC from January 2012-May 2022. Groups were primarily divided by =14 DOL and secondarily by =7 DOL. 140 variables were evaluated. Continuous variables were compared with the Wilcoxon Rank Sum test, and categorical variables were compared with Fisher's exact test. Summary of Results: Of 13 020 infants inborn between 2012 and 2022, 105 infants were classified as developing NEC during NICU admission. Infants were divided by =14 DOL (n = 46, Early NEC) and >14 DOL (n = 59, Late NEC). Early NEC infants had higher birth weight (p < 0.001), greater head circumference (p < 0.001), were three weeks older in gestational age (p < 0.001) and had 4-fold higher rates of maternal diabetes (p = 0.069). Early NEC infants received >60% less pressor use (p = 0.004), 50% less dopamine use (0.007) and had 5 fewer days of antibiotic exposure (p < 0.001) with 90% less exposure to cephalosporins (p < 0.001). Early NEC infants had less negative weight (p = 0.015, p = 0.002), length (p = 0.072, p < 0.001) and head circumference (p = 0.128, p = 0.001) z scores at birth and discharge, respectively. Early NEC infants had 30% fewer feeding delays >24 hours (p = 0.012), were fortified three days earlier (p < 0.001), had fewer days to first NEC diagnosis (p < 0.001) but had no difference in corrected gestational age at first NEC diagnosis (p = 0.869). Early NEC infants had 3.5 times more Stage 3B at diagnosis (p = 0.013). Early NEC infants had 45% less exposure to enteral caffeine at the time of NEC diagnosis (p = 0.003). Further analysis of patients who developed NEC at =7 DOL showed no difference from those who developed NEC > 7 DOL. Conclusions: Phenotypic differences exist between infants diagnosed with early compared with late NEC. The review of our data show that patients who develop NEC at =14 DOL are different from those who develop it later in their hospitalization. Some of these differences are less exposure to known risk factors for NEC, such as antibiotics. Most intriguing was the higher proportion of infants born to mothers with diabetes. Further studies are warranted to define early NEC and its associated risk factors.
