N2  - Purpose of Study: Early-onset sepsis (EOS) refers to sepsis that occurs within 72 hours of birth. Late-onset sepsis (LOS) occurs after 72 hours of birth. Neonatal sepsis may be difficult to recognize due to its overlapping clinical presentations with other conditions common in very low birth weight (VLBW) neonates (=1500 g), thus leading to a lack of uniform guidelines in diagnosis and treatment. Empiric antibiotics (abx) are prescribed for most VLBW babies with consideration of culture negative sepsis. Antibiotic stewardship is essential to address within the NICU because of antibiotic resistance and short- and long-term consequences of antibiotic use in neonates on health outcomes in adulthood. We studied the characteristics of VLBW babies receiving prolonged antibiotics and its impact on neonatal morbidity. Methods Used: A retrospective study from January 2021 to June 2022was conducted for 108 VLBW neonates. The number of culture positive and culture negative events were investigated for both early onset sepsis and late onset sepsis. The demographics and outcomes of VLBW neonates who were culture negative for EOS and received abx for <72 hours were compared with those who received abx for at least 72 hours. Summary of Results: Of the 108 VLBW neonates receiving empiric abx on admission for EOS, 2 were culture positive. Of the 106 VLBW neonates who were culture negative, 24 (22%) had abx for =72 hours; they tended to be smaller in weight (894 vs 1061 g, p = 0.02), they had a higher mean CRP (5.83 vs 2.49 mg/dl, p = 0.002), a greater percent had a CRP > 1 (p = 0.000018), they had a higher mean WBC count and I:T ratio (p = 0.00117 and p = 0.01), a higher need for HFO/Jet within the first 3 days of life (p = 0.008), and the mean number of days of antibiotics given was significantly greater than in the other group (5 vs 1.9d). Other clinical outcomes are in the table below. [Table presented] Conclusions: In our cohort, smaller and critical babies seemed to get longer abx for culture negative EOS. They also tended to have longer hospital stays and a higher chance of death or BPD prior to discharge. Further data collection and analysis is needed to ensure these associations are true and antibiotic stewardship should be focused on this most vulnerable babies.
