N2  - Elizabethkingia meningosepticum is ubiquitous in nature, though not a normal human flora but it is an emerging pathogen amongst immunocompromised patients. E. meningosepticum can cause a variety of infections and septicaemia and meningitis have been reported amongst immunocompromised. It is difficult to treat with a high mortality rate. This is likely due to its resistance to commonly used antibiotics for Gram-negative infections. In this report, we present a case of a neonate who was admitted for perinatal hypoxia to the neonatal intensive care unit whose blood and cerebrospinal fluid culture sample grew E. meningosepticum. The patient was initially treated with empiric antibiotics for Gram-negative infections and later linezolid. Considering the organism’s unusual susceptibility, profile close surveillance and rapid identification are needed to assist in appropriate and effective treatment.
