N2  - BACKGROUND-AIM Perinatal sepsis constitutes a medical emergency and is still one of the major causes of mortality and morbidity. Meconium aspiration syndrome occurs when a newborn breathes a mixture of meconium and amniotic fluid into the lungs around the time of delivery. Meconium aspiration syndrome, a leading cause of severe illness, asphyxia and death in the newborn, occurs in about 5 - 10 % of births. A 36-year-old pregnant woman underwent an urgent caesarean section due to premature rupture of membranes and risk of loss of fetal well-being. The newborn presented neonatal depression: bluish skin colour, bradycardia (50bpm), limpness, respiratory distress and breathing problems. METHODS Due to poor clinical appearance, the newborn was translated to neonatal Intensive Care Unit and empirical antibiotic therapy with ampicillin and cefotaxime 300mg/kg/day was started. Mechanical ventilation by CPAP was initiated. Blood, CSF and respiratory mucosa samples were collected for microbiological and biochemical analysis. RESULTS Blood analysis presented glucose 73 mg/dL, urea 19 mg/dL, total bilirubin 2.9 mg/dL, lactate dehydrogenase 2321 U/L, ALT 193 U/L, AST 88 U/L, CRP < 6.9 mg/L and 19780 leukocytes per μL. Gasometry showed: pH 7.19, pCO2 36.3 mmHg, PO2 45.1 mmHg and bicarbonate 14.4 mmol/L. Analysis of CSF was carried out and found 51mg/dL, 98.4 mg/dL and 44 leukocytes per μL (abundant red blood cells, 43% lymphomononuclear cells and 57% polymorphonuclear cells). It was worth noting the high degree of activation and vacuolization of monocytes and neutrophils. Microbiological and genetic analysis showed no sign of bacterial or viral infection, except for the detection of Gardnerella vaginalis, a common finding during premature rupture of membranes. CONCLUSIONS Neonatal asphyxia is one of the main diagnoses in neonatal intensive care units, defined as a set of symptoms or clinical and analytical signs within the first 4 weeks of life. It was produced in this occasion by the aspiration of meconium, which together with the infection by Gardnerella vaginalis, could have caused hyperactivation of immune system cells both in blood and CSF. Eventually, increase in LDH would correspond to the situation caused by asphyxia.
