N2  - Purpose of Study: Ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP) is a common hospital acquired infection in neonates on prolonged mechanical ventilation resulting in high morbidity and mortality. Incidence of VAP varies greatly among different institutions and geographic regions ranging from 2.7 to 10.9 episodes per 1,000 ventilator days in developed countries. Variation of incidence may be due to the lack of consensus in diagnostic criteria for VAP in neonates. Furthermore, diagnosing neonatal VAP can be challenging as other disease processes may have similar presentations and radiographic identification of pneumonia can be difficult due to shifting atelectasis and evolving bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) changes. This often led to frequent antibiotics use to treat positive tracheal aspirate culture in neonates with changes in respiratory status. The primary objective of this study is to determine the incidence, risk factors, and antibiotic use of VAP in VLBW infants. Methods Used: Retrospective study of all intubated VLBW infants admitted to neonatal intensive care unit who had routine respiratory cultures obtained from July 2015 to July 2021.Neonatal and maternal demographics, clinical course, antibiotic use, radiologic reports and laboratory results including microbiology and hematology were collected. Infants were grouped into no VAP, suspect VAP and confirmed VAP. VAP was defined according to modified CDC criteria for infants less than 1 year old. Suspected VAP was determined if neonate received antibiotic treatment for positive tracheal aspirate culture but did not meet VAP criteria. No VAP was determined when neonates did not receive antibiotic treatment for positive tracheal aspirate cultures. Summary of Results: A total of 87 VLBW intubated infants were included in the study. The incidence of confirmed VAP was 7.1 per 1,000 ventilator days. There was no significant difference in birth weight, gestational age, gender, mode of delivery, rate of prolonged rupture of membranes, maternal GBS colonization and intrauterine inflammation and infection between the three groups. Infants who had suspected VAP and confirmed VAP had a higher rate of maternal receipt of antenatal steroids, surfactant administration, longer days of intubation and length of stay (Table). Infants with suspected VAP were exposed to antibiotics equally (median of 10 days) compared to confirmed VAP. There were 27/87 (31%) infants who did not meet the diagnostic criteria for VAP but had tracheal colonization and exposed to antibiotics. Conclusions: VAP is associated with prolonged use of invasive mechanical ventilation in VLBW infants. Obtaining routine tracheal aspirate culture can increase antibiotic exposure in these infants. The challenge and inconsistency in the diagnosis of VAP in neonates can also lead to unnecessary use of antibiotics. Attempts to extubate early and use of non-invasive ventilation should be discussed daily in the management of these critically ill infants.
