N2  - Introduction. Subdural empyema is a servious complication of bacterial meningiits, a therapeutic challenge to clinicians and associated with long-term neurological sequelae. Objectives. To evaluate the clincial features, therapeutic strategies, and outcome of subdural empyema complicating acute bacterial meningitis in neonates. Methods. Case series from a retrospective cohort study conducted in two medical centers in Taiwan between 2003 to 2020. Results. During the study period, a total of 153 neonates with bacterial meningitis were identified and enrolled. Among them, a total of 24 cases with subdural empyema or abscess were identified. Streptococcus agalactiae (Group B Streptococcus, GBS) and E. coli accounted for 37.5% (n = 9) and 29.2% (n = 7) of all neonatal bacterial meningitis with subdural empyema, respectively. Neonates with subdural empyema usually had more severe clincial manifestations than those with acute bacterial meningitis without subdural empyema, including more likely to have consciousness changes, focal seizures, and paresis. The cerebrospinal fluids findings cannot be predictive for occurrence of subdural empyema. Surgical intervention was performed for all cases with subdural empyema, and longer therapeutic duration of antibiotics was also noted. A higher percentage (70.8%, n = 17) of them had neurological sequelae at discharge, although the final mortality rate was comparable to those without subdural empyema. Conclusions. Subdural empyema and abscess is not uncommon in neonates with acute bacterial meningitis. Both GBS and E coli are important pathogens to cause subdural empyema in neonates with acute bacterial meningitis. Neurosurgical intervention with longer broad-spectrum antibiotics are required to optimize the outcomes. Long-term follow-up is indicated for high risk of neurological sequelae.
