N2  - Case Detail: Introduction: Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is a hereditary primary immunodeficiency disorder characterised by defect of NADPH enzyme leading to recurrent infections, mostly with catalase positive organisms, with or without hyperinflammation. The defective Reactive Oxygen Species leads to improper phagolysosomal lysis which leads to granuloma formation. It is mostly a X linked disorder (70% of cases) with a minority of autosomal recessive inheritance patterns. The median age of presentation is 3 years but diagnoses have been made in adolescents and adults also. It is imperative to identify this condition as the therapeutic modalities to manage infectious diseases may change. Here we present a case series of two patients who were diagnosed as CGD based on high clinical suspicion. Case 1: A 51-year-old diabetic female with reasonable glycemic control presented with complaints of pain in abdomen and fever for ten days. She was diagnosed to have multiple liver abscesses on clinical evaluation and imaging. Significantly, amoebic serology was negative and she required a prolonged course of broad spectrum antibiotics. There was no family history suggestive of recurrent infections or early death of a child. Other secondary causes of immunodeficiency were ruled out. Primary immunodeficiency workup was sent and the patient was diagnosed to be having CGD on the basis of Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) test. Whole Exome sequence (WES) was not done due to unaffordability. The screening of the family has been planned. Case 2: A 24-year-old female presented with a complaint of fever for the last 2 weeks with a palpable spleen. Past history was significant with recurrent fevers and disseminated tuberculosis (pulmonary and central nervous system). Patient was started on anti-tubercular therapy (ATT) and while on treatment for 6 months, she developed microbiologically confirmed rifampicin sensitive tubercular lymphadenitis. This led to prolongation of ATT till complete clinical response. She was managed empirically with antimalarials and cephalosporins for her present illness. Subsequently, serology for enteric fever was found to be positive. Fever defervescence was prolonged and she remained on a prolonged course of antibiotics. There was no history of prolonged antibiotic use or any premature deaths in family members. Secondary causes of immunodeficiency were ruled out and a workup for primary immunodeficiency was sent. The DHR test was found to be positive which suggested a diagnosis of CGD. WES was not done due to affordability issues. The screening of the family has been planned. Conclusions: The present two cases highlight the importance of remembering warning signs of primary immunodeficiency. A high index of suspicion needs to be maintained to diagnose these conditions so as to offer most appropriate life saving therapeutic interventions.
