N2  - BACKGROUND AND AIM: Sepsis is presumed to account for 15% of neonatal deaths, with incidence highest in lowincome countries; however diagnostic blood cultures are often unavailable in areas of highest burden, limiting precision of the exact incidence of sepsis in neonates. This study aimed to determine the incidence of bacterial sepsis in a population of young infants admitted to a main regional centre in Lilongwe, Malawi. METHOD: Retrospective review of cohort data from all admissions to a regional neonatal unit in Lilongwe, Malawi between June 1, 2018 and Aug 31, 2019 with a nested prospective cohort sample of neonates with suspected sepsis who underwent additional microbiological testing (complete blood count and blood culture in all, plus urine/ cerebrospinal cultures if necessary). RESULTS: There were 2997 admissions over 14 months; 74% at <1 day of life and 544 (18%) with suspected sepsis. Of those, 240 had microbiological assessment (median age 3 days). Neonates with suspected sepsis were generally more mature, bigger, and had lower mortality (12% versus 24%). Forty-seven (19.6%) had a positive blood culture, with approximately half of isolates representing known neonatal pathogens, and 55% showing antibiotic resistance. All received antibiotics (median 6 days). CONCLUSIONS: Even after considering potential contaminants, the incidence of bacterial sepsis was relatively high in this tertiary care neonatal centre population. However, a majority of neonates received prolonged antibiotics without definitive evidence of bacterial sepsis. This supports wider access to bacterial cultures and the need for antibiotic stewardship to reduce the unnecessary use of antibiotics. In those cases, clinicians should consider alternate diagnoses.
