N2  - SESSION TITLE: Uncommon Presentations and Complications of Chest Infections SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/18/2022 10:15 am - 11:10 am INTRODUCTION: Pleural effusion is frequently found in hospitalized patient and the underlying etiology can be predictive of morbidity and mortality. Most common causes include heart failure, infection, pulmonary embolism and cancer. Thoracentesis is important in diagnosing the etiology. Infections can be common and not easily isolated in cultures. We present a patient with Bacillus cereus bacteremia associated with B. cereus empyema. B. cereus food poisoning are commonly reported, but true bacteremia is difficult to identify, in which most of the time samples are regarded as contaminants. CASE PRESENTATION: A 71 y/o M with Hx of HTN, HLD, polysubstance use disorder (on methadone), PICC, supraglottic squamous cell carcinoma (T3N3bM0) s/p laryngectomy, lung adenocarcinoma (T3N1) s/p VATS resection of R middle lobe with later recurrent adenocarcinoma s/p paclitaxel, carboplatin, and radiotherapy with further adjuvant cisplatin treatment, comes to ED complaining of SOB by week. He had decreased breath sound in R-lung, was tachycardic, and tachypneic. Afebrile with labs unremarkable. CT chest angiogram: large R-sided pleural effusion, near complete collapse of R-lung, negative for PE. Pleural fluid cultures and blood culture resulted positive for B. cereus resistant to penicillin and erythromycin. He received a fourteen-day antibiotic course with Vancomycin and chest tube for drainage. He improved immediately with re-expansion of R-lung and further blood cultures were negative. Chest tube was removed with plans for Pleurex catheter placement vs pleurodesis. DISCUSSION: Bacillus cereus are catalase positive aerobic or facultative anaerobic gram-positive bacilli, often associated with food poisoning, but also with more serious conditions such as catheter-related bloodstream infections, fulminant sepsis, meningitis and progressive pneumonia, particularly in immunocompromised patients, neonates and IV drug users. Our patient had B. cereus empyema and bacteriemia with lung adenocarcinoma and malignant pleural effusion. Two cases were reported with B. cereus empyema related with underlying hematologic malignancy. Whether malignant pleural effusion increases the probability of B. cereus empyema remains unknown. CONCLUSIONS: Empyema remains a rare complication of B. cereus infection. Early empirical antibiotic therapy with Vancomycin remains the cornerstone of therapy. Therefore, based on the potential of B. cereus to cause serious, life-treating infections, physicians should not regard bacteria as merely a contaminant and empiric antibiotics regimen should not be delayed. Reference #1: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/labs/pmc/articles/PMC6647819/ Aygun, F. D., Aygun, F., & Cam, H. (2016). Successful Treatment of Bacillus cereus Bacteremia in a Patient with Propionic Acidemia. Case reports in pediatrics, 2016, 6380929. https://doi.org/10.1155/2016/6380929 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Moses Bachan No relevant relationships by Chen Chao No relevant relationships by Zinobia Khan No relevant relationships by Anastas Kostojchin
