N2  - Aims The aim of this project was to determine the rate of blood culture contaminant results in neonates screened for sepsis over the past 1 year at a district general hospital and review the need for improved blood culture taking procedures and reduced sepsis screening. Sepsis is a significant cause of both mortality and morbidity in neonates. Blood cultures are the gold standard test for identifying bacteraemia in patients where sepsis is suspected, making it a crucial and commonly used diagnostic tool within neonatal units. However, the growth of contaminants in blood cultures presents a diagnostic challenge for clinicians and weakens the reliability of blood culture results. Multiple studies show the costs associated with each contaminated blood culture as between USD $3000 and $10,000.1 Methods A BadgerNet search was conducted for all neonates screened for sepsis ('All Sepsis Screens') from May 31st 2020 - May 31st 2021, and filtered to those that had positive blood cultures. Of these, the following factors were determined: • Blood culture result and number of positive cultures • Clinical correspondence to baby (examination findings and CRP result) • Whether lumbar puncture was performed • Length of antibiotic treatment • Clinical indication for antibiotics • Whether to start antibiotics based on the Kaiser Permanente (KP) Calculator Results The BadgerNet search for neonates screened for sepsis over the past 1 year yielded 999 patients. Of these, 18 had positive blood cultures (1.8%), of which 17 were contaminant results (94%) and only 1 significant positive culture: group B streptococcus. 6/18 (33.3%) had a normal examination. 4/18 (22%) had a lumbar puncture. All 18 neonates received a course of antibiotics. Average length of antibiotic duration was 4 days. According to the KP Calculator, only 2 neonates would have required antibiotic use, 11 would not require antibiotic use and 5 were not applicable due to the gestation. Conclusion According to the KP calculator, if used we would be screening less babies, therefore treating less with antibiotics and reducing inpatient stay for neonates. Out of the positive blood cultures, 94% were contaminants, therefore this can be reduced by ensuring an asceptic non-touch technique. There are multiple methods identified to reduce contamination including skin preparation, culture bottle preparation, single versus double needle for bottle inoculation, source of culture, use of dedicated phlebotomy teams, use of sterile blood culture collection kits, use of initial specimen diversion devices, and the use of sterile gloves. Studies show that the introduction of standardized protocols to support blood culture collection methods and staff education on best practices significantly reduces blood culture contamination rates. We plan to re-audit this in May 2022 to compare the number of neonates screened for sepsis once the KP calculator has been implemented, the rate of positive blood cultures and blood culture contaminant results over the year and compare to current data.
