N2  - Objectives and Study: Antibiotics for suspicion of early-onset-sepsis (EOS) are routinely administered in preterm infants. When EOS diagnosis is rejected based on blood culture results 4872h postnatally, the antibiotic course is sometimes prolonged based on the clinician's judgement. Potential influence of antibiotic exposure on the risk of necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) and late-onset sepsis (LOS) is still debated. Aim of this study was to investigate the duration of early empirical antibiotic exposure (EEAE) in relation to the risk of NEC and LOS in preterm infants. Methods: In this multicentre cohort study, conducted in nine neonatal intensive care units in the Netherlands and Belgium, infants born at <30 weeks' gestation were included between October 2014 and July 2019. Association of EEAE, and development of NEC (modified Bell's stage ≥ 2a) and culture-proven LOS was analysed by multivariate logistic regression. Results: A total of 1259 infants born before 30 weeks of gestation were included. Overall, NEC and LOS incidence was 8% and 33%, respectively. Infants with a short course (≤72h) of antibiotics had a lower incidence of NEC when compared to both infants without initial antibiotic exposure (adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 0.39; 95% confidence interval (CI) [0.19-0.80]; p=0.01) and with prolonged (>72h) antibiotic exposure (aOR [95%CI]: 0.58 [0.35-0.96]; p=0.03). With every additional day of antibiotic exposure, LOS incidence decreased (aOR [95%CI]: 0.90 [0.85-0.97]; p=0.003). Conclusions: NEC and LOS incidence differed based on duration of EEAE for EOS suspicion. The role of antibiotics, and potentially induced dysbiosis, in development of NEC and LOS should further be explored both in longitudinal microbiota sequence studies and RCTs.
