N2  - Background: Early onset neonatal sepsis (EOS) is defined as sepsis under 72 hours of life. EOS can present with non-specific signs and left untreated, may lead to rapid deterioration and death. The Kaiser Permanente EOS Risk Calculator aims to target use of antimicrobials for neonates with true risk of serious bacterial infection whilst reducing unnecessary antibiotic use. It is currently implemented in many Australian neonatal centres without reported adverse outcomes, with most using an EOS rate of ∼0.5 in 1000 births. Current Royal Darwin Hospital guidelines are modelled after the risk-based NICE guidelines. Methods: In this single centre retrospective case series, we reviewed a 10 year cohort of culture-confirmed EOS at the Royal Darwin Hospital. We retrospectively applied the EOS calculator using EOS incidences of 0.5, 1, and 4 in 1000 to assess proportion of cases which would have been appropriately detected using the EOS calculator, compared with hospital's current guidelines. Results: Using the Kaiser Permanente EOS calculator applying EOS incidences of 0.5, 1 and 4 in 1000, would not have detected 5 of 12 (41.67%) cases, 2 of 12 (16.67%) cases, and 1 of 12 (8.33%) cases respectively. Conclusions: The possibility of missing EOS using the Kaiser Permanente EOS calculator must be weighed against benefits of reducing investigations and empiric antibiotic treatment in our hospital. A prospective study implementing the calculator with adequate safety controls to compare the rates of blood collection, empiric antibiotic use, morbidity and mortality outcomes will assist in decision to implement the tool into routine care.
