N2  - Background: Empirical antibiotic exposure in the neonatal period increases the risk of morbidity and mortality. The Kaiser Permanente Neonatal Sepsis Calculator reduces neonatal antibiotic exposure by predicting the likelihood of early-onset sepsis (EOS) in neonates born at >34 weeks gestation. However, there is currently no equivalent tool for very premature (VPT) neonates. The aim of this study is to develop an equivalent tool for VPT infants. Methods: The ANZNN provided data for neonates born at <32 weeks' gestation and then admitted to an Australian or New Zealand neonatal intensive care unit between 1 January 2007 and 31 December 2018. We conducted a retrospective cohort study and created a logistic regression model, which formed the basis of our predictive tool. Results: This study included 43,178 neonates that met the inclusion criteria. The most significant predictors for early-onset infection from the regression model included prolonged rupture of membranes >18 hours (OR 2.49; 95% CI 2.10-3.00, P < 0.001), fetal distress (OR 1.78; 95% CI 1.48-2.14, P < 0.001) and preterm labour (OR 1.45; 95% CI 1.19-1.78, P < 0.001). Protective maternal factors included hypertension in pregnancy (OR 0.44; 95%CI 0.29-0.66, P < 0.001) and non-singleton pregnancy (OR 0.42; 95% CI 0.32-0.54, P < 0.001). These factors were then included in our predictive tool. Conclusions: We have developed a tool that predicts the likelihood of EOS in VPT neonates, which we plan to validate prospectively.
