N2  - Sepsis accounts for approximately 20% of global deaths, and early diagnosis is a critical factor in intervention. Procalcitonin (PCT) is an established biomarker for bacterial infection and there are recognised intervals for clinical interpretation in the context of sepsis. Antibiotic therapy is indicated for PCT results >2.0 ng/mL and this has implications for antibiotic stewardship in the age of antibiotic resistance. A recent review of PCT EQAs has highlighted variable performance between assays.1 Analysis of the RCPAQAP quantitative PCT program results indicated there was method variation in PCT measurement, which was statistically significant at higher PCT concentrations. This suggests clinical decision points need to be validated for each assay. For semi-quantitative PCT methods, concordance to the target interval, based on submitted quantitative results, ranged from 17–92%. Based on our observations at the 2.0 ng/mL cut off, use of semi-quantitative methods could lead to a delay or premature cessation of antibiotics in 8–15% of sepsis cases, or inappropriate or prolonged use in 21–31% cases. Note that commutability of the program material has yet to be determined. Reference 1. Huynh H-H, Bœuf A, Pfannkuche J, et al. Harmonization status of procalcitonin measurements: what do comparison studies and EQA schemes tell us? Clin Chem Lab Med 2021; 59: 1610–22.
