N2  - Objectives: Ascending infection by Gram negative pathogens, such as E. coli are associated with maternal morbidity, preterm delivery, and neonatal sepsis. By comparison, only a small number of studies have explored adverse reproductive outcomes associated with nontypable Haemophilus. Our objective was to determine if adverse pregnancy outcomes are associated with placental cultures positive for Haemophilus species. Methods: Placental cultures were sent per hospital protocol, with positive cultures reviewed for bacterial identification and drug resistance profiles per laboratory protocol. All positive cultures isolated between 2014-2017 were reviewed and a chart review for maternal, fetal and neonatal characteristics was performed. A composite maternal adverse outcome measure was comprised of delivery <29 weeks, prolonged antibiotic course of 7 or more days, ICU admission, or readmission for infection. A composite neonatal adverse outcome was comprised of IUFD/neonatal death, pre-viable delivery, prolonged antibiotic course of 7 or more days, or diagnosis of NEC. As a comparison, adverse outcomes were determined for placental cultures positive for E. coli. Results: In total, 168 placental cultures were positive for pathogenic isolates. Of those, 35 were positive for Haemophilus species (20.8%) and 60 for E. Coli (35.7%). Nontypable haemophilus cultures were associated with adverse outcomes at similar rates to E. Coli including PPROM (40% vs. 40%, p > 0.99), delivery/SAB prior to 25weeks (37.1% vs. 30%, p=0.47) and intrauterine infection (48.5% vs. 41.6%, p =0.51). The composite maternal and neonatal adverse outcome rates were also similar (51.4% vs. 46.7%, p=0.65) and (57.1% vs. 56.7%, P=0.96). Conclusions: In this contemporary cohort, pregnancies with placental culture with Haemophilus species were associated with adverse outcomes at rates similar to established pathogens. Our studies suggest that H. influenzae should continue to be studied for its pathogenic potential in pregnancy. [Formula presented]
