N2  - Background: Neonatal sepsis (NS) is a potentially life-threatening clinical condition that requires early intervention. Initial symptoms are generally nonspecific and may mimic several other medical conditions. NS is an important cause of mortality and morbidity in neonatal populations. There has been constant search of an ideal sepsis biomarker with high sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV) and negative predictive value (NPV), so that both the diagnosis and exclusion of neonatal sepsis can be made at the earliest possible and appropriate antibiotics can be started to neonate. Ideal sepsis biomarker will help in guiding us when not to start antibiotics in case of suspect sepsis and total duration of antibiotics course in case of proven sepsis. The objective of this study was to study the correlation between MPV and SUA and diagnosis and of neonatal sepsis. Methods: This study was a case control study which conducted at Neonatology unit, Pediatrics department, Zagazig University Hospitals. Mean Platelet Volume and Serum Uric Acid were measured for all neonates. Results: Caesarian section among patients with neonatal sepsis was (60%). Regarding blood culture among cases group, Klebsilla was the common organism. Mean platelet volume was significantly higher in patients than controls. There was statistically significant difference between Confirmed neonatal sepsis group, Suspected neonatal sepsis and Control group regarding MPV. In the current study, we found There was statistically significant difference between Confirmed neonatal sepsis group and Suspected neonatal sepsis group regarding uric acid. There was statistically significant difference between Confirmed neonatal sepsis group and Control group regarding uric acid. There was no statistically significant difference between Suspected neonatal sepsis group and Control group regarding uric acid. In this study, regarding Diagnostic accuracy of MPV, Sensitivity was 96.7%, Specificity was 93.3%, PPV was 67.4%, NPV was 50% and accuracy was 66.7%. Regarding Diagnostic accuracy of Uric acid, Sensitivity was 50%, Specificity was 93.3%, PPV was 93.8%, NPV was 48.3% and accuracy was 64.4%. Conclusion: MPV increases significantly in neonates with sepsis while SUA decreases significantly in neonates with sepsis. MPV could be a useful as early diagnostic marker in neonatal sepsis, than SUA level.
