N2  - Background:About 1.6 million deaths occur annually in neonates due to septicaemia and meningitis in developing coun- tries. Worldwide, there is emerging resistance to antibiotics for neonatal septicaemia. In perinatal period due to multi- ple exposures and compromised immune system, neonates are predisposed to infection. In this study,the pathogens causing neonatal sepsis are studied with their antibiotic sensitivity and resistance pattern Methods:A total of 150 blood samples were received from Neonatal intensive care unit of old GGH VJA, from January 2020 to October 2020 to Department of microbiology. Samples consisted of 2ml of venous blood obtained from neo- nates by aseptic procedures and inoculated into brain heart infusion broth (BHI) and incubated for 24 hours. Subcul- tures were done on to fresh 5% sheep blood agar and Mac Conkey’s agar. Isolated organisms were identified by cultural characters, morphology and standard biochemical tests. Antibiotic susceptibility testing was performed by Modified Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion method, and results were interpreted as per CLSI guidelines 2019. Results:Of the 150 blood samples processed, 108 (72%) were culture sterile. Klebsiella pneumoniae were 17 isolates (40.47%) was the most commonly isolated organism, followed by Staphylococcus aureus 10 isolates (23.8%), Escherichia coli 7 isolates (16.66%), Coagulase negative staphylococcus aureus 4 isolates (9.52%), Pseudomonas aeruginosa 2 iso- lates (4.76%) and Acinetobacter baumanii 2 isolates (4.76%). Gram negative organisms were sensitive to imipenem, cefoperazone & sulbactam, amikacin, Piperacillin & Tazobactam, gentamycin, cefixime and resistant to Ceftriaxone, Levofloxacin and Ceftazidime.Gram-positive organisms were sensitive to linezolid, vancomycin, ciprofloxacin, ceftazidime and resistant to penicillin, doxycycline, Amoxycillin and Erythromycin. Conclusions:The above study concludes that the pathogens causing neonatal sepsis are mostly gram negative organisms predominantly Klebsiella pneumoniae and gram positive being Staphylococcus aureus. Effective hand washing, barrier nursing and strict aseptic measures are to be followed while taking care of neonates. Antibiotic stewardship policies should be followed to decrease the emergence of multi drug resistant organisms.
