N2  - Background:Neonatal sepsis is the invasive bacterial infection occuring in the first month of life.It is significant cause of morbidity and mortality in the neonates.The current spread of multidrug resistant bacteria has further heightened the need for regular bacteriological review of blood infection and regular antibiotic surveillance to avoid the unguided em- perical treatment of blood infection. Methods:A total of 246 neonates were included in the study.Blood from each neonate was cultured and isolates were identified using standard biochemical test.Antibiotic sensitivity testing was done by modified Kirby -Bauer disc diffusion method according to CLSI guidelines. Results:Out of 246 samples, 108(43.9%) were culture positive.Most of the cases 174(70.73%) were of early onset sepsis while 72(29.2%) late onset sepsis.Gram negative bacilli were isolated in predominance 82(75.92%) and Gram positive cocci were 26(24.07%).klebsiella spp. (51.85%) was the predominant organism isolated followed by staphylococcus au- reus (17.59%). Conclusions:Increasing resistance to antimicrobials increases the risk of morbidity and mortality.Hence,there is urgent need of implementation of measures to restrict the health care associated infection.There is need to institute antibiotic stewardship and effective infection control measures in the hospital
