N2  - Introduction Sepsis, due to pregnancy related infections, is the third leading, direct, cause of maternal mortality in the UK. Prompt assessment and management is integral to improving maternal outcomes. Methods An audit proforma was created using local guidelines. Data were collected prospectively identifying women experiencing pyrexia in labour on the postnatal ward following delivery. Results Twenty-four women were included. Nine-women (33%) were obese but no-one had gestational diabetes, anaemia or preterm rupture of membranes. One-woman had undergone an amniocentesis and one had a recent illness contact. The majority (75%) of women where asymptomatic, 7-women had >6 vaginal examinations, 6-women had ruptured membranes for >18-hours and 2-women were GBS positive. The was no positive microbiology but blood cultures and MSU's were missed in the management of 9 and 11 women respectively. In keeping with local guidelines 21-women (88%) were correctly prescribed intravenous antibiotics while in contrast only 4-women (17%) were correctly converted to oral antibiotics. 19-women (79%) had antibiotics accurately prescribed, including a documented indication, but only 12-women (50%) had a documented justification for conversion to oral antibiotics. Conclusions Management of women experiencing pyrexia in labour was good but the conversion to oral antibiotics did not, for the majority, follow local guidelines. It can be difficult when trainees rotate around trusts to keep abreast of local guidelines so it has been recommended that they download 'Micro-Guide' an App containing a local antimicrobial guide to see if this can improve compliance prior to the next audit.
