N2  - AIMS & OBJECTIVES: Atypical germs are emerging,newborns are more and more infected with these germs, difficult to diagnose and manage. Serratia Marcescens (S. Marcescens)is a ubiquitous pathogen, which can cause nosocomial infection, rare in newborns, but serious (considerable morbidity and mortality). The objective of our study is to report our experience, during the 4 epidemics to S.Marcescens, in order to reveal the epidemiological and etiological factors. METHODS: These are 4 epidemics in S.Marcescens dated respectively: in the month of August 2017 relating to 8 patients, in January 2018 relating to 6 patients, in June 2018 with 5 cases and in February 2020 relating to 2 patients. In University Hospital, Mohamed VI, Marrakesh, Morocco. RESULTS: The mean gestational age of the cases was 33 weeks of gestation.Boys represented 70% of cases. The average weight was 1828 grams.All the sick were were initially put on empirical antibiotic therapy, as soon as they were admitted, based on C3G and Gentamycin.The average duration of onset of nosocomial infection,diagnosed in blood cultures in 90% of cases and by respiratory PCR in 10% of cases, was 7 days.All patients were treated with Imipenem and Amikacin.The outcome was fatal in 70% of the cases. CONCLUSIONS: Nosocomial infections with S.Marcescens are often reported in epidemic series and their eradication is not always easy.
