N2  - INTRODUCTION: Early antibiotic administration is one of the only measures consistently shown to decrease mortality in sepsis. Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines recommend full loading doses of broad spectrum antibiotics within 1 hr of diagnosis. We evaluated timeliness of first and second antibiotic doses and appropriateness and adequacy of initial antibiotic doses in patients with sepsis at our institution, hypothesizing that most initial doses would be given within 1 hr of diagnosis. METHODS: In this IRB-approved, single-center, retrospective study of patients with sepsis at our institution from 1/2018-6/2019, we reviewed medical records to assess timeliness of first antibiotic doses using SEP-1 criteria to determine time 0. Secondary outcomes were timeliness of second doses, defined based on whether they were given within 25% of the appropriate interval, and whether initial antibiotics were appropriate and dosed adequately based on IDSA and institutional guidelines. Exploratory endpoints included whether an antibiotic with Gram-positive or Gramnegative coverage was given first, hospital length of stay, and mortality. Data collected included demographics, comorbidities, labs and vitals at time of diagnosis, disposition, and antibiotic information. Outcomes were reported with descriptive statistics. RESULTS: 153 patients were assessed. Median time to first antibiotic was 50 min (IQR 0-96 min). Of 105 patients who received antibiotics after time 0, 37% received the first antibiotic within 1 hr, 34% received it within 1-2 hr, and 29% received it after 2 hr. Second doses were given within 25% of the recommended interval in 59% of cases with most doses outside this window given prematurely. Initial antibiotic choice and dosing were appropriate in 79% and 60% of cases, respectively. Vancomycin was inadequately dosed in 72% of cases. Median length of stay was 7 days and 14% died in-hospital. CONCLUSIONS: Initial antibiotics were given more than 1 hr after sepsis diagnosis in a notable number of cases, as evidenced by the 63% of patients who received doses after time 0 and outside of the 1 hr bundle goal. Second doses were often given within the correct interval and initial antibiotic choice was appropriate. Initial antibiotic doses were inadequate in a sizable group of cases, mainly due to vancomycin dosing.
