N2  - INTRODUCTION: Objective criteria are needed to guide antibiotic duration in pediatric intensive care units (PICUs) to reduce unnecessary antibiotic use and bacterial resistance in this setting. Inflammatory biomarkers have been successfully used to personalize treatment duration in adults and neonates,1,2 but their utility in children is unknown. Our objective was thus to characterize the temporal behaviour of inflammatory biomarkers (C-reactive protein [CRP], procalcitonin, white blood cell [WBC] count, and temperature) in children with severe bacterial infections, and to determine the association between temporal behaviour and hospital length of stay. METHODS: This is an ongoing prospective cohort study in 7 Canadian PICUs (since February 2017). Eligible patients are between 1 month and <18 years old admitted with suspected or proven severe bacterial infection (sepsis, pneumonia, and central nervous system and intrabdominal infections) and for whom ≥1 antimicrobial was initiated. We measure levels of the aforementioned biomarkers from days 1 to 7 and day 10 of antibiotic treatment and collect data on patient demographics and clinical evolution. We used multivariable linear regressions to evaluate the association between a ≥50% drop in CRP and procalcitonin levels from days 1 to 3 and hospital length of stay. RESULTS: We have enrolled 208 patients. The median age was 56.0 months (interquartile range [IQR] 14.8 - 103.0) and 53% presented comorbidities. Median antibiotic duration was 9.5 days (IQR 6.0 - 14.0). Absolute levels of CRP and procalcitonin were highest on day 1 and descended over the course of antibiotic treatment. By day 5 of antibiotic treatment, 82% and 43% of patients had ≥80% drop in procalcitonin and CRP levels, respectively. Patients who had ≥50% drop in CRP levels from days 1 to 3 had a shorter length of hospital stay (-6.4 days, 95%CI -12.2, -0.8). There was a strong correlation between mean daily values of multiple organ dysfunction syndrome scores with mean daily CRP (0.93, 95%CI 0.66, 0.99) and procalcitonin values (0.98, 95%CI 0.88, 0.99). CONCLUSIONS: Preliminary data suggest that CRP and procalcitonin may be good candidate biomarkers to guide antibiotic duration in children. Further analysis will determine the best cut-off and combination of biomarkers to guide duration.
