N2  - INTRODUCTION Neonatal infections are a frequent cause of morbidity and mortality in neonatal intensive care units. Early-onset sepsis (EOS) can occur in the first 72 h of life, where the pathogens, often Group B streptococcus and Escherichia coli, are transmitted vertically. Initial symptomology is insidious and the most commonly used diagnostic tests have low sensibility and specificity at onset. It is for this reason that unnecessary antibiotic therapy is often started. The sepsis calculator is a predictive model for use in the management of neonatal risk of EOS. STUDY AIM To estimate the suitability of the sepsis score in identifying which newborns with a gestational age (GA)>34 weeks need antibiotic therapy. MATERIALS AND METHODS An observational study was carried out (February-December 2019) with the enrolment of all newborns, GA >34 weeks, who, according to the protocols used, required blood tests. All newborns received antibiotic therapy. Newborns were subdivided into 3 groups, according to the presence of: • Maternal risk factors: 51 newborns (68%) • Symptomology: 10 newborns (13.3%) • Maternal risk factors associated to symptomology: 14 newborns (18.7%) Each group was subdivided on the basis of culture exam (positive/negative). According to the sepsis score, the population was divided as follows: • 53 newborns (70.7%): did not need either haemoculture or antibiotic therapy • 2 newborns (2.7%): needed haemoculture with evaluation of vital signs •5 newborns (6.7%): 'to evaluate' antibiotic therapy • 15 newborns (20%): rapid start of antibiotic therapy RESULTS 75 newborns were enrolled out of a total of 3514 born; 6 with positive haemoculture. The incidence of EOS considered is 1.7/1,000 live births. In the patients with a positive haemoculture, 4 showed symptoms and in accordance with sepsis score indications required immediate antibiotic therapy without waiting for the results of the culture tests. Nonetheless, according to the sepsis score evaluation, one patient with a positive E. coli culture, high CRP and PCT values, symptoms and presence of maternal risk factors, who started antibiotic therapy, was 'just to evaluate.' CONCLUSIONS While it can be rapidly applied, in our opinion the sepsis score should be used with the aim of better identifying newborns with certain or suspect sepsis. More attention should be placed on maternal risk factors, in particular, a positive anamnesis for neonatal GBS infection; GBS bacteriuria; E. coli infection; chorioamnionitis. The sepsis score suggests carrying out only haemoculture that surely represents the test which allows a confirmed diagnosis of infection while identifying the most suitable antibiotic therapy. However, the bacterial load and the quantity of blood taken may influence the positivity of the exam: with 1ml, sensitivity is 30-40%, with 3ml 70-80%. For this reason, it might be useful to associate haemachrome, CRP and PCT. Moreover, a negative haemoculture does not exclude meningitis and thus a lumbar puncture would appear to be indispensable for a diagnosis of sepsis. The limitation of the study is that it is retrospective, taking into consideration only patients who underwent antibiotic therapy and not all patients who underwent exams for higher risk of infection.
