N2  - Through assisted reproductive technology (ART) a considerable number of infertile couples are given the chance to have healthy infants. It is not yet clear whether ART-related twins have higher risks than spontaneous conceived twins. Literature reported an increase in preterm birth, higher proportion of NICU admission, without augmented perinatal death and/or severe morbidity. But what is clear, is that multiple pregnancies are associated with increased risk of perinatal, maternal, mortality and morbidity compared to singleton pregnancies. We reported a case of maternal cholestasis in a multiple pregnancy of ART. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is characterized by pruritus, elevated bile acids and liver enzymes, and occasionally jaundice. Bile acid levels and liver function tests may be normal at presentation in women with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP). Symptomatic and therapeutic treatment with ursodeoxycholic acid is usually initiated. Preterm delivery, meconium-stained amniotic fluid, and respiratory distress commonly complicate these pregnancies. The differential diagnosis includes preeclampsia, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, HELLP syndrome, choledocholithiasis, and hepatitis. Although there are no universally accepted guidelines for delivery, the specific timing of delivery may be guided by patient symptoms, bile acid levels and reproductive history. Elevated levels of bile acid can be documented in amniotic fluid and cord serum of infants whose mothers are affected with ICP. The exact mechanism leading to fetal and subsequent neonatal complications is poorly understood. We describe a case of a 40-year-old woman with a triplet pregnancy resulting from a heterologous in vitro fertilization presented at 31 weeks for generalized pruritus. The patient has been admitted for case care. Blood tests revealed elevated liver enzymes, with normal coagulation parameters and sierum bilirubin, without hemolysis, other causes of hypertransaminasemia were ruled out. Non-significant maternal sierologies, pregestational hypothyroidism, in anamnesis. Serial ultrasonography was done with a normal fetus biometry was documented. During her hospital stay, the patient received two doses of betamethasone. A progressive increase in liver enzymes, despite the treatment with ursodeoxycholic acid and s-adenosylmethionine, with a peak value of ALT 1322 UI/L, AST 732 UI/L, indicated an elective caesarean section. She had 3 female babies with low birth weights (1252 g,1230 g, 1438 g). Apgar score were at 5' and 10' min respectively: 9-9, 7-9, 8-9. The babies required nCPAP, as delivery room management, and were transferred to NICU for supportive therapy. No malformation except for first and second toe complete syndactyly of the first child. The first two children received empiric antibiotic treatment, (blood cultures remained sterile with negative C reactive protein) and 2 weeks of nCPAP support, with substantial clinical improvement and stable hemodynamic conditions. The first child required also a transfusion due to severe anemia. The largest baby developed anemia requiring tranfusion, jaundice treated with phototherapy and sepsis needing intubation, an intensive care unit stay and she is still hospitalized. The placenta was bichorionic weighing 1020 g with mild signs of chronic hypoxia. This case proves that pregnancy could represent a considerable stress test for the woman, and an adequate timing of delivery could be the best cure.
