N2  - Background: Biofilms play a pivotal role in the establishment of chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA) pulmonary infection in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF). Chronic PA infections are associated with accelerated lung function decline and premature mortality. We previously showed that PA isolates from new-onset infections that failed inhaled tobramycin eradication therapy (persistent isolates) produce a form of the exopolysaccharide Psl in biofilms that binds to the staphylococcal protein A (SpA). Psl-SpA binding resulted in bacterial aggregation, leading to tobramycin tolerance. Upon further investigation, we also found that these persistent PA isolates had greater binding to anti-Psl monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) compared to successfully eradicated isolates when grown as biofilms. Thus, the goal of this study was to examine biofilm structural differences between PA isolates that were eradicated compared to those that persisted in the airways of children with CF following antibiotic treatment. Furthermore, differences in viability of the two groups following antibiotic exposure was evaluated. Methods: Using PA isolates from the SickKids Eradication Cohort, cultured from new-onset infections (defined as a PA-positive sputum culture with at least 12 preceding months of negative respiratory tract cultures), we compared 7 eradicated and 7 persistent isolates. Eradication was defined as having a negative culture for PA from the first sputum sample taken following inhaled tobramycin therapy. PA biofilms were grown in an 8-chambered coverglass prior to addition of fluorescently labelled (red) anti-Psl mAbs, targeting three defined epitopes of Psl (Psl0096, WapR001 and WapR016). A fluorescently labelled (red) isotype of IgG was used as a control to confirm absence of non-specific binding. Structural biofilm changes in the presence of anti-Psl mAbs were examined using a live-cell (green) fluorescent stain and visualized by confocal microscopy, followed by COMSTAT analysis. Cell viability was also confirmed using an ATP assay. Results: There was increased anti-Psl mAb binding to persistent compared to eradicated PA isolates in biofilms, resulting in increased aggregation and tobramycin resistance. COMSTAT analysis of live cells showed tobramycin decreased biomass in both eradicated and persistent isolates. In the presence of both tobramycin and anti-Psl mAbs however, there was increased biofilm thickness and biomass, as well as decreased surface-to-biovolume ratio (indication of aggregation) compared to the IgG control. Furthermore, a significantly greater luminescence (signal that is directly proportional to the amount of ATP present) was measured among persistent compared to eradicated PA isolates in presence of tobramycin and anti-Psl mAbs (p<0.0001). Conclusions: PA isolates causing new-onset infection in children with CF that failed inhaled tobramycin therapy had higher anti-Psl mAb binding compared to successfully eradicated isolates when grown as biofilms. This resulted in bacterial aggregation, tobramycin resistance, and increased viability (ATP present) in persistent isolates, suggesting that certain bacterial characteristics may contribute to failure of antimicrobial eradication therapy.
