N2  - Objective: The diagnosis of ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) in the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) remains debatable because cultures from endotracheal aspirates are often contaminated. We aimed to investigate the role of non-bronchoscopic bronchoalveolar lavage (NB-BAL) for diagnosis of VAP. Methods: A 24-month (October 2017 to September 2019), prospective randomized controlled trial was conducted at a tertiary-level neonatal intensive care unit (NICU). All patients with clinically suspected VAP were randomized enrolled as the study group (NB-BAL was done) and the control group (diagnosis based only on cultures of endotracheal aspirates). The diagnostic accuracy, treatment outcomes and duration of ventilation were compared. Results: During the study period, a total of 130 episodes of VAP were clinically diagnosed. 64 received NB-BAL and 66 were treated based on cultures of endotracheal aspirates. Their demographics were comparable. 37 (57.8%) of the NB-BAL showed positive cultures. In the study group, the concordance between endotracheal aspirate and NB-BAL was complete in 54.0% and completely discordant in 18.9%. The appropriateness of antibiotic prescription, duration of antibiotic treatment, VAP-contributed mortality, and duration of ventilation were not significantly different between the study group and control group. Conclusions: The quantitative cultures of endotracheal aspirate are poorly concordant with quantitative cultures obtained through NB-BAL. The treatment outcomes were not significantly different between cultures from NB-BAL and traditional endotracheal aspirate.
