N2  - Introduction Disseminated enteroviral disease can be devastating with high morbidity and mortality. There are few reported cases of neonatal enteroviral sepsis and myocarditis and only one case report on twins who received veno-arterial extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (VA-ECMO) who survived. Currently no standard of care for severe enteroviral sepsis exists, however early VA-ECMO and intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) may show improve survival. Case Report Ten-day old twin, term neonates presented to the emergency department somnolent with petechiae after three days of decreased oral intake. Both had severe thrombocytopenia and were admitted to the PICU for empiric antibiotic management and stepwise evaluation of severe alloimmune thrombocytopenia. Upon PICU admission both twins developed mild respiratory distress and received supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula. Within hours twin A developed acute abdominal distention, irregular breathing and suffered subsequent cardiac arrest. Given her thrombocytopenia with presumed ongoing hemorrhage ECMO was not recommended and she expired after an hour of resuscitation. Her respiratory viral panel returned positive for rhinovirus/enterovirus infection. Within hours twin B developed progressive respiratory compromise requiring endotracheal intubation. She remained hemodynamically stable for 24 hours prior to developing profound hypotension requiring high dose vasoactive support. She was cannulated onto VA -ECMO. Echocardiography demonstrated severely reduced left ventricular function. An autopsy of twin A eventually revealed lymphocytic myocardial infiltrate consistent with viral myocarditis, as well as bilateral adrenal hemorrhage and bronchopneumonia. Serum testing in both twins was positive for enterovirus. Twin B received IVIG 5 g/kg over the first 5 days of hospitalization. She was eventually weaned off ECMO and all vasoactive support with gradual normalizing echocardiographic left heart function in the outpatient setting. Discussion Disseminated enteroviral infection can be lethal in the neonate. Proposed risk factors for increased mortality include prematurity, infection in first week of life, and lack of maternal neutralizing antibodies. Pocapavir is an investigational agent that may show some future promise in this clinical setting. Enterovirus has a high prevalence in the population, and as such, standard IVIG preparations contain high levels of virus-neutralizing antibodies. In vitro studies show more rapid clearance of viremia in patients who receive IVIG, and there are some case studies which suggest a myocardial protective effect of IVIG when given to patients with other manifestations of severe enteroviral infection. Despite the benefit of IVIG in our patient, however, the severity of her cardiac dysfunction would have resulted in a poor outcome without ECMO. Conclusion: We presented a patient who was successfully treated for severe disseminated enteroviral disease with early use of VA-ECMO and IVIG. We suggest that early initiation of VA-ECMO may be necessary and life-saving in the setting of overwhelming neonatal enteroviral infection.
