N2  - Background: Signs of late-onset septicemia (LOS) and necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) overlap with normal variations in preterm neonatal physiology, and about 10-20 sepsis evaluations are performed and antibiotics given for each case of proven LOS or NEC. Early diagnosis is important for improving outcomes, but unnecessary antibiotics increase multiple morbidities. Objective: To develop a model for accurate prediction of LOS or NEC using clinical variables and signs at the time of LOS/NEC evaluation. Methods: Records of consecutive very low birth weight (VLBW) infant admissions at 3 NICUs were reviewed, excluding those that died within 3 days of birth or were admitted after 28d. For infants with F0B31 blood culture for suspected LOS/NEC, the first positive workup was evaluated and if all workups were negative, the first negative was evaluated. A positive LOS/NEC evaluation was defined as a positive blood culture or radiographic diagnosis of NEC and F0B3 5d of antibiotics given, and sepsis ruled out (SRO) was defined as negative cultures and <5d antibiotics given. Cases of focal infection and clinical sepsis (F0B35d antibiotics despite negative cultures) were excluded from modeling. Demographic, perinatal and clinical variables were analyzed to develop a baseline risk model predicting LOS/NEC. Medical record documentation of clinical, laboratory or radiographic signs prompting each workup were analyzed to develop a separate model, which included signs prompting workups that resulted in LOS/NEC diagnosis more often than SRO by univariate analysis (Figure 1). Predictive models were developed using multivariable logistic regression, with model performance assessed using area under the receiver operator characteristic curve (AUC) with confidence intervals determined using bootstrap methods. Models were compared using net reclassification improvement (NRI). Results: 633 VLBW infants met inclusion criteria and, of these, 451 (71%) had at least one blood culture evaluation for LOS or NEC. Of those reviewed, 161 resulted in a diagnosis of LOS/NEC, 177 SRO and 113 focal infection or clinical sepsis. Figure 1 shows the proportion of evaluations in which LOS/NEC vs. SRO was diagnosed for each clinical sign documented. Results of logistic regression models using baseline variables alone, clinical signs alone and the two models combined yielded AUCs [95% CIs] with increasing predictive performance: baseline model AUC = 0.700 [0.641 - 0.760], clinical signs model AUC = 0.736 [0.681 - 0.792] and combined model AUC = 0.790 [0.739 - 0.841]. The continuous NRI of the combined model was 0.657 [0.442 - 0.872] (p<0.0001), which translates to a 65.7% improvement in prediction over the baseline model (Figure 2). Conclusions: In a multicenter cohort of VLBW infants, a combined baseline risk and clinical signs model performed better than either model alone to predict, at the time a sepsis workup was performed, the ultimate diagnosis of LOS or NEC versus SRO. The list of clinical signs shown includes the most commonly cited reasons for blood culture to evaluate for sepsis. Some overlapping signs were grouped into more general clinical signs for analysis. The size of the horizontal bars represents the proportion of positive (red bars on the left, LOS or NEC) and negative (blue bars on the right, sepsis ruled out) evaluations in which a particular clinical sign was a factor in the decision to draw a blood culture and start antibiotics. Asterisks indicate clinical signs with a significant difference in the proportion of evaluations resulting in LOS/NEC vs. SRO by Fisher's exact test (∗p<0.05). ABDs = apnea, bradycardia, desaturation spells; CBC = complete blood count; CRP = C-reactive protein. Results of predicted probability of LOS/NEC by each model are plotted for infants where blood culture evaluation resulted in a diagnosis of LOS or NEC (red dots) versus SRO (blue dots). The baseline model includes gestational age, birthweight, 1 and 5 minute Apgar scores, sex, delivery mode, whether the infant was on a ventilator at the time of evaluation and whether they had a central venous catheter. The combined model incorporates a second predictive model developed using clinical signs prompting blood culture where univariate p-value was <0.05 (See figure 1: CRP, abnormal CBC, abdominal distention, abnormal X-Ray findings, hypotension, acidosis, urgent intubation). Plotting these two models illustrates the net proportion of infants correctly reclassified as high risk or low risk by adding the clinical signs model to the baseline risk model. Visually, this is represented by the LOS/NEC cases in the left upper quadrant and the SRO cases in the right lower quadrant. Dotted horizontal and vertical lines indicate the score at which the number of infants with higher scores equals the incidence of the outcome.
