N2  - Background: Prolonged and unnecessary use of antibiotics is associated with multi drug resistant organisms. Sepsis Prevention in neonatal intensive care units (NICU's) Group (SPRING) consisting of all NICUs in NSW and ACT has been monitoring and benchmarking the NICUs for neonatal blood stream infections using point of care data that include dates and times of interventions and outcomes. Local QI projects were developed to (a) monitor infections, (b) identify the practice points requiring improvement, and (c) identify areas of priorities for improvement in sepsis reduction. QI Project: Our NICU Infection control team liaised with Antimicrobial stewardship team to rationalise antibiotic usage within our NICU. A meeting held in November 2018 reviewed the antibiogram data available for the year 2016. For late onset sepsis, coagulase negative staphylococcus (CONS) was identified as the most common organism with sensitivity to flucloxacillin at 26% and vancomycin at 100%. Most true positive blood cultures returned the organisms within 24 hours and all within 48 hours. With this information, we decided to develop antibiotic policy to rationalise and cease at 24 hours in infants deemed at low risk of sepsis. Results: Royal Hospital for Women(RHW) NICU (Purple line, F in the graph) showed the shortest duration of antibiotics in the first 72 hours of life in 2019 within NSW and ACT. This was not associated with increased sepsis rate. There was also a reduction within the unit in vancomycin days per 100 baby days. Conclusion: Implementation of new antibiotic policy in our NICU resulted in the lowest usage of antibiotics in the first 72 hours of life within NSW and Act. There was also a reduction in the usage of vancomycin.
