N2  - Background: Sepsis, a leading cause of neonatal mortality worldwide, contributes to almost one-third of neonatal deaths in India. Microorganism profile, resistance pattern, and epidemiology of sepsis in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) are poorly understood. Objective: To study the microorganism profile and antimicrobial resistance (AMR) pattern of neonatal sepsis over 6 years. Methods: Over 6 years (Jan 2013-Dec 2018) demographic profile of neonates labelled as sepsis (based on clinical signs and/or laboratory criteria) was noted along with a meticulous record of microorganisms isolated and AMR profile. Multidrug resistance (MDR) among gram-negative (GN) isolates was defined as resistance to ≥3 antibiotic classes. Unit protocol for empirical antibiotic therapy was: first line: piperacillin, tazobactum, and amikacin, second line: meropenam and amikacin, third line: colistin and amikaicn and vancomicin (in MRSA). Results: Over the study period, 79,655 neonates were born, 16,015 (20%) required admission with 60% (n = 9,700) being preterm. The overall incidence of sepsis was 23% and that of culture-positive sepsis 5%. Of total sepsis, early onset (≤72 h of life) developed in 53 and 14% had meningitis. Three-fifths of all deaths were attributable to sepsis. Out of 795 isolates, 60% were GN. Klebsiella was the most common organism (30%) followed by Staphylococcus aureus (23%), coagulase-negative staphylococci (CONS) (14%), E. coli (10%), and acinetobacter (10%). A high level of MDR was observed in GN (54%) isolates: acinetobacter (63%), klebsiella (55%), and E. coli (50%). Among gram-positive infections, high methicillin resistance was seen: Staphylococcus aureus (62%) and CONS (56%). The rising trend of MDR and methicillin resistance over the years is disturbing (Fig. 1). Conclusion: This study provides insight into the common pathogens responsible for neonatal sepsis along with their antibiotic susceptibility pattern. An alarming degree of AMR underscores the need to understand the pathogenesis of neonatal sepsis in LMICs as well as to conduct trials to determine the best possible antibiotic regimens.
