N2  - Introduction Perinatal-type group A streptococcus (GAS) toxic shock syndrome is rare, occurring in 1-2 cases per 1 million childbirths. It progresses rapidly, with high fetal and maternal mortality rates. We report two cases I the first case led to prompt care of the second, resulting in survival of both mothers. Case 1 The pregnancy of 32-year-old, G2P2, progressed well. At 36 weeks, she consulted her previous doctor for fever and lower abdominal pain, who confirmed the fetus was dead. On suspicion of premature separation of normally implanted placenta, she was transported to our medical department. We had treatments for DIC, and had the emergency cesarean section. Hysterectomy was performed for severe atonic hemorrhage. Culture via endotracheal aspiration revealed GAS on day 8 after. Case 2 • The pregnancy of 33-year-old, G3P1, progressed well. At 37 weeks, she developed abdominal pain and fever. She was diagnosed with non-reassuring fetal status, and underwent emergency cesarean section, but the fetus died. Because postoperative bleeding persisted, she had treatment for DIC in the ICU. Blood tests conducted 4 hours after admission confirmed GAS. Owing to our previous experience, multimodality therapy was initiated for perinatal-type GAS toxic shock syndrome. Discussion • We encountered two perinatal-type GAS toxic shock syndrome cases, in which both mothers survived. Qualitative analysis using the MALDI Biotyper system (Bruker) revealed the bacterial strain in 10 minutes, enabling early antibiotics selection and administration. For fever during the third trimester, pretreatment testing is necessary to identify the bacterial strain to differentiate perinatal-type GAS toxic shock syndrome.
