N2  - Purpose: Recent reports indicate that early antibiotic exposure may worsen the severity of bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) in very low birthweight infants. The mechanisms mediating worsened outcomes are unknown, but antibiotic exposure directly perturbs the intestinal microbiome stability and emerging evidence suggests the intestinal microbiota may influence systemic inflammation. Accordingly, we investigated if alteration of the intestinal microbiota altered the development of hyperoxic lung injury in neonatal mice. Methods: We created a two-exposure model that randomized pregnant C57BL/6J mice on the tenth day of gestation to maternal antibiotic treatment (penicillin 500mg/L, MAT) or control. After birth, newborn offspring were randomized to hyperoxia (FiO2 0.85 or 0.60) or FiO2 0.21 for 14 days. Alveolarization was analyzed by histology and morphometry [mean linear intercept (MLI), alveolar heterogeneity (D2) and septal thickness]. Immunohistochemical staining was used to assess cellular proliferation (Ki67) and neutrophilic infiltration (myeloperoxidase). Colonic contents were collected, and the intestinal microbiome was analyzed by 16S ribosomal RNA amplicon sequencing and qRT-PCR. Data are expressed as mean ± SEM (n = 5-8/group in 4-5 repeated experiments) and were analyzed by ANOVA (Bonferroni) with a significance level of P < 0.05. Results: MAT increased the MLI, D2 and septal thickness, in combination with oxygen exposure at FiO2 0.60. When combined with oxygen exposure at FiO2 0.85, interalveolar septal thickening was so markedly pronounced that MLI and D2 were only minimally increased (Figure 1). Neonatal mortality was also increased by the combined exposure. Independent of hyperoxia, MAT was associated with a characteristic septal thickening associated with increased cellular proliferation on Ki67 staining and increased neutrophilic infiltration on myeloperoxidase staining. The complexity and absolute abundance of the neonatal microbiota in colonic contents increased with time. As quantified by alpha diversity and RT-PCR, MAT limited the development of the microbiome by 15 days of age to a level of complexity and abundance equivalent to 5-day old newborn mice. At the bacterial family level, MAT markedly reduced the presence of Lactobacillaceae (42.5% vs. 4.12%) and Verrucomicrobia (41.18% vs. 17.64%) allowing for a corresponding expansion of Staphylococcaceae (2.82% vs. 44.66%) and Bacteroidaceae (2.8% vs. 25.79%, Figure 2). Conclusion: Peripartum penicillin exposure influences the development of BPD in this mouse model of hyperoxic lung injury and inhibits the maturation of the intestinal microbiota. Changes in cellular composition and proliferation within the interalveolar septa suggest differences in alveolar architecture are immune-mediated. (Figure Presented).
