N2  - Background: Antibiotic over-use continues to be a concern worldwide contributing to antibiotic resistance and other serious morbidities. There is considerable variation in antibiotic use across Neonatal Intensive Care Units (NICU). Antibiotic exposure may alter the neonatal microbiome, increasing the risk of Necrotizing Enterocolitis and other morbidities. Results of published Quality Improvement (QI) projects have demonstrated success with implementation of standardized antibiotic treatment protocols. However, more information is needed to identify additional strategies to safely reduce antibiotic use in the NICU. Aim: Reduce the median Antibiotic Utilization Rate (AUR) in the NICU by 25% from baseline during an 18 month period; the NICU had a baseline median AUR of 21% (1/15-5/16) Design: Quality Improvement (QI) project, as part of a state-wide collaborative, to evaluate the effect of multiple interventions on median AUR over a two-year period. Evidence-based interventions, developed by a multi-disciplinary team, were implemented and evaluated using the Plan Do Study Act (PDSA) methodology; no experimental practices or equipment were involved. Setting: 45-bed NICU with a high-risk perinatal delivery service, neonatal surgery, and specializing in care of the very low birth weight infant Patients: All infants admitted to the NICU at UCI Medical Center from 6/2016-12/2017 that required antibiotics as part of their medical care. Interventions: Standardized Antibiotic time-out (consisting of a review of indications, plan for length of therapy, narrowing therapy) conducted daily on all patients receiving antibiotics, implementation of an Earl Onset Sepsis (EOS) algorithm for asymptomatic infants >35 weeks, standardized initiation and treatment protocol for those infants admitted to the NICU, diagnosis and treatment protocol for Urinary Tract Infections (UTI), and development of an Antibiotic Utilization Review Committee. Measurements: The primary outcome measure was AUR (number of patient days with exposure to antibiotics per 100 patient days). Process outcome measure included compliance with antibiotic time out. Balancing measures include premature discontinuation of antibiotics. Results: A 33% reduction from baseline median AUR; achieved median AUR of 14% post- intervention phase (9/17-12/17). Compliance with daily antibiotic time out was 99%; the primary intervention during antibiotic time out was plan for “auto-stop” and review of indications for therapy. No case of premature discontinuation of antibiotics was observed. Factors that affected success: multidisciplinary teamwork was critical to the success of this QI project along with participation in a state-wide collaborative. Next steps: refine existing protocols and develop new ones focused on diagnosis and treatment of pneumonia, late onset-sepsis, and Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC). Conclusions: multiple interventions resulted in a reduction of median AUR by 33%; the most effective and sustainable intervention was the standardized antibiotic time out.
