N2  - Background: Decision thresholds are critical components of evidence based decision making. Providers assess their patient's disease risk and determine if it exceeds their threshold for additional testing or treatment. Neonatal early onset sepsis (EOS) occurs infrequently but is associated with significant morbidity and mortality. Less than 1 in 1000 newborns have EOS but 15% have blood tests and 5-10% receive antibiotics. The Kaiser-Permanente (KP) calculator, an online decision aid, recommends blood testing for EOS risk > 1 per 1000 and antibiotics for EOS risk > 3 per 1000. These decision thresholds are not empirically derived, not transparent on the calculator, and not adjustable by the user. Additionally, it is not known if providers accurately assess EOS risk or if their decisions are consistent with their intuitive treatment thresholds. Objectives: (1) Identify EOS test and treatment decision thresholds among an international sample of neonatal health providers and (2) Determine if these thresholds are consistent with those used by the KP calculator. (3) Determine if EOS treatment decisions based on clinical history are consistent with selfdescribed treatment thresholds and consistent with KP recommendations. Design/Methods: Electronic survey distributed in the USA, Brazil, and Italy. Respondents (n=988) identified their intuitive EOS treatment threshold (range 1/25 to 1/4000) that would lead them to order a blood culture or start empiric antibiotics, were presented with 4 newborn scenarios with EOS risk ranging from the CDC baseline risk (0.5/1000=RR 1) to the KP treatment threshold (3/1000=RR 6) and were asked if they would order a blood culture and/or start antibiotics. Results: Among respondents, the thresholds for blood testing (1/250 risk of EOS) and antibiotic treatment (1/100 risk of EOS) were significantly higher than the KP calculator thresholds (p < 0.001, Figure 1). At baseline risk (RR=1), treatment decisions were consistent with intuitive thresholds and KP recommendations. At moderate risk (RR=2-4), consistency decreased as respondents ordered antibiotics although the EOS risk was below both their intuitive threshold and KP recommendations. At highest risk (RR=6), less than one-third of respondents ordered antibiotics. By logistic regression, unknown or positive Group B Strep status in the scenario (unknown OR 2.11, positive OR 3.43), the actual EOS risk (OR 1.72), and including RR in the scenario (OR 1.60) predicted treatment decisions, but the provider's intuitive threshold did not (Table 1). Conclusions: Empirically derived test and treatment thresholds are widely distributed. The KP calculator recommends blood culture and antibiotics for newborns with significantly lower EOS risk than would be selected by most providers. Above minimal risk, intuitive EOS treatment decisions are inconsistent with self-identified treatment thresholds. To improve consistency, decision aids for EOS should reffect empirically derived thresholds or be adjustable by users to reffect individual, institutional or national consensus decision thresholds. (Figure Presented).
