N2  - Introduction Abnormalities in surfactant protein synthesis and secretion in the newborn can result in respiratory distress and potentially be fatal. The clinical presentation of surfactant protein deficiencies (SPDs) is variable and a strong suspicion is vital for diagnosis and management. Lung transplantation is a rare but viable therapeutic option for infants with SPD. This case report describes a case of infant lung transplantation in a 9 week-old who presented with neonatal respiratory distress and suspected SPD. Case Description The patient of consanguineous parents was born via caesarian section at term and was proportionally small for gestational age (∼5th percentile). Significant respiratory distress was noted at birth requiring intubation, mechanical ventilation and supplemental oxygen therapy. After failed extubation at day 1 of life, the patient received 3 doses of surfactant through the endotracheal tube with no benefit. At 9 days of life, the patient was extubated to non-invasive ventilation with significant support (i.e. pressures 20/7 cmH2O, FiO2 > 0.45), and continued to have persistent hypercapnia. Dexamethasone, pulse steroids, and hydroxychloroquine were given for suspected diffuse lung disease, and empiric antibiotic therapy started. Lung biopsy performed on day 41 of life, showed abnormal alveolar growth, periodic acid-Schiff positive intra-alveolar material and abnormal lamellar bodies on electron microscopy, compatible with ABCA3 deficiency. The surfactant protein gene sequencing panel (SPGSP) was negative for ABCA3, SFTP-B/C, FOXF1, and CSF2R-A/B. Whole exome sequencing is pending. Subsequently the patient was transferred, listed for lung transplantation and underwent a successful double lung transplantation at 9 weeks of age, with an ABO-compatible donor lung following donation after cardiac death (DCD). Discussion The patient's clinical presentation is suggestive of a surfactant protein deficiency, specifically ABCA3 deficiency based on the lung biopsy results, despite having a negative SPGSP. Given the patient's clinical status, lung transplantation provided definitive management, however the effectiveness of the temporizing measures remains unclear. It is recognized that infant lung transplantation poses unique considerations in comparison to older children and adults (such as immunologic, ethical and social factors), which are described in literature. In this case report, the decision to undergo lung transplantation, despite not having a defined diagnosis, was impart due to the severity of the patient's clinical presentation. Ultimately, this case raises further questions about what factors are important to consider when determining the timing of lung transplantation in SPD.
