N2  - Rationale: The Pediatric Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (pARDS) results from a dysregulated immune response to a variety of lung insults that disrupts lung homeostasis. pARDS is associated with significant morbidity and mortality, and there is no available therapeutic intervention aside from supportive care. To comprehensively characterize the disruption of lung homeostasis that occurs in pARDS, this study examines cell composition, single cell gene expression, T cell receptor (TCR) composition, and microbiologic diversity in tracheal aspirates from infants with pARDS secondary to LRTI. Methods: Patients are enrolled if they meet consensus criteria for pARDS (PALICC 2015), are 0-3 years of age, are intubated with mechanical ventilation, and if the causal etiology is known or suspected LRTI. Tracheal aspirates are collected on day one, four, and seven of mechanical ventilation from pARDS patients. One sample is collected from control patients (also aged 0-3 years) who are intubated and receiving mechanical ventilation for non-pARDS indications. Cellular samples are processed fresh for 3' single cell RNA seq (scRNAseq; 10X genomics). Additional assays performed are flow cytometry, TCR sequencing, and microbiome and virome from frozen cell and supernatant samples. Data are analyzed in R. Results: Four control patients and three pARDS patients have been enrolled thus far. The first pARDS patient was found to have Tropheryma whipplei, Haemophilus influenzae, and Elizabethkingia meningoseptica in high abundance, which varied in subsequent samples in a way that correlated well with antibiotic exposure and clinical course. Flow cytometry demonstrates the predominance of activated neutrophils and more classical than non-classical monocytes in pARDS patients. In contrast, control patients have more nonclassical monocytes in tracheal aspirates. In control and pARDS patients, γδ T cells are present in higher numbers than CD4+ or CD8+ T cells and appear to be the predominant lymphoid cell type. Preliminary scRNA-seq data appears consistent with flow cytometry data, and further analysis is ongoing to investigate differentially expressed genes and pathways. Conclusions: pARDS appears to be driven by a prominent myeloid response, with classical monocytes and neutrophils dominating early in the course of disease. Additionally, γδ T cells are an important component of the lower respiratory tract T cell compartment. Tropheryma whipplei may be an important respiratory pathogen in neonates. Enrollment and sequencing are ongoing with a goal inclusion of 20 pARDS patients and 5 control patients.
