N2  - Purpose of study Maternal chorioamnionitis (MC) is a known risk factor for early onset neonatal sepsis. The AAP currently recommends empiric antibiotic therapy for all neonates exposed to MC. Universal empiric antibiotics may expose low risk neonates to unnecessary antibiotics, alter the development of the microbiome, increase costs, and reduce mother-infant bonding. However, the potential mortality of neonatal sepsis can not be overlooked. The purpose of this study was to develop and implement a simple, risk based algorithm for the management of infants exposed to MC in an attempt to improve antibiotic stewardship. Methods used An algorithm was developed to risk stratify infants exposed to MC. Risk factors included gestational age (GA), duration of rupture of maternal membranes, infant's complete blood count at 6 and 24 hours of life, and clinical status based on 48 hours of observation in the NICU. High risk infants received 36-48 hours of empiric ampicillin and gentamicin. Low risk infants remained in the NICU for observation but did not receive empiric antibiotics. Maternal and neonatal demographic, clinical, and laboratory data was collected and compared to a historical, pre-intervention (PRE) cohort of neonates exposed to MC, all of whom received empiric antibiotic therapy. Summary of results A total of 45 neonates exposed to MC were identified in the PRE group and 26 neonates in the post-intervention period. Infants exposed to MC following algorithm implementation received antibiotics at a lower rate compared to the PRE group (73% vs. 100%, p<0.01). No significant differences were observed between the two groups in GA, birth weight, white blood cell count, immature-to-total neutrophil ratio, absolute neutrophil count, or length of stay. Blood culture results remained negative for all patients in both groups. Conclusions Empiric antibiotic use was reduced in infants exposed to MC following implementation of our risk-based algorithm. All blood cultures remained negative in both groups suggesting empiric therapy based on MC may be unnecessary. Future approaches to the management of infants exposed to MC may include initial observation only followed by antibiotic therapy based on postnatal clinical status potentially improving both antibiotic stewardship and preventing NICU admission for MC alone.
