N2  - Background: This audit was prompted by a lack of consistency when prescribing antimicrobials for UTI's in pregnancy. Urinary tract infections encompassing both cystitis and pyelonephritis are one of the most common presentations in pregnancy. Although a common and often benign complication of pregnancy - UTI's do have to the potential to have adverse outcomes - namely increased risk of preterm birth and delivering a low birth weight infant. NCHD's frequently move training posts. This results in confusion with regards to local guidelines. Therefore, the aim of of this audit was to identify areas of management where improvement could be made so as to reduce these adverse outcomes. Method: Clinical management was compared with the recommendations of RCPI guidelines and the local antimicrobial guidelines. This was a retrospective audit of pregnant inpatients admitted to theMRHMbetween January 1st 2018 and July 31st 2018 with a Urinary tract infection. Data was identified using a coding system. Data was collected from patient case notes using a data collection sheet. The total charts coded as UTI under this period was 60, however one chart could not be located. A further 11 charts were not actually diagnosed as UTI - therefore the final sample number was 48. This time period was chosen as it was consistent with an NCHD training period. Results: •N = 48. •Overall compliance with guidelines was 37.4%. •75% of UTI's were diagnosed using dipstick results and 4.2% diagnosed using MSU results. •Empirical antibiotics were commenced in accordance with local guidelines 36.2% of the time. •Antibiotics were switched according to sensitivities 50% of the time. Conclusions: •There is a lack of adherence to the national and local guidelines in regards to diagnosis and management of UTI's. •Empirical antibiotics as per local guidelines were not commenced in over 60% of cases. •NCHD's should be educated on local guidelines upon commencement of their training post. •MSU samples should be requested, sent and analysed by all physicians who are querying UTI in pregnancy - and therapy should be altered / stopped when sensitivities are returned. •Re-Audit in July 2019 after the above changes are implemented.
