N2  - Background: Hirschsprung's disease associated enterocolitis (HAEC) remains a frequent cause of morbidity and mortality in patients with Hirschsprung's disease (HSCR). This debilitating complication can still develop after surgical resection of the aganglionic segment and preventive therapies are not known. Therefore, the aim of this study was to identify risk factors associated with the development of postoperative HAEC in a cohort of patients with HSCR followed in our center. Methods: A retrospective analysis of 40 cases was performed. Patient related demographic, clinical, laboratory, radiological and surgical data was collected. Conventional descriptive statistics and non-parametric tests were performed for continuous variables. Results are expressed as mean values ± standard deviation (SD) as indicated. Proportions were compared using Chi-squared and Fisher's exact test. Logistic regression analysis was used to identify risk factors predictive of postoperative HAEC. Results: A total of 26 subjects (mean age at presentation, 35.7±147.9, range 1-759 days) had complete information for analysis, 77% percent were male, 54% received antibiotics after birth, 62% had a history of delayed passage of meconium, and 15% presented with HAEC at diagnosis. A genetic syndrome was present in 27% and Down's syndrome was the most common. Leukocytosis with a left shift (58%) and distal intestinal obstruction with proximal dilated bowel loops (42%) were the most frequent laboratory and radiological findings respectively. Short segment HSCR was present in 73%, long segment in 19%, total colonic in 4%, and total colonic with small bowel involvement in 4%. A laparoscopic Swenson endorectal pull-through was the most common surgical approach (54%). In the postoperative period a total of 17 subjects (65%) developed recurrent HAEC (mean number of episodes 3±1.23, range 1-5). There was no significant difference in the mean age at presentation, age at diagnosis, weight at diagnosis or time lapse between diagnosis and initial surgery between subjects with and without a history of postoperative HAEC. Of the measured variables, a history of delayed passage of meconium [OR=16.3 (95% CI: 2.19-121.4; P=0.005)]; nerve trunk hypertrophy on biopsy at diagnosis [OR=8.4 (95% CI: 1.27-55.4; P=0.025)]; development of a postoperative complication [OR=11.4 (95% CI: 1.15-113.1; P=0.024)] and the presence of an anastomotic stricture [OR=1.89 (95% CI:1.21-2.96; P=0.016)] were associated with the development of recurrent postoperative HAEC. The likelihood was lower in subjects who had a genetic syndrome [OR=0.10 (95% CI: 0.02-0.77; P=0.028)]. No association with found based on gender, race, history of prematurity, delivery mode, antibiotic exposure, presenting symptoms, presence of a congenital anomaly, preoperative HAEC, laboratory abnormality, radiologic evidence of obstruction, HSCR type, surgery technique or prior ostomy. Conclusion: In this cohort of subjects with HSCR, recurrent postoperative HAEC was common. Clinical, histological and surgery related complications were risk factors influencing the likelihood of developing HAEC. Larger prospective studies are needed to further identify modifiable factors in order to enhance risk prediction and develop targeted therapies for this complication.
