N2  - Background: Late onset sepsis remains a major cause of mortality and morbidity in preterm neonates. Since 2011, the NICUS Spring Group of NSW and ACT has met to compare practice variations, to review monitoring of neonatal infection, and to ensure quality improvement initiatives are developed, discussed and compared. Methods: Automated surveillance data is provided monthly to all NICUs. This report benchmarks ratios of sepsis/ central lines / antibiotics against risk denominators for babies ≤ 28 weeks gestation during day 3 - 35. Quarterly meetings occur where nursing and medical clinicians compare practices, discuss strategies and share ideas for improvement. These meetings include presentations from each NICU on site specific projects that have been developed and are available to share amongst the group. This study summarises the projects inspired by the SPRING Clinical Practice Improvement Group since its inception. Results: Each NICU has developed and implemented infection prevention strategies. The strategies include education, clinical audit and feedback, creation or update of policies/protocols, changes in clinical practice, antibiotic stewardship, sepsis review meetings, clinical trials and the formation of local infection control QI teams. Consistent across all NICUs are multidiscipilinary sepsis review meetings, clinical audit and feedback. Conclusions: Neonatal infection data review is now integral to all NICU's and has stimulated a variety of QI initiatives across NSW and ACT that are locally led but centrally shared. Momentum for QI projects has been sustained within the group as neonatal clinicians communicate, compare and commit to ongoing improvement.
