N2  - Background: Pediatric patients less than six months of age with structural heart disease are at higher risk for developing necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) than children without heart disease. In contrast to the premature infant population, there are no published guidelines establishing a standard of care for the diagnosis, treatment, and ongoing management of this vulnerable population. Furthermore, we recognized there was much variability in the diagnosis and clinical management of these patients at our center. Thus, we set forth to standardize assessment and treatment of NEC in pediatric cardiac patients. Methods: In May 2016, we put together a multidisciplinary team, including physicians (from cardiology, general surgery, neonatology, and radiology), nurses, nutritionist, pharmacist, physical, and occupational therapist. A clinical practice guideline was developed for the diagnosis, staging, ongoing monitoring, and duration of antibiotic treatment for cardiac NEC. Our guiding tenet was cardiac NEC is not the same process as NEC in a premature neonate, and we wanted to minimize unnecessary testing and treatment in these patients. At the same time, we did not want to underdiagnose and miss a patient who needed treatment. Finally, we sought to minimize NPO time by establishing a tiered enteral feeding plan. The decisions were largely consensus driven. These guidelines were implemented in February 2017. To support our clinical algorithm, an order set was created in our electronic medical record system. Patients who were more than six months of age and/or had a history of NEC as a neonate were excluded from the guidelines. Results: Prior to the initiation of NEC guidelines, there were a total of 26 cases of documented NEC of 1,757 patients from June 2015 to January 2017. Since the guidelines initiation, the incidence of NEC has significantly decreased (from 1.48% to 0.52%, P value =.005). There have been three cases of confirmed NEC of 579 patients since the implementation (from February to August 2017). Analysis supports that there is a significant decreasing trend, and the trend is not due to season-ality. Furthermore, we did not make any changes to our general feeding protocols or initiate any new NEC prevention strategies during the study period. Thus, this decrease in incidence is likely due to having a standard definition for the diagnosis and staging of NEC. Consequently, antibiotic usage is decreased with more consistent reinitiation of enteral feeding. Further-more, we have not seen any adverse outcomes (need for surgical intervention and death) attributable to the guideline (such as failure to diagnose or inappropriate treatment). Conclusions: Since the implementation of our cardiac NEC guidelines, we have seen a significant decrease in the diagnosis of NEC. Further analysis will look at the impact of this quality initiative on resource utilization, such as duration of noninva-sive or mechanical ventilation, rate of bloodstream infection and sepsis, number of central line days, initiation of enteral feeding, and cardiac ICU/hospital length of stay. Establishing a consistent definition for cardiac NEC and subsequent treatment regimen has enabled us to provide safer and more effective care for this fragile patient population.
