N2  - Introduction: Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) is a rare life-threatening disease characterized by uncontrollable hypercytokinemia and sometime caused by neonatal sys-temic herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection. It occurs most often in the early neonatal period and rapidly progresses to multiple organ failure (MOF) or disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). We report two neonates with systemic HSV-associated HLH (HSV-HLH). Case: Patients 1 and 2 became febrile and were hospitalized on day of life (DOL) 6 and 4, respectively. Despite the use of empiric antibiotic therapies, liver dysfunction and DIC progressed. After acyclovir administration on DOL 9 and 7, the newborns were transferred to our hospital. Peripheral blood (PB) tests revealed pancytopenia and hyperferritinemia, and bone marrow studies indicated hemophagocytosis. The copy numbers of HSV-DNA in the PB were extremely high (patient 1: 8×108 copies/mL; patient 2: 2×108 copies/mL). Neonatal systemic HSV-HLH was diagnosed. The patients were treated with high-dose acyclovir, Γ-globulin, steroids, cyclosporine, and plasma exchange in the pediatric intensive care unit. Despite a decrease in the HSV-DNA copy number, patient 1 died of MOF on DOL 19. Patient 2 achieved complete remission without complications, including those in the central nerve system. Conclusion: Early diagnosis and treatment with high-dose acyclovir and immune regulation therapies are crucial for reducing mortality. No established treatment strategy exists for neonatal systemic HSV-HLH; therefore, further studies are needed.
