N2  - Background and Objectives: Candidemia is emerging as an important cause of neonatal sepsis in Pakistan. With increasing number of preterm and low birth weight babies managed in Neonatal Care Units, use of central lines and other devices has become common. Thus the babies are more likely to succumb to endogenous and exogenous sources of infections, especially candidemia which is not routinely covered by empirical antibiotic therapy. We describe here the spectrum and susceptibility pattern of Candida species isolated from blood cultures sent to the Aga Khan University Clinical Microbiology laboratory from 2014 to 2016. Methodology: Laboratory data for neonatal blood cultures (Day0-30) yielding Candida spp. from Jan 2014 to Dec 2016 was extracted and reviewed. A total of 304 blood cultures from 289 neonates yielded 326 Candida isolates, 22 cultures having 2 or more Candida spp. The samples were received from all over Pakistan through a network of more than 200 sample collection points, transported to and processed at a single site at the Section of Microbiology, AKUH, Karachi. Candida spp. were identified using API 20C AUX (BioMérieux, France), pseudohyphae formation, urease production and growth characteristics on BiGGY and Mycosel agar. In most cases, Antifungal susceptibility testing was performed by disc diffusion method according to CLSI M27-A3. MICs were determined using YeastOne Sensititre plates where disc diffusion interpretative criteria could not be applied. Descriptive statistics were computed using Microsoft Excel®. Results: Of the 304 cultures with Candida species, 71 (23%) were from the first 72 hours of life, suggesting candidemia is not restricted to late neonatal sepsis. The yield of Candida spp. increased four-fold over the 3 years, from 48 in 2014 to 200 in 2016. C. tropicalis (35%) was the most common specie seen, followed by C. parapsilosis (16%), C. albicans and C. pelliculosa (both 13.5%). C. pelliculosa, C. lusitaniae and C. guilliermondii, found mostly on skin and hands, were seen more often in early (first 3 days of life) candidemia than other species. Fluconazole susceptibility was 97.5%, and no acquired resistance was seen. Fluconazole resistance species (C. krusei and C. glabrata) were extremely rare (6/326). Of note, no C. auris were isolated from neonates, although 75 strains of the specie were identified from different clinical specimens from adults during the same time period. Conclusion: The species causing early neonatal candidemia suggest exogenous source of acquisition. In light of the high fluconazole susceptibility, we recommend the use of fluconazole as empiric therapy when suspecting candidemia in neonates.
