N2  - Objective: Children with cystic fibrosis (CF) are susceptible to chronic pulmonary infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa (PA), and consequent poor lung function and mortality. Recent change in practice with early antibiotic treatment at the first positive PA culture has been shown to eradicate PA and improve lung function. Additionally, adoption of newborn screening for CF allows for earlier detection and treatment of PA. The objectives of this study were to quantify the impact of routine early PA eradication and early CF diagnosis on the risk of chronic PA infection. Method: This retrospective cohort study compared “early eradicators,” treated with inhaled tobramycin (80 mg/2 mL twice daily) or tobramycin inhalation solution (300 mg/5 mL twice daily) at the first sign of PA infection, with historical controls. Lung function at age seven (% predicted FEV1) was also compared between cohorts. The probability of developing a chronic PA infection as a function of age (0-18 years) was estimated using Kaplan-Meier curves. The risk of developing chronic PA function was assessed using multivariable Cox proportional hazards modeling with adjustment for CF genotype, gender, and early CF diagnosis. Results: Of 148 CF patients followed from 1995-2015, 121 met study inclusion criteria. A smaller proportion of early eradicators developed chronic PA infection (24% versus 78%; p<0.001) or mucoid PA (5.3% versus 59%; p<0.001) compared to controls. From Kaplan-Meier curves, the probability distributions between “early eradicators” (median age=11.6 years) and controls (median=3.6 years) significantly differed across the assessed age range (log rank test, p<0.001) (Figure). Multivariable Cox regression revealed a significantly greater risk of chronic PA infection among controls compared to “early eradicators” (adjusted hazards ratio = 2.9 [95%CI: 1.5, 5.8], p=0.002). There was no difference in lung function at age 7 between cohorts and early diagnosis did not impact risk of chronic PA infection. Conclusions: This study suggests that early eradication of PA protects against the development of chronic PA. Future monitoring of both study cohorts over a longer duration would allow characterization of survival benefit in patients with CF. (Figure Presented).
