N2  - BACKGROUND-AIM Neonatal sepsis (NNS) is the most common cause of morbidity and mortality in the neonatal period. The clinical picture of sepsis is variable in newborn infants. The diagnosis of NNS is difficult due to the clinical signs often overlapping with non-infectious causes of systemic inflammation. Currently there is no single reliable test for an early definitive diagnosis of NNS. There is need to establish a reliable test that will differentiate between sepsis and non-infectious conditions to reduce unnecessary antibiotic use. The objective of this study; to determine levels of procalcitonin, among infants < 10 days with clinical diagnosis of sepsis, over a six months period at the Nairobi Hospital METHODS Retrospective evaluation of data on infants admitted with a clinical diagnosis of NNS beginning 1st March, 2015 to 30th September, 2015. : Data abstraction was performed using a predefined chart. All children with a diagnosis of neonatal sepsis admitted to NICU with at least 1 level of procalcitonin, blood culture results, full blood count and received antibiotic treatment were included. Levels of procalcitonin were determined among children with positive blood cultures and those with negative blood cultures. Risk factors of NNS were compared among children with high and those with low levels of procalcitonin. RESULTS A total of 348 blood samples from 231 babies in TNH were tested for PCT from 1st March, 2015 and 30th September, 2015; 103 were females (44.6%). PCT values ranged from (0.06 - 252.1) ng/ml; 185 results were > 0.5ng/ml. 49 babies had two PCT levels measured and 26 had more than two; 61 showed a reduction to < 0.5 ng/ml after treatment and 14 showed either no change or even an increase in PCT value. Normal circulating PCT levels should be < 0.05 ng/ml however treatment starts when the values is > 0.5 ng/ml. Out of 348 blood samples 127 (36%) had blood cultures of which 116 (91.3%) were negative after 5 days incubation and 11 (8.7%) had positive cultures. Organisms isolated included Staphylococcus aureus (1), Staphylococcus epidermidis (5), Streptococcus pneumoniae (1), Enterococcus faecalis (1), Klebsiella pneumoniae (2), MDR Acinetobacter baumanii(1); 2 isolates were methiciln resistant. These had varying PCT results however it should be noted that the highest levels of PCT were from this group of positive bacterial cultures. CONCLUSIONS Serum levels of procalcitonin may contribute to the diagnosis of NNS. In addition, it may be used as adjunct information to guide antibiotic use in NNS. More studies are warranted to determine the correlation of procalcitonin with NNS and its value in stopping antibiotic use once clinical signs and symptoms have settled.
