N2  - BACKGROUND-AIM Neonatal sepsis refers to a bacterial blood stream infection in the first month of life, such as meningitis, pneumonia, pyelonephritis, or gastroenteritis, but neonatal sepsis can also be due to infection with fungi, viruses, or parasites. Neonatal sepsis can be difficult to diagnose as newborns may be asymptomatic. If a newborn shows signs and symptoms suggestive of sepsis, antibiotics are immediately started and are either changed to target a specific organism identified by diagnostic testing or discontinued after an infectious cause for the symptoms has been ruled out. Blood cultures are recommended preferably before antibiotics are started. This study was conducted to determine whether the use of blood culture as a diagnostic marker for detection of neonatal sepsis was appropriate as well as getting a clear picture of how it presents in neonates that have been found non-septic and clinically well. METHODS Seventy two (72) neonates suspected of being septic ( 49 males and 23 females) and fifty-two (52) apparently healthy neonates (35 males, 17 females) were recruited as test and control subjects respectively using the same diagnostic criteria. Two millimeters of venous blood was taken by venipuncture and innoculated into blood culture bottle containing thioglycollate broth and the bottle was placed into a blood culture machine, which incubates the specimens at body temperature. The blood culture instrument reports positive blood cultures. A Gram stain was performed on the blood for a rapid, general identification of the bacteria. The blood was also subcultured or “subbed” onto agar plates to isolate the pathogenic organism for culture and susceptibility testing, which identified the species of bacteria. Antibiotic sensitivities were then assessed on the bacterial isolate. RESULTS 23 test subjects (65% males and 35% females) and 12 control subjects (67% males and 33% females) had positive blood culture. 49 test subjects ( 71% males and 29% females) and 40 control subjects (68% males and 32% females) had negative blood culture results. Sensitivity was 32% while specificity was 77%. False positive (FP) was 23% while false negative (FN) was 68%. Positive predictive value (PPV) was 66% while negative predictive value (NPV) was 45%. Relative risk (RR) was 1 while attributable risk (AR) was 11. Odds ratio (OR) was 2. Six (6) organisms were isolated; three (3) gram positive cocci, two (2) gram negative bacilli and one (1) yeast cell. The organisms are: Staphylococcus spp (30% for test and 25% for control), Entorococcus spp ( 9% for both test and control), Streptococcus spp (9% for test and 0% for control), coliform spp (13% for test and 58% for control), Klebsiella spp ( 17% for test and 8% for control), Candida albicans (17% for test and 8% for control). Sensitivity pattern showed that the drugs that were sensitive to all isolates were: Augmentin, Cefuroxime, Gentamycin, Ceftriaxone, Amoxyllin and Tetracycline. Resistant drugs were Cotrimoxazole, Ceftazidine, Nitrofurantoin and Cephalexin. CONCLUSIONS This study has shown that even apparently healthy neonates enrolled as control subjects were not actually healthy and some of them may present with late onset sepsis which can become difficult and complicated for the attending clinician. Thus the use of blood culture as a diagnostic tool for the detection of neonatal sepsis should be used in conjunction with other established biomarkers of sepsis. The sole use of blood cultures to diagnose neonatal infection has a number of limitations. It may take 24 to 72 hours to obtain culture results. The sensitivity of blood cultures might be impaired by exposure to intrapartum antibiotics, which are administered to 15% to 40% of mothers in labor. Intrapartum antibiotic exposure can result in a partially treated infant, delaying the onset of clinical signs and symptoms of infection and further complicating the expedient definitive diagnosis of early-onset sepsis in the infant. With the development of multiple drug-resistant bacteria and the cost of therapy with multiple antibiotics, the ability to diagnose or rule out sepsis is an essential tool to limit inappropriate antibiotic exposure.
