N2  - Background: Neonatal sepsis is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality among neonates. As signs of sepsis are non-specific, there is a low threshold for commencing intravenous antibiotics. Antibiotics are usually ceased at 48-72 h if blood culture remains negative. Recent studies have reported on adverse outcomes in newborns exposed to antibiotics. Our study looked at time taken to identify a positive culture following inoculation. Method: Retrospective cohort study of blood culture results and the time of reporting in newborns admitted with suspected neonatal sepsis over a 12-month period. The blood is inoculated into BD BACTEC Peds Plus/F bottles, which are continuously monitored in the BD BACTEC FX blood culture system for 5 days. Patient data were collected from electronic record system and the clinical notes. Results: Of the 178 neonates analysed, 48 (27%) were preterm (<37 weeks) and 31 (17.5%) were LBW (<2.5 kg). Commonest reason for commencing antibiotics was respiratory distress in the presence of maternal risk factors. Symptoms were persisting after 48 h in 55 (31%) of these neonates. Of the 178 babies, 124 (70%) completed 48 h of IV antibiotics, 28 (16%) received more than 72 h, 4 (2%) for less than 48 h and data were unavailable in 21 (12%). Only 3 (1.7%) had a positive culture and all were reported within 24 h of incubation. Conclusions: Positive blood culture results were available within 24 h. Prospective studies confirming this may have major implication on duration of admission and antibiotic exposure in suspected neonatal sepsis.
