N2  - Neonatal infection is a frequent complication in neonatal intensive care unit attending patients who require lengthy hospitalization and frequent invasive techniques. It has been previously demonstrated that relying on symptoms alone may not be sufficient in diagnosing neonatal infections. Serial determination of biomarkers (C-reactive protein, procalcitonin.) may be useful adjunctively in the diagnosis and management of neonatal infection but the gold standard is the blood culture. Because of the mortality and morbidity related to neonatal infections many clinicians empirically start broad spectrum antimicrobial regimen for infants considered at risk for sepsis while waiting for the blood culture isolates, but antibiotics are occasionally continued despite a negative blood culture. This can increase the pressure for selecting multidrug-resistant organisms. It's very important to know the infectious epidemiology of the neonatal unit in other to choose the best antibiotic empirical treatment. This's a retrospective study made in a tertiary neonatal intensive care unit. All patients admitted to NICUs between the 1st of January of 2003 till 31st December of 2013 were observed. Hospital Castrillo group's criteria were used as the standard definition for early-onset sepsis, late-onset sepsis and clinical sepsis. A total of 4343 neonates (41,7 % female) were admitted during the surveillance period. A total of 427 infections were diagnoses: 65(15%) early-onset sepsis and 362 (85%) late-onset sepsis. Overall incidence of infection was 9,8 %: the incidence of early-onset was 1 % and for late-onset was 8 %. EGB was the most common agents of early-onset sepsis among the Gram positives organisms and E. coli among the Gram negatives. Klebsiella sp. was the predominant cause of late-onset sepsis among the Gram negatives and Staphylococcus sp. in the group of Gram positives. Overall incidence of mortality because of infection was 8 % (13/165) with higher mortality among the group with fungical and Gram negatives infections. In 2008 the spectrum of organisms that cause neonatal infections changes: till 2008 Gram negatives were the predominant cause, after 2008 the most frequent were Gram positives. The empirical treatment used was ampicilin plus gentamicin for early onset infection and vancomycin plus cefotaxime for the late onset sepsis. The knowledge of the epidemiology infection of the unit is an useful arm to correctly treat such an important pathology. Early diagnosis and therapy optimize patient outcomes. If you also chose the right empirically antibiotic treatment, your possibilities of success are higher.
