N2  - BACKGROUND: Antibiotic exposure alters the pattern of microbial colonization of neonatal intestine and affects neutrophil homeostasis in the neonates. Neutrophil infiltration into the lung is an important component of innate immune response to pneumonia, an important cause of mortality in the neonates. However, the role of commensal bacteria in regulating lung innate immune response in neonatal mice is unclear. METHODS: We exposed pregnant dams to ampicillin and gentamicin or no antibiotics (control) in drinking water beginning 5 days before delivery and challenged their neonates at PN 3d with Escherichia coli (E. coli) K1 (104 CFU/g) or PBS via intratracheal route and measured the number of neutrophils, levels of tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α, interleukin (IL)-1β, IL-8 and macrophage inflammatory protein (MIP)-1a in the brochoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid, bacterial load and the number of IL17 producing cells in neonatal lungs and determined composition of intestinal commensal bacteria in antibiotic-exposed neonates (PN 3d) by sequencing of microbial 16s ribosomal DNA. We transferred the intestinal contents from no antibiotic-exposed (control) mice (PN 3d) to age-matched antibiotic-exposed mice by gavage, to reconstitute the commensal bacteria in antibiotic-exposed mice. We interrogated the role of IL17 or toll like receptor (TLR) 4 by using gene targeted neonatal mice (PN 3d) (Il17ra-/- or Tlr4-/-) or by using anti-IL17A or anti-TLR4 neutralizing antibody. RESULTS: Perinatal antibiotic exposure altered the pattern of microbial colonization, abolished the early appearance of Gammaproteobacteria in intestine, decreased the number of neutrophils, decreased the levels of TNFa, IL1b, IL8 and MIP1a in BAL fluid, increased the bacterial load in the lungs and increased the susceptibility of neonatal mice to E. coli pneumonia as compared to controls (median survival 15h > 48h). These changes were associated with reduced number of IL17 producing cells in the lungs. Transfer of normal intestinal commensal bacteria restored the number of neutrophils, TNFa, ILb, IL8, MIP1a in the BAL fluid and restored the susceptibility of neonatal mice to E. coli pneumonia. The number of neutrophils was significantly lower in neonatal Il17ra-/- or Tlr4-/- mice. Pretreatment of antibiotic-exposed mice (PN 3d) with anti-IL17A or anti-TLR4 neutralizing antibody blocked increase in host resistance to E. Coli pneumonia in antibiotic-exposed neonatal mice after transfer of normal commensal bacteria. CONCLUSIONS: Commensal bacteria are critical in regulating lung innate immune response and host resistance to E. coli pneumonia via TLR4, IL17A-dependent mechanisms raising the possibility that therapies to ameliorate antibiotic-induced intestinal dysbiosis will improve neonatal mortality.
