N2  - Background: The utility of surveillance swabs in the neonatal unit is uncertain, with recent studies finding the sensitivity and specificity of surveillance swabs to predict sepsis to be suboptimal. We conducted a retrospective cohort study to determine if flora on surveillance swabs reflected infecting organisms in NICU patients. Method: Babies admitted to the neonatal unit at RPAH with ear and/or nose swabs taken between 1/1/2007 and 30/6/2013 were included. We identified babies with a positive blood culture with a potential pathogen other than coagulase negative Staphylococci (CNS). Swabs taken before or on the same day of positive blood cultures were included in analysis. Results: A total of 3237 swabs were collected from 1723 babies (44% of admissions). Potential pathogens were found in 21% babies who had swabs. Blood cultures were positive in 0.7% of admitted infants. In 5 of 6 cases of early onset sepsis, invasive strains and ear strains were phenotypically identical. Amongst babies with late onset sepsis and a preceding nose swab, there was a 64% chance that the invasive organism had been previously detected with a screening swab. All pathogens causing late onset sepsis were covered by the unit's empiric antibiotic policy. Conclusions: In our cohort, ear swabs were useful for guiding management of EOS. In contrast, screening nose swabs poorly predicted the infecting organism in a septic baby. There may be utility of more targeted screening for infection prevention and control.
