N2  - Background: Histological horioamnionitis (HCA) complicates up to 50% of preterm births and is associated with significant immediate and longerterm outcomes in infants. We previously reported that HCA modulates the risk of late-onset neonatal sepsis, presumably through immune-modifying effects. The effects of HCA on childhood infection risk are unknown. Methods: HCA was diagnosed by standardized semi-quantitative histology in 1218 preterm infants and total population data on infection-related hospitalisations and mortality were linked. Potential effects of HCA on re-hospitalisations with infection diagnoses were evaluated using Cox proportional hazards regression models, with adjustment for gestational age, birth weight z-score, chronic lung disease, neonatal infection, neonatal antibiotic exposure, and age at NICU discharge. Results: HCA was identified in 577 of 1218 (47.8%) of infants (median gestational age 27 weeks, IQR 27.4-28.6). Of the 1088 infants alive at NICU discharge, two thirds were re-hospitalised at least once with an infection before the age of 24 months. Lower (26.4%) and upper (19.6%) respiratory tract, invasive bacterial (9.8%) and gastrointestinal (7.4%) infections predominated. In fully adjusted analyses, HCA was associated with a 32% increased risk of lower respiratory tract infection (hazard ratio 1.32, 95% CI 1.02-1.7, P = 0.033). No relationships between HCA and re-hospitalizations for not otherwise specified infection diagnoses were observed. Conclusions: HCA is associated with increased risk of lower respiratory tract infection, independent of chronic lung disease. Perinatal inflammation may result in subtle structural and immunological abnormalities to the preterm lung that predispose to more severe respiratory viral infections in early life.
