N2  - Introduction/background In the United States alone, adverse drug events are the sixth leading cause of death. It has been demonstrated that children are three times more likely to experience an adverse drug event than adults. Children who experience an adverse drug reaction are 18 times more likely to die than those who do not. It has been documented that children are at a higher risk of medication errors than adults as the dosing is mainly weight-based. Methods A retrospective study was done by the pharmacist, looking back over the rounds that have been performed over the period of a year. The rounds are generally performed at least three times a week in the neonatal ICU, paediatric ward and paediatric ICU. On average, 30 patients are seen in the paediatric unit and 5 patients in the NNICU per week and take approximately 1h30min to complete. The rounds focus on antibiotic principals, namely; prolonged hang time, treatment duration ( >7 days and >14 days) as well as treatment duplication. Results Ward rounds have allowed for the monitoring of at least 35 patients per week. Interventions made thus far include: prolonged hang time (>1hour), treatment duration and duplication. Of these interventions 19 interventions made thus far in the neonatal ICU and 10 of those were accepted. Conclusion The number of interventions reflected above may be seen as few, however many positives can be taken, such as, the number of interventions that were accepted, given the lack of studies and limited knowledge of drugs used in these populations groups as well as the long term effects. As much as this experience has been a steep learning curve, it has also been a welcomed challenge that has inspired the ward pharmacist to take a more active role as a part of the paediatric health care team.
