N2  - Study Objective: Sepsis is a leading cause of neonatal mortality. Emergency department (ED) early goal-directed therapy with early antibiotics and fluid resuscitation improves outcomes. Delayed intravenous (IV) access is a barrier to rapid resuscitation in neonates. The aim of this project was to identify effective interventions to ameliorate significant first-hour delays to IV access for ED neonatal severe sepsis resuscitation Methods: Cross-sectional, retrospective chart review comparing two groups of neonates < three months old with two or more Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome criteria and a suspected source transferred from a Level 1 academic ED to a pediatric specialty hospital over a period of 28 months. Group A (pre-intervention, n=18) included transfers from December 2011 through December 2012. Group B (post-intervention, n=24) included transfers from January 2013 to March 2014. The following interventions were implemented: 1. Implementing a formal algorithm with an escalation strategy for rapidly adopting sequential IV access strategies 2. Establishing a formal page-alert system to enlist the help of neonatal intensive care unit nursing staff 3. Hiring pediatric ED-trained RNs and training existing staff in pediatric IV placement 4. Utilizing ultrasound technology and neonate-specific catheters 5. Providing ad hoc feedback to ED providers involved in the care of neonates with suspected sepsis. These interventions were introduced starting in January 2013. Primary outcome was time from door to IV access. Secondary outcomes included blood cultures, lab work, and administration of IV fluids and antibiotics. Results: 55.6% of the pre-intervention group had IV access obtained within one hour (n=10) versus 85.5% (n=20) of neonates in the post-intervention group, effect size 29%, (95% CI 5 - 57%, P < 0.02). In the pre-intervention group, 70% ( n=7) received IVF bolus versus 70% (n=14) in the post-intervention group, effect size 0%, (95% CI -29 - 34%). In the pre-intervention group, 60% (n=6) received antibiotics versus 85% (n=17) in the post-intervention group, effect size 25%, (95% CI -6 - 55%). In the pre-intervention group, 100% (n=10) received labs and blood work versus 90% (n=18) in the post-intervention group, effect size 10%, (95% CI -18 - 30%). Discussion: This intervention improved the percentage of neonates who received IV access in an hour or less by 29%, which was statistically significant. There were no measured differences between groups in any other secondary outcome. These interventions in the ED may help to decrease time to key clinical milestones in neonatal severe sepsis resuscitation.
