N2  - Rationale: The BRC-NP is a serious complication with high mortality in hospitalized patients. Objectives: To describe the prognosis of the BRC-NP and medium-term impact of multidisciplinary prevention strategy. Prevention program: hand hygiene campaign began in 2007, implementation of the Zero Bacteremia Project in ICUs in 2008, educational program for the prevention of peripheral BRC and design by the Clinical Nutrition Committee of a poster with the critical points of the central venous catheter care protocol in 2011. Methods: Prospectively all patients with BRC were included between 2007 and 2012. Factors associated with early death (1st week) were analyzed using logistic regression. Results: BRC: 847, 291 (34%) were identified BRC-NP (surgical units in 54%, medical units in 29% and 17% in ICU). A 14.5% of patients died during the first month and 8% in the first week. On multivariate analysis, predictors of early death factors were multiresistant germs infection (OR 4.14, 95% CI 1.24 13.76, p=0.0205) and age (OR 1.11, 95% CI: 1.05 1.17, p= 0.0001) Were protective factors for mortality catheter removal in the first 48 hours (OR 0.19, 95%CI 0.07 to 0.54), p= 0.0019) and appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment (OR 0.18, 95%CI 0.06 to 0.55, p = 0.0025) The incidence of BRC-PN decreased sequentially along the prevention program from 5.36 episodes/1000 catheter days in 2007 to 2.9 episodes/1000 catheter days in 2012. Conclusion: Implementing a multidisciplinary prevention strategy significantly reduces the incidence of BRC-NP. Early catheter removal and appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment can reduce the mortality of this serious complication.
