N2  - Background and aims: Sternal wound infection is a known complication after pediatric cardiac surgery. Treatment included wound revision, debridement, prolonged antibiotic treatment and muscle flap closure, requiring long hospitalization and morbidity Vacuum assisted closure has increased use for wound infection, mainly in adults. Aims: Our unit operates 500 cases annually, with 2% wound infection, since 2003 VAC was used to treat sternal wound infection. Methods: All children (ages 0-18 years) that developed sternal wound infection were included. Results: 46 children were treated (2003-2013). Ages 2 weeks to 14 years (average 23 months, median 8 months), with average weight 8.7 Kg (3-27). Majority was closed in OR(37, 80%), 3 required re-sternotomy, 9 had delayed sternal closure (up to 10 days). Infection appeared after 13 days (3-100, median 9). The main infective cause was Staph Aureus (MSSA) (30, 65%), 4 had combined infection and 1 had negative culture. VAC was applied 16 days post-op (4-104), for 10 days 1-25. The majority of cases were uneventful, 2 cases were stopped due to contact dermatitis or minor hemorrhage. No mortality or hemodynamic compromise. Initial cases (2003-2004) were removed surgically with Pectoralis flap, all cases since 2005 were removed bed side with primary closure or in OR with secondary closure. Conclusions: VAC is an efficient and safe way to treat deep sternal wound infection after pediatric cardiac surgery, without major complication or hemodynamic instability. This system can be safely used with neonates or low weight infants Using this method re-operation for debridement and flap can be avoided.
