N2  - Background: Antibiotics are commonly used in neonatal patients. Neonatal nosocomial infection (NNI) is a serious and frequent problem encountered by preterm infants, associated with increased morbidity and mortality. Prolonged exposure to antibiotics is associated with increased morbidity including NNI and NEC. We aimed to document the rate of antibiotic usage in our neonatal unit (NCCU), and compare antibiotic exposure to the rate of NNI. Methods: Data on antibiotic use was extracted for infants born at less than 29 weeks gestation within the first 35 days of life for a 7 year period (2006 through 2012). A novel statistic of antibiotic use “antibiotic density” was calculated as the ratio of antibiotic days to days of admission. Episodes of NNI were identified as part of the ongoing NNI surveillance within NCCU and compared to the rate of antibiotic use. Results: 1039 infants were studied. Increased antibiotic density was demonstrated with reducing GA (p < 0.001). After standardising for GA a reduction in antibiotic density was documented over the 7 year period (p < 0.01). The standardised rate of NNI trended down during our study period. A ratio of approximately 1 NNI to 50 antibiotic days remained constant. Conclusion: We described a measure of antibiotic usage, “antibiotic density” in a premature population. Application of this statistic will permit interunit comparisons of antibiotic use and further investigation of the relationships between antibiotic use and NNI. Antibiotic density was reduced with increased gestational age and over the years studied. The rate of NNI also reduced over time.
