N2  - Background: There is limited data on the long term neurodevelopmental outcomes of neonatal enteroviral meningitis. Method: Retrospective review of all cases of neonatal enteroviral meningitis (June 2002-July 2012). Results: 37.6% of the neonates with enteroviral infections (41/109) had meningitis. 31 had a positive CSF enteroviral PCR, 10 had clinical features of meningitis in the presence of systemic enteroviral infection. 29% (12/41) were preterm. Fever with lethargy (19/41) was the most common presenting symptom. 6 neonates had seizures requiring anti convulsants. Short duration of antibiotic treatment and hospital stay indicated brief illness. There were no deaths. Complications occurred in six (encephalitis, hepatitis, coagulopathy, myocarditis) with encephalitis being the commonest (14% = 6/41). One year follow up data was available in 30 (73%) . Median weight, length and head circumference at 1 year were in the 72nd, 65th and 84th centile respectively. On the Griffiths mental developmental scales, median General quotient at 1 year of age was 97(IQR 91-102). The neurological recovery was complete for all neonates but one who had diplegic CP and progressive hydrocephalus requiring Ventriculoperitoneal shunt at 1 year. Conclusions: Neonates with enteroviral meningitis have brief illness and relatively less morbidity. At 1 year of age, they appeared to have optimal growth and good neurodevelopmental outcomes.
