N2  - Introduction: Legionella pneumophila is a common cause of communityacquired pneumonia and can be associated with multiorgan system dysfunction. Age-appropriate empiric antibiotics for community-acquired pneumonia are often started prior to the diagnosis, but due to the fastidious nature of Legionella, the etiology is often not discerned until well into the initial therapy. We describe two atypical clinical presentations of Legionella pneumonia in which the resultant ARDS was treated with ECLS (Extracorporeal Life Support) at our institution. The first case involved a 6 day old neonate with pneumonia and sepsis following an underwater birth. The second case consisted of a young adult immunocompromised woman with environmental exposure who required transfer to a pediatric ECLS center for management of fulminant ARDS. Neither case improved with the escalation of support to high frequency oscillator ventilation. Both required support with veno-venous ECLS for approximately one week. Bronchoscopy, which was ultimately diagnostic for the young woman, was facilitated by stabilization on VV ECLS. Both patients survived with excellent outcomes. Discussion: Whereas escalating mechanical ventilatory support is often required to sustain sufficient gas exchange, with the reversibility of the underlying illness and the duration of treatment frequently unknown, VV ECLS provides a critical period of respite for pulmonary failure. We believe that these cases highlight 1) the benefits of using ECLS as a life saving therapy to minimize ventilator induced lung injury and optimize outcomes for severe pneumonia and 2) the use of experienced pediatric ECLS centers to support young adults with highly treatable and reversible causes of severe hypoxemic respiratory failure when adult ECLS centers are not available. When the diagnosis is uncertain in these young adults, ECLS may provide an opportunity for further diagnostic workup and evaluation, which could improve mortality.
