N2  - Background: Gastrointestinal (GI) tract contains the largest lymphoid organ to react with pathogenic micro-organisms. Patients with primary immunodeficiency diseases (PIDs) have immune impairment in GI tract and suffer from refractory diarrhea or colitis. Methods: Those patients with diarrhea who had limited effect after convention treatment of non-peros (NPO), hydration and empiric antibiotics over 3 months were defined as refractory colitis. Metabolic disorders have been excluded by clinical course and pathology. Patients with refractory colitis were suspected of immune disorders and investigated in PICAR Institute. After IRB proven, functional assessments including lymphocyte proliferation, superoxide (H2O2) production and IL-10 signaling were evaluated. Candidate genes responsible for these defective functions including IL2RG, ZAP70, CD3 gamma, ORAI-1, ITK, NCF2/RAC2, NCF4, IL10, IL10RA, IL10RB, and NEMO, STAT1, FOXP3 and STAT3 were analyzed. Results: Five male patients (onset age between neonatal stage to 11 years old) with refractory colitis received functional assessments. All had normal superoxide production and IL-10 signaling. Among two patients with profound defective lymphocyte proliferation, one with a splicing mutation of XIAP gene [Int5 (+1) G>A] experienced hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and catastrophic mediastinumitis before suitable hematopoietic stem cell available (HSCT. In the other with recurrent opportunistic infection as well as refractory colitis, successful HSCT cured his colitis. In another three patients, one with missense mutation of FOXP3 gene [Met370Leu] and two with missense and splicing mutstions of STAT3 gene [Gln469Arg and IVS 10 (-2 A>G)] were reminded of maternal uncle mortality from diabetes complication in toddlers and distinct features of Hyper IgE recurrent infection syndromes (HIES), respectively. Conclusion(s): Patients with T cell defects, including interrupting Treg and Th17 development and defective anti-apoptosis signaling by FOXP3, STAT3 and XIAP mutations; respectively, indeed presented as refractory colitis. The awareness of such PID patients is obligated to be aroused for early effective intervention.
