N2  - Attention has recently been directed to the composition of the gastrointestinal microbiome and its critical role in nutrition, metabolic and immune function in infants, young children and adults (1). Intestinal dysfunction and frequent exposure to antibiotics in patients with CF results in an evolving dysbiosis of the GI microsystem (2). Further, mutations in CFTR alter the airway and intestinal microenvironment along with the colonization of miroorganisms in patients with CF (3). This dysbiosis and abnormal colonization contributes to undernutrition, growth failure and long term morbidity and mortality in children and adults with CF (4). Understanding the complex interactions between the CFTR mutations, microbial colonization and mucosal immunity is of major public health importance to inform novel treatment strategies (such as restoring a healthier microbiome) to improve nutritional status and decrease morbidity and mortality in CF. The exposome and the developing intestinal microbiome The microbiome is established within the first 3 years of life and remains relatively stable throughout life (5). The developing microbiome is impacted by mode of delivery, gestational age, infant diet, hospitalizations and medications, particularly antibiotics (6). The greatest intraand inter-personal variation in microbial communities occurs during this time period, potentially reflecting the differential development of the microbiome in relation to environmental factors, many of which can be altered (7). The intestinal microbiome and immunity Several seminal studies in germ free animals demonstrate that absence of microbial colonization results in altered gut epithelialization, growth, and immune function (8). The establishment of symbiotic bacteria can act as a central stimulus for maturation of the immune system and may alter risk of disease manifestation (9). Targeted investigation of neonatal microbial colonization patterns with Bifidobacterium found associations between enhanced maturation of protective mucosal immunoglobulins, and early intense colonization with Bacteroides fragilis which might decrease immune responsiveness in infancy (10). Interestingly, specific bacteria have been associated with early onset allergy and atopy, particularly an increase in Clostridia and a decrease in Bifidobacteria (11). Lack of microbial diversity in the first weeks of life is related to risk of allergy and atopy in infants and school age children (12). Correspondingly, gut microbes influence the maturation of T helper cells (Th1) immune responses, Cd4+ T-cell phenotype, Th1/Th2/Th17 development and activity, and regulatory T cell function (13). Th17 cells are found in abundance in early life in the lungs of patients with CF, and are associated with Pseudomonas colonization and disease severity (14). It is possible that repeated disturbances of the gut microflora might cumulatively affect immune function over time in CF and correlate with disease progression (15). The evidence for an altered gut microbiome in CF A cross-sectional and longitudinal comparison of the microbiota of children with CF in comparison to their healthy siblings identified instability and decreased microbiome diversity with a decrease in Clostridia, Bifidobacterium, and Veillonella in subjects with CF (2). Recent work in a murine model has clarified that CFTR knockout mice acquire an aberrant GI microbiota in the absence of antibiotic exposure (15). CFTR allelic variants correlate with patterns of gastrointestinal dysbiosis, and subjects with homozygous ΔF508 deletion demonstrate more significant alteration in gut microbiome patterns (16). Schippa et al identified specific bacterial species related to CF disease phenotype, and found that more severe disease, in addition to ΔF508 homozygosity, correlated with an E. coli and E. biform predominant pattern (16). These studies highlight that dysbiosis in CF may be related not simply to antibiotic exposure and impaired mucus clearance, but points to CF as a systemic disease with an interrelationship between the intestinal and respiratory systems. The potential interaction between the gut and respiratory microbiome in CF Several studies have identified bacteria in the respiratory tract in CF that are typically part of the intestinal microbiota, and are theorized to contribute to the continuum of interactions between the host and microbial community in CF that relates to both the lung and gut microbiota (17).A longitudinal study of infants and young children with CF revealed distinct microbiota in the respiratory tract and gut with an overlapping core, with significant concordance between genera in both systems; more than 8 genera were highlighted as colonizing in the gut prior to the respiratory tract, identifying potential interactions (18). In this same study, significant relationships were discovered between patterns of respiratory colonization and diet, specifically breastfeeding, outlining potential relationships between intestinal microbiome, immune development, and respiratory microbiome in CF.A pilot study of probiotics in subjects with CF linked alterations of the gut microflora with decreased pulmonary exacerbations (19). Is therapeutic alteration of the gut microbiome in CF a viable strategy? There is emerging evidence that variable disease expression in patients with CF who share identical CFTR mutations may be explained by differences in microbial acquisition, the interaction between the microbiome and the immune system, and gut-lung interactions through immunity in this systemic disease. This highlights the potential importance of future studies to better elucidate the relationship between the gut microbiome and immunity in CF; the potential therapeutic benefits of altered antibiotic or probiotic interventions in CF; and potential dietary interventions, especially in infants and young children with CF, that might have short and long term health implications.
