N2  - Background: Cystic fibrosis (CF) related lung disease is often characterized by exaggerated host inflammatory response and chronic infection of the airways. Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus) is often the first bacterial pathogen to colonize the respiratory tract of infants with CF and has been implicated in the development of lung inflammation and tissue destruction. The immunologic cascade and subsequent inflammation triggered by S. aureus infection is thought to lead to lung injury and contribute to further respiratory infections with P. aeruginosa, a pathogen which has been shown to increase both morbidity and mortality. The identification of CF through newborn screening (NBS) provides an opportunity for early interventions to delay or prevent some complications of CF prior to development of clinical signs and symptoms. S. aureus prophylaxis is based on the evidence of limited studies, but used primarily on the basis of expert opinion. North American and European CF treatment guidelines differ in their recommendations regarding the need for prophylaxis of S aureus in infants. All neonates diagnosed with CF at British Columbia's Children's Hospital (BCCH), start S. aureus prophylaxis at diagnosis and continue until they are 1 year of age. Infants diagnosed through NBS and treated at BCCH CF clinic will receive cloxacillin for the first month of life or until the liver enzymes and bilirubin normalize, and then are switched to sulfamethoxa-zole/trimethoprim (SMX/TMP). Currently, there are no studies available evaluating the use of SMX/TMP as a prophylactic antibiotic for S. aureus. Aim: To describe the effectiveness and safety of S. aureus prophylaxis in infants with CF. The primary outcome was to determine the number of infants who develop positive cultures for S. aureus. Secondary outcomes were to describe bacteria grown, presence of antibmicrobial resistance to SMX/TMP, and adverse events. Methods: Data were collected retrospectively from the health records of all neonates diagnosed with CF between November 2009 and June 2012 that were initiated on S. aureus prophylaxis and received care from the BCCH CF clinic. Descriptive statistics were used to report demographic and outcome data. Effectiveness of antibiotic prophylaxis was defined as the total absence of S. aureus cultures for the duration of antibiotic prophylaxis. Results: Thirteen infants were initiated on S. aureus prophylaxis. Six (46%) infants grew S. aureus while receiving prophylaxis. Resistance to SMX/TMP was demonstrated by Escherichia coli (64%), Haemophilus spp. (40%), Acinetobacter spp. (30%), Pseudomonas putida (75%) and Achromobacter spp. (100%). Three infants were hospitalized for pulmonary exacerbations and two infants had their prophylactic antibiotic changed due to an adverse event while receiving antibiotic prophylaxis. Conclusion: The effectiveness and safety of S. aureus prophylaxis with SMX/TMP is questionable. Further investigations are needed to determine the risk versus benefits of antibiotic prophylaxis for S. aureus in infants with CF.
