N2  - Hemolysis due to Clostridium perfringens septicemia is an uncommon syndrome with a high mortality rate. It is easily mistaken for other disease processes. Early recognition is essential to instituting the appropriate treatment. A 61-year-old man presented to the emergency department with fevers, diarrhea, and lethargy. Past medical history was notable for diabetes, hemochromatosis, and orthotopic liver transplant eight years prior. On initial assessment the patient was tachycardic, hypotensive, and febrile. WBC count was 17.1 x 109/l, creatinine 3.5mg/dl, pH 7.16, and lactic acid 9.4mmol/l. The patient was treated for septic shock with IV fluid bolus and empiric antibiotics to treat a suspected intra-abdominal source. Hypotension persisted and prevented safe transfer to obtain an abdominal CT; vasopressors were initiated. Shortly thereafter, the patient developed an unusual complication: serial CBC revealed a drop in hemoglobin from 12.9g/dl to 6.8. Metabolic and coagulation profiles could not be completed by the laboratory due to severe hemolysis of the specimens. Disseminated intravascular coagulation was suspected. Surprisingly, peripheral blood smear did not show many schistocytes suggesting an alternative etiology. Admission blood cultures grew gram positive rods; the pathogen was identified as C. perfringens. Unfortunately, the patient expired little more than 24 hours after presentation. Septicemia due to clostridial species accounts for approximately 0.5% of all cases of septicemia in hospitalized patients. Of all clostridial species, C. perfringens is the most common pathogen. It is a commensal bacterium of the gut. Not surprisingly, the source of infection is most commonly the intestinal tract, including the hepatobiliary tree. The bacterium produces alpha and theta exotoxins that disrupt the erythrocyte cell membrane resulting in massive intravascular hemolysis. This complication occurs in approximately 15% of patients with C. perfringens septicemia and yields a mortality rate of up to 80%. Several case reports have described an inability to perform routine laboratory tests because of the extent of hemolysis. On peripheral blood smear, this pattern of hemolysis will show an abundance of spherocytes but few schistocytes. Early recognition of this syndrome should prompt aggressive treatment. High-dose penicillin along with metronidazole or clindamycin should be administered. Broad-spectrum antibiotics should not be stopped prematurely as many patients will have co-infection from another gut organism. Case reports strongly suggest that surgical correction of any intra-abdominal source of infection will improve survival. Had the syndrome been recognized in our patient, more audacious efforts to obtain abdominal CT and different antibiotic selection could have been made.
