N2  - Antibiotics are the most used medicament in NICU, especially during first days of live in preterm babies. Intrauterine occult infection, chorioamnionitis and PPROM are often risk factor for preterm labor and initial empirical antibiotic are started in all this babies and in all newborns with clinical symptoms for sepsis. Prolonged antibiotic treatment in non septic babies is often associated with high frequencies of late onset sepsis, disbacteriosis and neonatal sepsis from opportunist microorganism. Different author have tested hypothesis that prolonged initial empirical antibiotic treatment in preterm babies, may increase incidence of late onset sepsis, NEC, and increasing rate of mortality in this babies. Method: Retrospective study during January2011- May2012 HUOG 'Koc¸o Gliozheni'Tiranë, Albania. We analyze clinical chart of all newborn =35weeks, birth weight =2000g, antibiotic treatment in maternal, neonatal risk and clinically suspected neonatal sepsis. We collected data of clinical course, NICU stay in days, incidence of LOS, NEC and mortality rate. Objective: To correlate prolonged empirical antibiotic treatment ( = 5days) in Negative Blade Culture premature infants with LOS,NEC, high rate of mortality. Result: 103 case treated >5 days with antibiotics in negative Blade culture newborn. EOS incidence in this group was 2%( 2/103), and in 98 % of cases antibiotic treatment was performed unnecessary. 5.9% (6/102) of babies developed LOS, vs 6.7 % of incidence of LOS in total for this period. Totally, in 85.7%(6/7) of LOS, newborn was in initial empirical antibiotic treatment.NEC was present in one case. Mortality rate in this babies was higher.
