N2  - Background: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is the most common gastrointestinal emergency in preterm babies. Previous case controlled studies have identified several risk factors, with prematurity being the common variable. Although Human milk fortifier (HMF) is widely used to meet the caloric requirements of preterms, data on its relationship to NEC is lacking. The aim of this study was to detect if addition of HMF to breast milk increased the risk of NEC in preterm babies. Method: Data from medical records were used to identify all 21 cases diagnosed with NEC based on modified Bells criteria (stage II or greater) over a 10-year period (01/2000-12/2009). They were matched with 2 controls/case by gestational age, birth weight, volume and type of feed. Demographic and clinical data previously shown to predispose to NEC were recoded for cases and controls. Stratified Mantel-Haenszel Chisquare test was used to calculate the odds ratio for categorical variables and paired t-tests for comparing the continuous variables. Results: Incidence of NEC at <34 weeks gestation was 1%. The odds ratio for HMF use was 9 (p = 0.1, 95% CI = 0.86-94.3). There were also trends towards increased risk of NEC with prolonged antibiotic use, recent blood transfusion and recent administration of dilating eye drops. Conclusions: Addition of HMF to breast milk is associated with increased incidence of NEC in preterm infants <34 weeks gestation. Future casecontrol studies should match cases and controls for multiple attributes as described, to delineate the real effect of HMF.
