N2  - Background: Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a potentially life threatening illness affecting about 4-6% preterm very low birth weight (VLBW) infants. Aberrant microbial colonization of the gut is an important risk factor in the pathogenesis of NEC. Prolonged antibiotic exposure might disrupt normal colonization of the developing gut and increase the risk of NEC. We conducted a systematic review to study the association between prolonged initial antibiotics (PIA) and NEC. Methods: The standard methodology of neonatal systematic reviews was followed. PubMed, Embase, Cochrane and CINAHL databases were searched in October 2012. Quality assessment was done by Health Technology Assessment (HTA) for non-randomised studies, and by the 'Risk of Bias' assessment tool (Cochrane Handbook) for randomised controlled trials (RCTs). Meta-analyses were performed by using random effect model, and odds ratio and 95% confidence interval were calculated. Results: Four non-randomised studies (N = 4,983) and one RCT (N = 140) with subgroup of 53 preterm infants were included. Meta analysis of data from these studies showed a significant association between PIA and NEC [OR: 1.21 (95% CI: 1.01-1.49); p = 0.04]. PIA also significantly increased the odds of death [OR: 1.43; (95% CI: 1.26, 1.62); p < 0.00001], and the composite outcome of NEC or death [OR: 1.52 (95% CI: 1.19, 1.94); p = 0.0008]. Two studies reported increased risk of late onset sepsis with PIA. Conclusions: PIA is associated with increased risk of NEC and death in preterm VLBW infants.
