N2  - Subcutaneous fat necrosis (SCFN) of the newborn is a form of panniculitis that mostly affects full-term neonates who often have suffered either perinatal asphyxia or hypothermia. Therapeutic hypothermia is now routinely used in newborns to reduce the mortality and morbidity associated with moderate to severe hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy. The true incidence of SCFN in neonates undergoing cooling is unknown. Hypercalcaemia, thrombocytopenia, hypoglycaemia and other complications of SCFN have been well described in the literature. Mild elevation of C-reactive protein, which is consistent with SCFN being an inflammatory process, has been described in only few reports. We report two infants in whom CRP levels were markedly elevated and paralleled the evolving skin lesions. Both cases were full term infants with hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy and both were therapeutically cooled. In both cases, diagnosis of subcutaneous fat necrosis was made clinically. CRP was done initially as part of a septic screen at the onset of skin lesion. In first case, CRP peaked on day 4 to 272 mg/L and still remained very high (110 mg/L) on day 14. While in case 2, CRP peaked on day 3 to 135 mg/L. In both cases, repeated infection screen including viral PCR was negative. Conclusion: Our case report emphasizes that clinicians should recognize that therapeutic cooling may place neonates at increased risk for SCFN. Although one of the differential diagnoses of SCFN is infection; we wish to highlight that the persistently elevated CRP which caused prolonged antibiotic treatment, can reflect the inflammatory component of SFCN.
