N2  - INTRODUCTION Necrotizing pneumonia caused by community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) has been reported with increasing frequency in children. Patients with MRSA pneumonia have a high rate of morbidity and mortality. Here we describe a previously healthy newborn with MRSA necrotizing pneumonia complicated by bronchopleural fistula, septic shock, and abdominal compartment syndrome. Several measures, including multiple chest tubes, high frequency oscillatory ventilation (HFOV) and decompressive abdominal laparotomy, led to successful treatment of this syndrome. CASE PRESENTATION: A three week-old full term infant male was admitted to the pediatric intensive care unit with bilateral pneumonia. Presenting history was notable for his mother developing a hordeolum one week prior to the onset of his illness. His initial therapy on admission included of fluid resuscitation and empiric antibiotic coverage. Within six hours of admission he developed respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation; inotropic support for ongoing hemodynamic instability. His initial blood and respiratory cultures returned positive for MRSA. Over the subsequent 24 hours his lung compliance worsened while on conventional mechanical ventilation, leading to the development of bilateral tension pneumothoraces and subsequent cardiac arrest, which reversed with bilateral needle decompression. Over the following days he required multiple thoracostomy tubes bilaterally for bronchopleural fistulas and recurrent pneumothoraces. He transitioned to HFOV with a minimal mean airway pressure to allow for healing of the bronchopleural fistulas. Additionally, abdominal compartment syndrome developed in the setting of significant anasarca, which ultimately was alleviated with a bedside decompressive laparotomy. He was successfully extubated after 28 days of mechanical ventilation and was discharged home after 44 day hospital stay. Discussion: Because of widespread colonization with community acquired MRSA, consideration of this as a potential pathogen in severely ill otherwise healthy newborns is important. A careful family history of MRSA related issues may reveal increased risk and justify broadening antibiotic choices in the neonate. Treatment of MRSA necrotizing pneumonia and its complications present a therapeutic challenge to the pediatric intensivist. The presence of bronchopleural fistula is particularly difficult. HFOV may offers advantages over conventional ventilation by avoiding a high peak pressure and eliminating the cyclic opening and closure of inflamed alveoli. Additionally, when this clinical scenario is complicated by sepsis and the development of abdominal compartment syndrome, decompressive laparotomy may be necessary to allow for adequate oxygenation and ventilation. Such therapeutic strategies may become more prevalent as we continue to care for increasing numbers of patients afflicted with community acquired MRSA pneumonia. (Figure presented) .
