N2  - Introduction: Clinical trials of treatment of LOS are urgently required but current management of LOS is poorly characterised. We aimed to describe appropriatness of expert-panel defined clinical and laboratory criteria and characterise empric antibacterial treatment of LOS in NICUs participating in studies NeoMero1 and NeoMero2. Methods: A prospective observational study was conducted from July 2010 to September 2011. Results: Of 166 screened patients 113 were enrolled. Median age at onset of LOS was 14 days, median birth weight 1190g. 61% had culture-positive sepsis (41% CoNS, 35% Enterobacteriaceae, 16% other Gram positive and 9% non-fermentative Gram negative organisms). Most common clinical criteria were mottled skin, impaired peripheral perfusion and increased oxygen requirement seen approximately in 40% of patients each. Of laboratory markers, CRP>15 mg/L and platelet count <100 × 109 cells/L were the most frequent. Overall 18 antibiotics in 47 treatment regimens were used empirically. Meropenem ± vancomycin or vancomycin + amikacin were the most frequent, used in 9% of patients each. All-cause mortality was 8%. Conclusion: Unspecificity of diagnostic criteria and extremely high variability of empiric antibiotic regimen for LOS across Europe was observed. This all may pose difficulties in conducting pan-European clinical trails in LOS.
