N2  - Introduction: Blood culture is a systematic examination before any suspicion of infection in children; it is responsible for a high rate of morbidity and mortality. Objective: Analyze the epidemiological profile and sensitivity of blood culture isolates from patients hospitalized in the Children's Hospital to optimize empirical antibiotic therapy. Material and methods: This is a retrospective study that was performed at the microbiology laboratory of the University Hospital IBN ROCHD at Casablanca, based on the use of computerized database of over three years (From January 2009 to December 2011), and on all bacteria isolated from blood cultures on BACTEC (Becton Dickinson) at the hospital of children of CHU IBN ROCHD of Casablanca. Results: We received 6127 blood cultures with 3929 (64%) sterile cases. Among the positive blood cultures, Staphylococcus coagulase negative constituted 60.8% of cultures, Bacillus sp. 4.3%, alpha haemolytic Streptococcus 2.8%, Staphylococcus aureus 2.8% and E.coli 2.6%. The highest prescribing service is neonatology (23.7%). Concerning antimicrobial susceptibility, Staphylococcus aureus is resistant to methicilin in 16.1 % of cases. For Enterobacteriaceae, resistance to ampicillin was found in 88.6% of cases, amoxicillin-clavulanic acid 61.8%, third generation of cephalosporins 45.2%, gentamicin 34.3%, amikacin 9.4% and to ciprofl oxacin 23.9%. Of 138 research requests of ESBL, 64 strains were productive what mean 46.4%. For Acinetobacter baumannii, we noted a resistance to third generation of cephalosporins in 83.3% of cases and a resistance to imipenem in 46.7%. For Pseudomonas aeruginosa, resistance to ceftazidime was present in 28.1% of cases, to imipenem in 18.8%, to gentamicin in 16.2 % and to tobramycin in 11.1% of cases. Conclusions: With the aim of a good working knowledge of the use and rationalization of antibiotics and improved therapeutic management of bacteraemia in children, it's necessary to know and to regularly analyze the epidemiological profile and status sensitivity of blood culture isolates.
