N2  - Empirical antibiotic treatment is prescribed to almost all extremely low birth weight (ELBW) infants (<1,000 g). Our objective was to assess the association between duration of antibiotic treatment and the risk of developing necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) in ELBW infants with sterile initial postnatal blood culture results. We conducted a retrospective cohort analysis of ELBW infants admitted to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit in the Coombe Women and Infants University Hospital between January 2008 and December 2010. We defined initial empirical antibiotic treatment as continuous days of treatment started in the first 24 h of life with a sterile initial postnatal blood culture result. Prolonged initial empirical antibiotic treatment was defined as > 2 days. 98 infants were eligible for analysis. The median birth weight was 785 g (range 470-993 g). The median gestational age was 26 weeks (range 23-31). The median duration of antibiotic therapy was 5 days (range 2-17). 84 infants (86%) received prolonged therapy and were more likely to develop NEC (25 vs. 7%); however this did not reach statistical significance (OR 4.3, 95% CI 0.6-192.8, p = 0.18). In logistic regression models, duration of antibiotic treatment was identified as a statistically significant factor for developing NEC (OR 1.22; 95% CI 1.04-1.42; p = 0.01) and remained significant after including birth weight and gestational age, however this disappeared after including multiple perinatal variables. Prolonged duration of empirical antibiotic treatment may play a role in the multifactorial pathogenesis of necrotising enterocolitis, however it is not an independent factor in our study.
