N2  - Objectives: Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia (SaB) is a serious infection associated with complications. Recommended duration of treatment in adults is 4-6 weeks. To audit duration of antimicrobial treatment in neonates who had SaB and their subsequent complications. Methods: Neonates with SaB from January 2001 to December 2010 admitted in the NICU at the study centre were included. Results: There were 45 SaB and 3 MRSA bacteraemias. The mean postmenstrual age (PMA) at presentation was 31.03 weeks (25.4- 35.7) and the mean PMA at birth was 28.07 (24.28-33.71). The mean birth weight was 939 grams (560-1940). Central line was present in 29 cases and was removed in 28 following culture, mean CRP was 68.25 mg/L (5-280). Duration of intravenous antibiotic usage varied. Out of 45 episodes, 35 received antibiotics for less than 14 days. 6 died, 5 had NEC and 2 had concurrent Klebsiella septicaemia. 5 neonates treated with <28 days of IV antibiotics subsequently developed abscess or osteomyelitis (OM), 3 were discharged on home oxygen and 1 developed S aureus meningitis. Of the 4 neonates treated with 28 days of antibiotics 2 developed NEC (none died). Conclusion: 10 year data from our institution demonstrate a wide variation in the duration of antibiotic usage in SaB. Death was the commonest outcome (13.3%) followed by abscess and OM (11%). It seems that incidence of death and complications were less in the babies with longer antibiotic courses, however, numbers were small to make robust conclusions.
