N2  - Introduction: S. marcescens has emerged in recent years as an opportunistic pathogen of severe nosocomial infections of neonatal intensive care unit (NICU). Purpose: To analyzes clinical signs, risk factors in newborns with sepsis by this bacteria as well as the therapy which is commonly used. Methods: Retrospective study that includes all newborns with confirmed sepsis by Serratia marcescens admitted in our NICU from January 2007 to December 2010. Results: 28 babies were enrolled. 68% males. Mean birth weight and gestational age (GA) 1276 g and 29 + 6, respectively. Most of patients presented several potential risk factors for nosocomial infection being the admission to NICU (100%) and epicutaneous line the most frequent (86%). Laboratory finding: thrombocytopenia 54%. Acute phase reactants were elevated in all patients. In 22 cases was the only microorganism isolated. 39% presented other infections documented: 25% bloodstream infection, 25% meningitis, 17% necrotizing enterocolitis, 17% conjunctivitis, 8% pneumonia, 8% cholecystitis. Patients received monotherapy with amikacin (6), meropenem (2) cefotaxima (2) according to antibiogram. In seven cases was necessary added combined therapy with meropenem due to clinical worsening. Conclusions: Sepsis caused by S. marcescens continue being a problem in NICU. They are usually difficult to treat because of serratia propensity to become resistant to antibiotics. Susceptibility to the antimicrobials may differ in a manner that is dependent on whether these antibiotics were previously used in the units. Thus, current strategy of empiric antibiotic treatment should be reevaluated by a medical microbiologist.
