N2  - It is very important to decide whether clinical signs are a suspected sepsis or another neonatal emergency problem. Laboratory tests are not specific but they help the decision of diagnosis. In Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) early antibiotic therapy is necessary for therapy. The objective of this study was the evaluation of laboratory test procalcitonin (PCT) in decision making on ceasing antibiotic therapy in suspected late-onset sepsis. METHODOLOGY: A retrospective study was held. Neonates ≤35 weeks with suspected late-onset sepsis were assigned to treatment based on conventional laboratory parameters (CRP, WBC, PLT) (control group, n=43) or to PCT based treatment (PCT group, n=44). The duration of antibiotic therapy was 5 days in the control group, but in the PCT group antibiotic therapy was stopped when 2nd PCT values were below cut-off values. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION: Data from 87 preterm neonates were collected. Two groups were similar for baseline demographics, risk factors for late-onset sepsis, and they had clinical signs of infection as assessed by the neonatologist. There was a significant difference in the proportion of neonates treated with antibiotics ≥5 days between the control group (76.7%) and the PCT group (54.5%) RR 1.41; odds ratio 2.75 (95% CI 1.00-7.71), p = 0.029) and in the proportion of neonates treated with antibiotics for ≤4 days 13,9% in the control group and 36,36% in PCT group; RR 0.38; odds ratio 0.28 (95% CI 0.09-0.91), p = 0.031). Clinical outcome was similar in both groups but sample size was insufficient to exclude rare adverse events. CONCLUSIONS: The duration of therapy in suspected late-onset sepsis was shortened due to the values of PCT in preterm neonates. This is recommended in NICU but it has to be confirmed with a larger multi center prospective randomized intervention study.
