N2  - Background: According to practice recommendations, clinically suspected chorioamnionitis (CSC), a complication of pregnancy, requires intervention and treatment to prevent early onset neonatal sepsis (EONS). Objective: The objective was to evaluate the clinical burden of CSC with the goal of implementing more effective and efficient identification, monitoring, investigation and treatment of at-risk newborns. Design: As part of a quality improvement (QI) project, a retrospective chart review was conducted between 1st January 2010 and 30th June 2010, on infants' greater than or equal to 35 weeks gestation who were admitted to a tertiary Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) and were evaluated for EONS because of CSC. Data were abstracted for both maternal and infant clinical and laboratory findings, including placental histology. Results: Of 2665 babies delivered during this period, 689 were admitted to the NICU, and 103 (14.9%) were investigated and treated for CSC. The most common maternal sign suggesting chorioamnionitis was fever >38.0°C, seen in 76 (74%) of mothers. Other characteristic clinical signs of chorioam- nionitis included fetal tachycardia (>160bpm), maternal tachycardia (>100bpm), maternal leukocytosis (WBC >15,000/pL), uterine tenderness, and malodorous vaginal discharge. Although maternal and fetal tachycardias are associated with the constellation of symptoms for diagnosis of chorioam- nionitis, both were typically associated with maternal pyrexia. Fourteen infants (14%) presented with respiratory distress and/or hemodynamic instability, including the only baby with a positive blood culture. Eighty-nine infants (86%) were asymptomatic at delivery and remained without clinical signs of sepsis while receiving antibiotics during the 48 hour NICU admission. Among the asymptomatic infants, 5 neonates received an extended antimicrobial course secondary to maternal positive blood or uterine cultures. Placental histopathology confirmed acute chorioamnionitis with funisitis or chorionic deciduitis in 65 (83%) of 78 available placental reports, showing a significant overlap of clinical and histologic diagnosis. Conclusions: CSC is frequently diagnosed and, although rarely associated with adverse outcome, is a significant burden on babies, families and the healthcare system. Strategies should be developed to improve the diagnosis of CSC, while reducing the intensity and duration of monitoring, investigation and treatment. Suggested QI interventions include stricter criteria for CSC diagnosis, and for asymptomatic infants, observation and treatment at the mothers' bedside, as well as use of validated acute phase reactants (like CRP) to minimize the duration of antibiotic therapy. A clinical care map is being developed to clarify these changes.
