N2  - Background: Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) causes serious morbidity and mortality in very-low birth weight premature infants. The Australia & New Zealand Neonatal Network (ANZNN Report 2006) recently reported an incidence of 4.8% in this group, with a case mortality rate of 31.3%. Our objective was to review our NEC-related mortality rate and to determine if it is changing over time. Methods: We performed a cohort study on all infants born before 32 completed weeks completed gestation weighing less than 1500 grams, who were cared for at RWH from January 2001-December 2009. Eligible infants were identified from prospectively compiled, computerised records. Those diagnosed with NEC ≥ Bells Stage 2 were compared with those who were not. The primary outcomes were NEC-related mortality and the change in mortality rate over time, assessed using the chi-squared test by comparing 2 eras; early (01/01/05-30/06/05) versus late (01/07/05-31/12/09). Results: 1985 infants were eligible for inclusion. Eighty-four cases of NEC were identified (incidence rate 4.2%). The overall NEC mortality rate was 48.8%, which increased over time; 32.4% in the early era vs 61.7% in the later one (p = 0.11). No significant differences were detected between the eras with regards to incidence, possible confounders (e.g. gestational age or birth weight); or known risk factors (e.g. antibiotic exposure). Conclusion: The incidence of NEC at RWH is stable, although the NECrelated mortality rate has increased over the past decade. This requires further investigation.
