N2  - Background: Group B Streptococcus (GBS) is a major cause of severe systemic infections among the newborn. Early-onset sepsis appears in the first 3 days of life and late-onset sepsis occurs from day 7 up to 3 months of age. Despite optimal treatment of GBS-infected neonates it is associated with significant morbidity and mortality, and prevention strategies are required. Early treatment and then antibiotic prophylaxis were shown to reduce morbidity and mortality rates; however, GBS remains a leading cause of perinatal infection. The use of chemoprophylaxis in GBS-colonised pregnant women, especially when it is incomplete, may not be sufficient to prevent clinical neonatal infection, but may inhibit the growth of GBS in blood and CSF cultures. Antibiotic prophylaxis reduced the incidence of early form of 70% but not of late. Pathogenesis of late-onset sepsis (LONS) remains uncertain: prematurity is the first risk factor. With increasing survival of preterm infants, late-onset sepsis and specially nosocomial bloodstream infection will continue to be a challenging complication that affects other morbidities, length of hospitalization, cost of care, and mortality rates. Especially the very low birth weight (VLBW) infants are sensitive to serious systemic infection during their initial hospital stay. It is possible vertical, horizontal (nosocomial) transmission and through breast milk. The average age of onset is 27 days. Fever, skin marbling, lethargy, irritability, and feeding difficulties are the initial symptoms. Mortality rate is 5-20%. We describe a preterm infant who, although treated in prophylactic manner for GBS, later developed GBS sepsis, with meningitis, probably due to feeding of infected breast milk or hand-to-hand transmission. Case report: The case of preterm female infant born at 35 weeks by vaginal delivery is presented below. Apgar scores were 7 and 8, at 1 and 5 min, respectively. She was an IUGR with birth weight 1700 g (3-10th percentile), length 43.5 cm (10th percentile), and head circumference 30.5 cm (10th percentile). Pregnancy was uncomplicated and vaginal/rectal swab (48 h before delivery) were positive for GBS. The mother of the infant received optimal GBS prophylaxis intrapartum. During hospitalization the baby had no problems. She received prophylactic antibiotic therapy for 7 days because of the presence of umbilical catheter. Laboratory screening studies (including inflammatory markers) did not reveal any abnormalities, all culture tests were negative. Imaging studies were normal. Discharged on the 10th day in good condition she was included in late preterm follow-up program. Two months after birth she was admitted to the Pediatric Intensive Care unit for respiratory failure, apnea, hyperpyrexia, bradycardia, and hypotension (41/28 mmHg). She was hypotonic, pale and with mottled skin. The critical conditions rapidly resulting in intubation and full ventilatory support. Blood cultures were obtained and antibiotics were initiated. The baby died for septic shock after a few hours. Laboratory screening revealed: neutrophilic leukocytosis, I/T 0.01, Elevation of inflammatory index and some alteration of coagulation tests compatible with disseminated intravascular coagulation. Cerebrospinal fluid and blood cultures were positive for GBS. Conclusion: Improved effectiveness of antenatal cultures and adequate maternal intrapartum prophylaxis might help to further decrease GBS early-onset disease. The recent recommendations for the prevention of perinatal GBS vertical transmission were not effective for reducing late GBS infection. This may be due to horizontal infections from maternal sources, community or cross infections. It is important suspect late GBS infection and start early antibiotic treatment. Persistence of GBS colonization of infants' mucous membrane is postulated to influence the pathogeneses of recurrent and late GBS infection. Transmission of GBS through breast milk should be considered in cases of late neonatal GBS infection and bacterial culture of breast milk, in addition to vaginal and rectal swabs, should be routinely performed in such cases, and repeated during the first 2 months postpartum.
