N2  - Background: Early-onset neonatal bacterial infections continue to be a major cause of morbidity and mortality in the newborn. The frequency of early-onset neonatal sepsis without prophylaxis is 1-5/ 1000 live births. Since year '70 the most frequent causative microorganism is the group B Streptococcus (S. agalactiae, GBS), followed by Escherichia coli. The mortality rate is now reduced to 4% due to the improvement of neonatal intensive care. Guidelines advise to collect cultures at 35-37 weeks' gestation and to administer intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis in case of GBS-positive cultures, as well as in all preterm deliveries. Improved effectiveness of antenatal cultures and adequate prophylaxis might help to further decrease GBS early-onset disease. The overall incidence of early-onset sepsis has not decreased inmany centers, and several studies have found an increase in sepsis due to Gram-negative organisms. Diagnosis of neonatal early-onset sepsis is difficult because clinical signs and laboratory tests are non-specific. Early antibiotic therapy is crucial for treatment success. This case report aims to describe the difficulty of diagnosis in patients with neonatal sepsis. Case report: We present the case of a female neonate born at 41st week by vaginal delivery to a 33-year-old primigravida. The parents were healthy and non-consanguineous. Family pedigree of the fetus was unremarkable. Laboratory studies (including HBV, rubella test and Wasserman reaction) performed during pregnancy were normal but at 38 weeks' gestation maternal vaginal and rectal swabs were positive for Group B Streptococcus (GBS) and Escherichia coli. At birth meconium-stained amniotic-fluid was detected but without premature rupture of membranes. The birth weight was 3780 g (50-90th percentile), length 53 cm (90th percentile), and head circumference 35 cm (50-90th percentile). Apgar scores were 7 and 9, at 1 and 5 min, respectively. In the first 2 days of life presented good general condition with hyperbilirubinemia at 48 h of life (Bt 14.2) and need of phototherapy for 18 h. Weight loss was 8% in the second day. Sixty-six hours after delivery there was a swift and sudden deterioration in clinical appearance. Low reactivity, lethargy, hypotonia, weak cry, pale-mottled skin and respiratory distress signs (polypnea RR 90 breaths/min and epigastric retractions) appeared. The baby showed also desaturation, bradycardia (HR 90-100 bpm) and hypotension (41/28 mmHg) with decrease in urine output. Because of her serious condition the newborn was admitted to our Neonatal Intensive Care Unity (NICU). She was put in incubator and started oxygen-therapy maintaining spontaneous breathing. Laboratory workup revealed hypoglycaemia, elevation of inflammatory markers (CRP 15 mg/L) and thrombocytopenia (PLT 60,000/μL). Blood and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) cultures were obtained, and ampicillin and gentamicin antibiotic treatment was initiated through an umbilical catheter. She rapidly progressed to respiratory failure and the need for mechanical ventilation and developed intravascular disseminate coagulation with v. cava thrombosis, pulmonary and cerebral hemorrhage. This clinical presentation is typical of neonatal sepsis but metabolic workup, including blood gases (that showed serious metabolic acidosis, pH 6.8, pCO2 25, HCO3 - 5, EB -26 mmol/L, managed with repeated bicarbonate infusion), lactate and aminotransferase levels (increased), hyperammonaemia and the ketonuria, oriented towards early onset of an inborn metabolic disease. Bolus of carnitine, biotin and vitamin B12 suspended oral nutrition was performed. CSF analysis, blood amino acid, liver and muscle biopsy was found to be within the normal range and the metabolic disease was excluded. Escherichia coli was isolated from the newborn's blood obtained on the initial sepsis workup, while CSF was sterile. A cranial ultrasonography (US) performed demonstrated cerebral edema with increased periventricular echogenicity and cerebellum hemorrhage. EEG during the first day demonstrated recurrent lowamplitude epileptic seizure activity on top of generalized suppressed pattern of background activity. The infant was slowly weaned from mechanical ventilation and antibiotics therapy was continued for a total of 16 days until the baby was discharged at the age of 20 days in a good general condition. However she needs careful follow-up for neurologic outcome. Conclusion: Group B Streptococcus (GBS) and Escherichia coli (E. coli) are the leading causes of early-onset neonatal disease (EOD). Intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis of GBS-colonized women decreases vertical transmission and EOD due to GBS. Nevertheless, no intervention has been developed to reduce the risk of EOD related to E. coli. Timely and accurate identification of colonized mothers is necessary to implement preventive strategies against neonatal sepsis. Inborn errors of metabolism are rare (1:5000 live births) and only a timely diagnosis and treatment improve the prognosis. The clinical picture is nonspecific and may mimic other conditions. A neonate with clinical symptoms caused by one of these disorders is a real clinical emergency, a situation complicated by the similarities to the manifestations seen in sepsis or asphyxia. The unexpected worsening after an initial period of 'free', however, is the most common presentation and should not be underestimated to avoid incurring dangerous delays in diagnosis.
