N2  - Introduction: Neonates with low birth weight (LBW) treated in the intensive care unit (ICU), usually are not able to be breastfed. It is however possible to feed them with mother's milk through a catheter. Aim of the study: Assessment whether feeding with predominantly mother's milk has a favorable effect on the course of treatment of LBW neonates treated in the ICU. Material and methods: 80 neonates with birth weight <2500 g, gestational age <37 weeks consecutively admitted due to respiratory failure were included. Exclusion criteria comprised major congenital anomalies. The following outcome measures were assessed: feeding tolerance, head and body growth, duration of: antibiotic therapy, phototherapy, central venous cannulation, the incidence of sepsis, retinopathy of prematurity (ROP), intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) and duration of ventilation and hospitalization in the ICU. Neonates were divided into 2 groups - below 50% mother's milk and over 50% of mother's milk during 30 days of treatment in the ICU. Results: There were no differences in baseline demographic data. Mean mother's milk content during hospitalization was 9.84% in group I, and 83.17% in group II. There was significantly better feeding tolerance and faster head circumference growth among babies from group II. The number of episodes of sepsis was similar in both groups, there was a longer period of central venous cannulation and a tendency for longer hospitalization time in group I. No significant differences in mortality were noted. Conclusion: Feeding predominantly with mother's milk in LBW neonates is advantageous in terms of better feeding tolerance, increased head circumference and tendency for shorter hospitalization time in the ICU.
