N2  - Background: Late onset sepsis (LOS) is an important cause of mortality and morbidity in VLBW infants. The best empiric antibiotic cover for gram positive bacteria is debated. Vancomycin is optimal for most CoNS, but provides selective pressure for resistant organisms. Flucloxacillin is the best antibiotic for sensitive Staphylococcus aureus, but will not treat MRSA or most CoNS. Method: VLBW infants born 05/01 to 07/05 who had Staphylococcus sepsis were identified and contaminants were excluded. Demographic and clinical variables were compared to identify those that predicted whether CoNS or Staphylococcus aureus was the infecting organism. Results: For 149 eligible infants, multivariate analysis identified HMD (OR 0.145; 95% CI: 0.033-0.630), previous blood transfusion (OR 0.206; 95% CI: 0.049-0.877) and currently receiving parenteral nutrition (OR 0.067; 95% CI: 0.016-0.291) as predictors of CoNS sepsis. Conclusions: Risk factors which predicted whether an infant had Staphylococcus aureus vs. CoNS sepsis were identified. A multicentre study using these methods would enable development of a nomogram, allowing better empiric antibiotic choices for VLBW infants with suspected sepsis.
