N2  - Nosocomial infections (late-onset-septicemia) are a major cause of both mortality and morbidity in newborns who are admitted to intensive care. Despite dramatic advances in neonatal intensive care leading to significant survival of very low-birth-weight premature infants, infections have remained a serious problem throughout the last decades. Diagnosis of late-onset-sepsis is difficult, and empiric antibiotic treatment is warranted because of the possible rapid deterioration of infected babies. The repertory of antibiotic therapy should take into account all the pathogens typical for the own intensive care unit. The use of broad-spectrum antibiotics and vancomycin should be considered critically in the light of increasing bacterial resistance. Knowledge of the own infection rate and educational programs for nursing teams in hygienic care are substantial tools for prevention of nosocomial infections. Risk factors, which may be part of intensive care medicine, such as central lines, or invasive therapeutic and diagnostic procedures should be avoided in premature babies as far as possible.
