N2  - Background. Probiotics are live microorganisms which, when administered in adequate amounts, confer health benefits on the host. Potentially, probiotics may offer benefit to premature and low-birth weight infants. Objectives. To assess the efficacy and safely of probiotics in preterm and low-birth weight infants. Search strategy. We searched the MEDLINE (1966 to December 2003), EMBASE (1980 to November 2003), the Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (The Cochrane Library issue 4, 2003), and reference lists from studies and reviews. Selection criteria. Randomised and quasi-randomised double-blind trials comparing a specified probiotic agent with placebo or no probiotic in preterm and low-birth weight infants. Main results. Six studies met the inclusion criteria with a total of 823 participants. The probiotics tested and dosage of organisms administred varied between studies. Trials also varied considerably in methodological quality and the outcome measures. Successful colonisation with probiotics was demonstrated in all trials, although the degree of colonization varied between trials. Growth data were analysed in four trials. One study (n=97) showed that the administration of Bifidobacterium breve is associated with better weight gain. Three other trials failed to show significant differences. One trial (n=585) showed that Lactobacillus GG (LGG) was not effective in reducing the incidence of necrotising enterocolitis, urinary tract infections, and bacterial sepsis. Probiotics (LGG, L. acidophilus) did not have any effect on the use and duration of antibiotic treatment. No trial reported adverse effects attributable to probiotic administration. Conclusions. Only limited number of controlled trials studied health outcomes following enteral administration of probiotic organisms in preterm infants. No firm conclusions on the effects of probiotics can be made on the basis of the available data. Further large studies with clinically important outcomes are needed.
